"year": 1950,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 3,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The sum of the roots of the equation \\(4x^2+5-8x=0\\) is equal to:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(8\\)",
"B": "\\(-5\\)",
"C": "\\(-\\dfrac54\\)",
"D": "\\(-2\\)",
"E": "None of these answers"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Assuming that the two complex roots are intended, call them \\(r\\) and \\(s\\). We can find their sum without calculating each root. Reordering the polynomial and expanding its factorization gives\n\\[\n4x^2-8x+5=4(x-r)(x-s)\n=4x^2-4(r+s)x+4rs.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(x\\) is therefore both \\(-8\\) and \\(-4(r+s)\\). Hence\n\\[\n-4(r+s)=-8,\n\\qquad r+s=2.\n\\]\nThe domain qualification matters because\n\\[\n4x^2-8x+5=4(x-1)^2+1>0\n\\]\nfor every real \\(x\\). Thus there are no real roots. Over the complex numbers, \\((x-1)^2=-1/4\\) gives the roots \\(1\\pm i/2\\), where \\(i^2=-1\\).\n\nConsequently, when complex roots are included, their sum is \\(\\boxed{2}\\), which is not among A through D, so the answer is \\(\\mathrm{E}\\). If only real roots are allowed, there are no roots to sum.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1950/solutions",
