"batch": "NA",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The real factors of \\(x^2+4\\) are:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\((x^2+2)(x^2+2)\\)",
"B": "\\((x^2+2)(x^2-2)\\)",
"C": "\\(x^2(x^2+4)\\)",
"D": "\\((x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2)\\)",
"E": "Non-existent"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Here, “real factors” means a factorization into polynomials of positive degree with real coefficients; constant factors are excluded.\n\nBecause \\(x^2+4\\) has degree \\(2\\), a nontrivial factorization would have two linear factors: their degrees must add to \\(2\\). A real linear factor \\(ax+b\\), with \\(a\\ne0\\), is zero at the real number \\(x=-b/a\\). Its product with any other polynomial would therefore be zero there.\n\nBut every real square is nonnegative, so for every real \\(x\\),\n\\[\nx^2+4\\ge4>0.\n\\]\nThus \\(x^2+4\\) has no real zero and hence no real linear factor. There is no nontrivial real factorization, choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1950/solutions",
