"year": 1950,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 33,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The number of circular pipes with an inside diameter of \\(1\\) inch which will carry the same amount of water as a pipe with an inside diameter of \\(6\\) inches is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(6\\pi\\)",
"B": "\\(6\\)",
"C": "\\(12\\)",
"D": "\\(36\\)",
"E": "\\(36\\pi\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Assume the pipes are full and the water has the same average speed in each pipe. In a fixed time, the water advances the same distance, so the volume carried equals the inside cross-sectional area multiplied by a common length.\n\nThe radii of the small and large pipes are \\(\\tfrac12\\) inch and \\(3\\) inches. Their cross-sectional areas are therefore \\(\\pi(\\tfrac12)^2\\) and \\(\\pi(3)^2\\). The required number of small pipes is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi(3)^2}{\\pi(\\tfrac12)^2}\n=\\frac{9\\pi}{\\pi/4}=36.\n\\]\nThus, under this equal-condition model, the answer is \\(36\\) pipes, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1950/solutions",
