"batch": "NA",
"number": 43,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The sum to infinity of \\(\\dfrac17+\\dfrac{2}{7^2}+\\dfrac{1}{7^3}+\\dfrac{2}{7^4}+\\cdots\\) is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac15\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac1{24}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac5{48}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac1{16}\\)",
"E": "None of these"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The numerators repeat every two terms, so group consecutive pairs. The first pair is\n\\[\n\\frac17+\\frac2{7^2}=\\frac9{49}.\n\\]\nEach later pair is \\(\\frac1{49}\\) times the preceding pair. Since \\(\\left|\\frac1{49}\\right|<1\\), the resulting geometric series converges, with sum\n\\[\n\\frac{9/49}{1-1/49}\n=\\frac9{48}\n=\\frac3{16}.\n\\]\nThis also gives the limit of the original partial sums: those ending after a pair approach \\(\\frac3{16}\\), and an odd partial sum differs from the preceding even one by \\(\\frac1{7^{2n+1}}\\), which tends to zero. Thus the sum is \\(\\boxed{\\frac3{16}}\\). It is absent from choices A through D, so the answer is E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
