"year": 1951,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 17,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Indicate in which one of the following equations \\(y\\) is neither directly nor inversely proportional to \\(x\\):",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(x+y=0\\)",
"B": "\\(3xy=10\\)",
"C": "\\(x=5y\\)",
"D": "\\(3x+y=10\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{x}{y}=\\sqrt3\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Direct proportionality has the form \\(y=kx\\), while inverse proportionality has the form \\(xy=k\\), with a fixed nonzero constant \\(k\\).\n\nThe alternatives can be rewritten as follows:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{A: }&y=-x,\\\\\n\\text{B: }&xy=\\frac{10}{3},\\\\\n\\text{C: }&y=\\frac{x}{5},\\\\\n\\text{E: }&y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus A, C, and E describe direct proportionality, and B describes inverse proportionality. In E, the original denominator requires \\(y\\ne0\\), which also forces \\(x\\ne0\\); the direct relation holds on that domain.\n\nFor D, \\(y=10-3x\\). Both \\((x,y)=(1,7)\\) and \\((2,4)\\) satisfy this equation. Their ratios \\(y/x\\) are \\(7\\) and \\(2\\), and their products \\(xy\\) are \\(7\\) and \\(8\\). Neither quantity is constant, so D is neither directly nor inversely proportional. The answer is choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
