"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 19,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A six-place number is formed by repeating a three-place number; for example, \\(256{,}256,\\) or \\(678{,}678,\\) etc. Any number of this form is always exactly divisible by:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(7\\) only",
"B": "\\(11\\) only",
"C": "\\(13\\) only",
"D": "\\(101\\)",
"E": "\\(1001\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Let \\(k\\) be the three-digit number, so \\(100\\le k\\le999\\). In the six-digit number, the first copy of \\(k\\) has been shifted three places to the left, multiplying its value by \\(1000\\). The second copy contributes another \\(k\\). Thus the number is\n\\[\n1000k+k=1001k.\n\\]\nIts quotient upon division by \\(1001\\) is exactly the integer \\(k\\), so divisibility by \\(1001\\) always holds. Also, \\(1001=7\\cdot11\\cdot13\\), so all three smaller divisors occur together, rather than any one of them only. The answer is \\(1001\\), choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
