"year": 1951,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 26,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In the equation \\[ \\frac{x(x-1)-(m+1)}{(x-1)(m-1)}=\\frac{x}{m}, \\] the roots are equal when:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(m=1\\)",
"B": "\\(m=\\dfrac12\\)",
"C": "\\(m=0\\)",
"D": "\\(m=-1\\)",
"E": "\\(m=-\\dfrac12\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Method 1: Factor and compare the roots\n\nFirst record the denominator restrictions:\n\\[\nm\\ne0,\\qquad m\\ne1,\\qquad x\\ne1.\n\\]\nUnder these restrictions, multiplying by \\(m(x-1)(m-1)\\) gives\n\\[\nm\\bigl[x(x-1)-(m+1)\\bigr]\n=x(x-1)(m-1).\n\\]\nThe terms containing \\(m x(x-1)\\) cancel, leaving\n\\[\nx^2-x-m(m+1)=0.\n\\]\nThe constant term suggests the factorization\n\\[\n(x+m)(x-m-1)=0,\n\\]\nso the candidate roots are \\(-m\\) and \\(m+1\\).\n\nThey are equal precisely when\n\\[\n-m=m+1,\n\\qquad m=-\\frac12.\n\\]\nThis parameter is allowed, and its repeated root \\(x=\\frac12\\) does not make a denominator zero.\n\nFor completeness, a candidate root can equal the forbidden value \\(1\\) only at the allowed parameter \\(m=-1\\). Then the other root is \\(0\\); discarding \\(1\\) leaves a simple root, not two equal roots. Thus the answer is \\(\\boxed{m=-\\frac12}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Use the discriminant\n\nThe original fractions require \\(m\\ne0,1\\) and \\(x\\ne1\\). Multiplying by the nonzero common denominator \\(m(x-1)(m-1)\\) and simplifying gives\n\\[\nx^2-x-m(m+1)=0.\n\\]\nA quadratic has two equal real roots exactly when its discriminant is zero. Here\n\\[\n\\Delta=(-1)^2-4(1)\\bigl[-m(m+1)\\bigr]\n=1+4m^2+4m\n=(2m+1)^2.\n\\]\nConsequently, equal roots require \\(2m+1=0\\), giving \\(m=-\\frac12\\). The quadratic then becomes\n\\[\nx^2-x+\\frac14=\\left(x-\\frac12\\right)^2=0.\n\\]\nBoth \\(m=-\\frac12\\) and \\(x=\\frac12\\) satisfy the original denominator restrictions, so this repeated root is valid.\n\nDiscarding a forbidden root does not create equal roots. In particular, at \\(m=-1\\), the quadratic is \\(x(x-1)=0\\), leaving only the simple admissible root \\(0\\). The answer is therefore \\(\\boxed{m=-\\frac12}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
