"year": 1951,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 39,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A stone is dropped into a well and the report of the stone striking the bottom is heard \\(7.7\\) seconds after it is dropped. Assume that the stone falls \\(16t^2\\) feet in \\(t\\) seconds and that the velocity of sound is \\(1120\\) feet per second. The depth of the well is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(784\\) ft.",
"B": "\\(342\\) ft.",
"C": "\\(1568\\) ft.",
"D": "\\(156.8\\) ft.",
"E": "None of these"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The sound starts traveling upward only after the stone reaches the bottom, so the two travel times must add to \\(7.7\\) seconds. Let \\(t\\) be the stone's falling time in seconds, with \\(0<t<7.7\\). The well's depth is \\(16t^2\\) feet, so the sound takes\n\\[\n\\frac{16t^2}{1120}=\\frac{t^2}{70}\n\\]\nseconds to return.\n\nThus\n\\[\nt+\\frac{t^2}{70}=7.7.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(70\\) and factoring gives\n\\[\nt^2+70t-539=0,\n\\qquad (t-7)(t+77)=0.\n\\]\nThe root \\(t=-77\\) cannot represent a falling time, so \\(t=7\\). The depth is therefore\n\\[\n16(7)^2=784\\text{ feet}.\n\\]\nThe sound then takes \\(784/1120=0.7\\) seconds, giving the required total \\(7+0.7=7.7\\) seconds. The answer is \\(784\\) feet, choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
