"year": 1951,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 42,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If \\(x\\) equals \\[ \\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\cdots}}}}, \\] then:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(x=1\\)",
"B": "\\(0\\lt x\\lt1\\)",
"C": "\\(1\\lt x\\lt2\\)",
"D": "\\(x\\) is infinite",
"E": "\\(x\\gt2\\) but finite"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Interpret the infinite radical through its finite truncations. Let\n\\[\nu_1=1,\\qquad u_{n+1}=\\sqrt{1+u_n};\n\\]\nthen \\(u_n\\) is the expression with \\(n\\) square roots. We will find a number that these truncations approach.\n\nA nonnegative number unchanged by adding another outer square root satisfies\n\\[\nr=\\sqrt{1+r},\\qquad r^2-r-1=0.\n\\]\nThe quadratic formula gives \\(r=(1\\pm\\sqrt5)/2\\). The negative root cannot equal a principal square root. Let\n\\[\n\\phi=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}.\n\\]\nIt satisfies \\(\\phi^2=1+\\phi\\), and hence \\(\\phi=\\sqrt{1+\\phi}\\). Also, \\(1<\\sqrt5<3\\) gives \\(1<\\phi<2\\).\n\nStarting from \\(1=u_1<\\phi\\), the recursion preserves the bounds \\(1\\le u_n<\\phi\\): if they hold for \\(u_n\\), then\n\\[\n1\\le\\sqrt{1+u_n}<\\sqrt{1+\\phi}=\\phi.\n\\]\nTo prove that the truncations actually approach \\(\\phi\\), rationalize their difference:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\phi-u_{n+1}\n&=\\frac{\\phi^2-u_{n+1}^2}{\\phi+u_{n+1}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\phi-u_n}{\\phi+u_{n+1}}\\\\\n&\\le\\frac{\\phi-u_n}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe denominator is greater than \\(2\\), so it is nonzero. Repeating this bound gives\n\\[\n0\\le\\phi-u_n\\le\\frac{\\phi-1}{2^{n-1}}.\n\\]\nSuccessive halvings make this upper bound smaller than any prescribed positive error. Thus the finite radicals approach \\(\\phi\\), establishing the value of the infinite expression. Therefore\n\\[\nx=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2},\\qquad 1<x<2,\n\\]\nwhich is choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
