"batch": "NA",
"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "An automobile went up a hill at a speed of \\(10\\) miles an hour and down the same distance at a speed of \\(20\\) miles an hour. The average speed for the round trip was:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(12\\dfrac12\\) mph",
"B": "\\(13\\dfrac13\\) mph",
"C": "\\(14\\dfrac12\\) mph",
"D": "\\(15\\) mph",
"E": "None of these"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Average speed is the total distance divided by the total elapsed time. Let each one-way distance be \\(d>0\\) miles. Using time equal to distance divided by speed, the uphill and downhill times are \\(d/10\\) and \\(d/20\\) hours. Thus the total time is\n\\[\n\\frac d{10}+\\frac d{20}=\\frac{3d}{20}.\n\\]\nThe total distance is \\(2d\\), so the average speed is\n\\[\n\\frac{2d}{3d/20}=\\frac{40}{3}\n=13\\frac13\\text{ mph}.\n\\]\nThe positive distance \\(d\\) cancels, so the result applies to any hill length. The answer is \\(\\boxed{13\\dfrac13\\text{ mph}}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
