"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If \\(y=f(x)=\\dfrac{x+2}{x-1},\\) then it is incorrect to say:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(x=\\dfrac{y+2}{y-1}\\)",
"B": "\\(f(0)=-2\\)",
"C": "\\(f(1)=0\\)",
"D": "\\(f(-2)=0\\)",
"E": "\\(f(y)=x\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Check the denominator before evaluating the function. Since\n\\[\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{x-1},\n\\]\nits domain excludes \\(x=1\\). Therefore \\(f(1)\\) is undefined, so statement C is incorrect.\n\nTo confirm that the other statements are valid, direct substitution gives\n\\[\nf(0)=\\frac2{-1}=-2,\\qquad\nf(-2)=\\frac0{-3}=0.\n\\]\nThese establish B and D.\n\nFor A and E, rearrange the given relationship \\(y=(x+2)/(x-1)\\), where \\(x\\ne1\\):\n\\[\ny(x-1)=x+2,\n\\qquad x(y-1)=y+2.\n\\]\nDivision by \\(y-1\\) is allowed because\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{x+2}{x-1}-1=\\frac3{x-1}\\ne0.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nx=\\frac{y+2}{y-1}=f(y),\n\\]\nwhich verifies A and E, including the domain needed for \\(f(y)\\). The incorrect statement is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
