"year": 1952,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 12,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The sum to infinity of the terms of an infinite geometric progression is \\(6.\\) The sum of the first two terms is \\(4\\dfrac12.\\) The first term of the progression is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(3\\) or \\(1\\dfrac12\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\dfrac12\\)",
"D": "\\(6\\)",
"E": "\\(9\\) or \\(3\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Let the first term be \\(a\\) and the common ratio be \\(r\\), so the terms are \\(a,ar,ar^2,\\ldots\\). The finite, nonzero sum implies \\(a\\ne0\\) and \\(|r|<1\\).\n\nRemoving the first two terms leaves a tail with sum\n\\[\n6-4\\frac12=\\frac32.\n\\]\nThat tail is \\(ar^2+ar^3+\\cdots\\), which is \\(r^2\\) times the entire convergent series. Hence\n\\[\n6r^2=\\frac32,\n\\qquad r^2=\\frac14,\n\\qquad r=\\frac12\\text{ or }-\\frac12.\n\\]\nWe must retain both signs because a geometric progression may alternate in sign.\n\nRemoving just the first term leaves \\(r\\) times the whole series, so\n\\[\n6=a+6r,\n\\qquad a=6(1-r).\n\\]\nFor \\(r=1/2\\), this gives \\(a=3\\). For \\(r=-1/2\\), it gives \\(a=9\\). Both ratios satisfy \\(|r|<1\\), and the corresponding first-two-term sums are \\(3+3/2=9/2\\) and \\(9-9/2=9/2\\). Their infinite sums are respectively \\(3/(1-1/2)=6\\) and \\(9/(1+1/2)=6\\).\n\nThus both first terms are valid: \\(\\boxed{9\\text{ or }3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
