"year": 1952,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 30,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "When the sum of the first ten terms of an arithmetic progression is four times the sum of the first five terms, the ratio of the first term to the common difference is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1:2\\)",
"B": "\\(2:1\\)",
"C": "\\(1:4\\)",
"D": "\\(4:1\\)",
"E": "\\(1:1\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Let \\(a\\) be the first term and \\(d\\) the common difference. The arithmetic progression sum formula \\(S_n=na+\\frac{n(n-1)}2d\\) expresses both sums using the same two quantities:\n\\[\nS_5=5a+10d,\\qquad S_{10}=10a+45d.\n\\]\nThe given condition therefore becomes\n\\[\n10a+45d=4(5a+10d)=20a+40d,\n\\]\nso \\(d=2a\\).\n\nIf \\(d=0\\), this equation also forces \\(a=0\\). The resulting all-zero progression satisfies the sum condition, but its first-term-to-difference ratio is undefined.\n\nIf the intended ratio is defined, we may assume \\(d\\ne0\\) and divide to obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{d}=\\frac12.\n\\]\nThus the ratio is \\(\\boxed{1:2}\\), choice A, under the necessary nonzero-difference assumption.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1952/solutions",
"figureSources": {},
