"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 43,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The diameter of a circle is divided into \\(n\\) equal parts. On each part a semicircle is constructed. As \\(n\\) becomes very large, the sum of the lengths of the arcs of the semicircles approaches a length:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "Equal to the semi-circumference of the original\ncircle",
"B": "Equal to the diameter of the original circle",
"C": "Greater than the diameter but less than the semi-circumference\nof the original circle",
"D": "That is infinite",
"E": "Greater than the semi-circumference but finite"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Let the original diameter be \\(d>0\\), and let \\(n\\) be a positive integer. Each small semicircle has diameter \\(d/n\\). Its curved arc is half the circumference of its circle, so its length is\n\\[\n\\frac12\\pi\\frac dn=\\frac{\\pi d}{2n}.\n\\]\nThere are \\(n\\) such arcs, giving total length\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{\\pi d}{2n}=\\frac{\\pi d}{2}.\n\\]\nThis equals the original circle's semicircumference for every \\(n\\), so it also remains that value as \\(n\\) becomes very large. The answer is choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
