"year": 1952,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 50,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A line initially \\(1\\) inch long grows according to the following law, where the first term is the initial length. \\[ \\begin{gathered} 1+\\frac14\\sqrt2+\\frac14+\\frac1{16}\\sqrt2\\\\ {}+\\frac1{16}+\\frac1{64}\\sqrt2+\\frac1{64}+\\cdots. \\end{gathered} \\] If the growth process continues forever, the limit of the length of the line is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\infty\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac43\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac38\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac13(4+\\sqrt2)\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac23(4+\\sqrt2)\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Although the individual terms do not have a constant ratio, consecutive pairs do. Group the terms as\n\\[\n\\left(1+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{4}\\right)\n+\\left(\\frac14+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{16}\\right)\n+\\left(\\frac1{16}+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{64}\\right)+\\cdots.\n\\]\nEach pair is one fourth of the preceding pair. Let \\(a=1+\\sqrt2/4\\), and let \\(S_n\\) be the total of the first \\(n\\) complete pairs. Multiplying this finite geometric sum by \\(1/4\\) and subtracting cancels the interior terms:\n\\[\nS_n-\\frac14S_n=a-a4^{-n}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nS_n=\\frac{4+\\sqrt2}{3}\\left(1-4^{-n}\\right).\n\\]\nBecause \\(0<1/4<1\\), the quantity \\(4^{-n}\\) tends to zero. Every term is positive, so a partial total ending between complete pairs lies between the neighboring complete-pair totals and has the same limit. The limiting length is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{4+\\sqrt2}{3}\\text{ inches},\n\\]\nchoice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1952/solutions",
