"year": 1953,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 6,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Charles has \\(5q+1\\) quarters and Richard has \\(q+5\\) quarters. The difference in their money in dimes is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(10(q-1)\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac25(4q-4)\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac25(q-1)\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac52(q-1)\\)",
"E": "none of these"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "First interpret “difference” as Charles's amount minus Richard's. A quarter is worth 25 cents and a dime is worth 10 cents, so each quarter has the value of \\(25/10=5/2\\) dimes. Their signed difference, in dimes, is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac52\\bigl[(5q+1)-(q+5)\\bigr]\n=\\frac52(4q-4)=10(q-1).\n\\]\nUnder this interpretation, the answer is A.\n\nIf “difference” means the nonnegative gap between their amounts, we must instead take the absolute value, giving \\(10|q-1|\\) dimes. This agrees with A when \\(q\\ge1\\), including a zero difference at \\(q=1\\). However, \\(q=0\\) is also consistent with the stated counts: Charles then has 1 quarter and Richard has 5 quarters. Their unsigned difference is 10 dimes, whereas A gives \\(-10\\). Thus A requires the signed interpretation or the additional condition \\(q\\ge1\\); for the unsigned general formula, the answer is E.",
