"question": "The fraction \\[ \\frac{\\sqrt{a^2+x^2}-\\dfrac{x^2-a^2}{\\sqrt{a^2+x^2}}}{a^2+x^2} \\] reduces to:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{2a^2}{a^2+x^2}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{2x^2}{(a^2+x^2)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{2a^2}{(a^2+x^2)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{2x^2}{a^2+x^2}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "For real \\(a\\) and \\(x\\), the original denominators require \\(a^2+x^2>0\\), so \\(a\\) and \\(x\\) cannot both be zero. Let \\(S=a^2+x^2\\). To combine the two terms in the numerator, give them the common denominator \\(\\sqrt S\\). Since \\((\\sqrt S)^2=S\\),\n\\[\n\\sqrt S-\\frac{x^2-a^2}{\\sqrt S}\n=\\frac{S-(x^2-a^2)}{\\sqrt S}\n=\\frac{2a^2}{\\sqrt S}.\n\\]\nNow divide by the outer denominator \\(S\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{2a^2}{S\\sqrt S}\n=\\frac{2a^2}{(a^2+x^2)^{3/2}}.\n\\]\nThe original restriction remains in force. The answer is D.",
