"number": 29,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The number of significant digits in the measurement of the side of a square whose computed area is \\(1.1025\\) square inches to the nearest ten-thousandth of a square inch is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2\\)",
"B": "\\(3\\)",
"C": "\\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(5\\)",
"E": "\\(1\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "A rounded area can bound the side, but it cannot reveal how many digits were recorded in the original measurement. For example, the side reports \\(1.05000\\) and \\(1.050000\\) inches have six and seven significant digits, respectively, yet squaring and rounding either gives \\(1.1025\\) square inches.\n\nIf the intention is to determine how many significant digits can be reported from the area information alone, let \\(s>0\\) be the side length in inches. Rounding the area to the nearest \\(0.0001\\) gives the bounds\n\\[\n1.10245\\le s^2\\le1.10255.\n\\]\nThese weak bounds allow either rule for rounding an exact midpoint. To see how the side rounds, compare the squares of the rounding boundaries around \\(1.0500\\):\n\\[\n1.04995^2=1.1023950025<1.10245,\n\\]\n\\[\n1.05005^2=1.1026050025>1.10255.\n\\]\nSince positive numbers preserve their order when squared, every permissible side satisfies \\(1.04995<s<1.05005\\). Thus every such side rounds to \\(1.0500\\) inches, which has five significant digits.\n\nSix significant digits are not fixed: \\(1.04998^2=1.1024580004\\) and \\(1.05002^2=1.1025420004\\) both round to the stated area, but their six-significant-digit side reports differ. Therefore the inferred-precision interpretation gives \\(5\\), choice D; the original measurement's significant-digit count remains unspecified.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
