"number": 33,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The perimeter of an isosceles right triangle is \\(2p.\\) Its area is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\((2+\\sqrt2)p\\)",
"B": "\\((2-\\sqrt2)p\\)",
"C": "\\((3-2\\sqrt2)p^2\\)",
"D": "\\((1-2\\sqrt2)p^2\\)",
"E": "\\((3+2\\sqrt2)p^2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Let each equal leg have length \\(a>0\\). The Pythagorean theorem gives the hypotenuse as \\(a\\sqrt2\\), so the perimeter condition is\n\\[\n(2+\\sqrt2)a=2p.\n\\]\nHere \\(p>0\\), since the triangle has positive perimeter. Rationalizing the positive denominator gives\n\\[\na=\\frac{2p}{2+\\sqrt2}\n=\\frac{2p(2-\\sqrt2)}{4-2}\n=(2-\\sqrt2)p.\n\\]\nThe legs are perpendicular, so one is a base and the other its height. The area is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{a^2}{2}\n=\\frac{(2-\\sqrt2)^2p^2}{2}\n=(3-2\\sqrt2)p^2.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice C.",
