"year": 1954,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The sum of the numerical coefficients in the expansion of the binomial \\((a+b)^6\\) is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(32\\)",
"B": "\\(16\\)",
"C": "\\(64\\)",
"D": "\\(48\\)",
"E": "\\(7\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Method 1: Set both variables equal to 1\n\nSetting \\(a=b=1\\) turns every monomial in the expansion into \\(1\\), so each term becomes its numerical coefficient. The sum of those coefficients is therefore the value of the original expression at \\(a=b=1\\):\n\\[\n(1+1)^6=2^6=64.\n\\]\nThe sum is \\(64\\), choice C.\n\nMethod 2: Count choices from the six factors\n\nThink of \\((a+b)^6\\) as a product of six factors \\((a+b)\\). Distributing the product means choosing either \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) from each factor. There are two choices at each of six steps, giving \\(2^6=64\\) selections.\n\nEach selection contributes a monomial with coefficient \\(1\\). When several selections give the same powers of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), combining like terms adds their contributions into that monomial's coefficient. Every selection occurs exactly once in the distributive expansion, so adding all the collected coefficients counts all \\(64\\) selections once. The sum is \\(64\\), choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1954/solutions",
