"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 31,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In triangle \\(ABC,\\) \\(\\overline{AB}=\\overline{AC},\\) \\(\\angle A=40^\\circ.\\) Point \\(O\\) is within the triangle with \\(\\angle OBC\\cong\\angle OCA.\\) The number of degrees in angle \\(BOC\\) is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(110\\)",
"B": "\\(35\\)",
"C": "\\(140\\)",
"D": "\\(55\\)",
"E": "\\(70\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Because \\(AB=AC\\), the base angles at \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) are equal. The triangle angle sum gives\n\\[\n\\angle ABC=\\angle BCA\n=\\frac{180^\\circ-40^\\circ}{2}=70^\\circ.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\theta=\\angle OBC=\\angle OCA\\). Since \\(O\\) lies inside the triangle, \\(0<\\theta<70^\\circ\\), and ray \\(CO\\) splits angle \\(BCA\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BCO=70^\\circ-\\theta.\n\\]\nThe angles at \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) in triangle \\(BOC\\) consequently sum to \\(\\theta+(70^\\circ-\\theta)=70^\\circ\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle BOC=180^\\circ-70^\\circ=\\boxed{110^\\circ},\n\\]\nchoice A.",
