"year": 1954,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 36,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A boat has a speed of \\(15\\) mph in still water. In a stream that has a current of \\(5\\) mph it travels a certain distance downstream and returns. The ratio of the average speed for the round trip to the speed in still water is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac54\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac11\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac89\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac78\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac98\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Average speed is total distance divided by total travel time. The current makes the downstream speed \\(15+5=20\\) mph and the upstream speed \\(15-5=10\\) mph.\n\nLet the one-way distance be \\(d>0\\) miles. The downstream and upstream times are \\(d/20\\) and \\(d/10\\) hours, respectively. Thus\n\\[\n\\text{total time}=\\frac d{20}+\\frac d{10}=\\frac{3d}{20}.\n\\]\nThe boat covers \\(2d\\) miles, so its average speed is\n\\[\n\\frac{2d}{3d/20}=\\frac{40}{3}\\text{ mph}.\n\\]\nThe positive distance \\(d\\) cancels, so the result holds for any distance traveled. The requested ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{40/3}{15}=\\boxed{\\frac89},\n\\]\nchoice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1954/solutions",
