"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The graph of \\(x^2-4y^2=0:\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "is a hyperbola intersecting only the \\(x\\)-axis",
"B": "is a hyperbola intersecting only the \\(y\\)-axis",
"C": "is a hyperbola intersecting neither axis",
"D": "is a pair of straight lines",
"E": "does not exist"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The left side is a difference of squares, which lets us separate the equation into two linear conditions:\n\\[\nx^2-4y^2=(x-2y)(x+2y)=0.\n\\]\nA product of real numbers is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero. Hence the original equation is equivalent to\n\\[\nx-2y=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x+2y=0,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\ny=\\frac{x}{2}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad y=-\\frac{x}{2}.\n\\]\nThese equations represent two distinct straight lines through the origin. Every point on either line satisfies the original equation, and the factorization shows there are no other points. The graph is therefore a pair of straight lines, choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
