"batch": "NA",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The ratio of the areas of two concentric circles is \\(1:3.\\) If the radius of the smaller is \\(r,\\) then the difference between the radii is best approximated by:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0.41r\\)",
"B": "\\(0.73\\)",
"C": "\\(0.75\\)",
"D": "\\(0.73r\\)",
"E": "\\(0.75r\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Circle area depends on the square of the radius, so we first translate the area ratio into a radius ratio. Let the larger radius be \\(R\\), with \\(R>r>0\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi R^2}{\\pi r^2}=3,\n\\qquad\n\\left(\\frac Rr\\right)^2=3.\n\\]\nSince radii are positive, \\(R/r=\\sqrt3\\). Thus the difference is\n\\[\nR-r=(\\sqrt3-1)r.\n\\]\nTo compare the proposed coefficients, observe that\n\\[\n1.73^2=2.9929<3<3.0276=1.74^2.\n\\]\nTherefore the difference lies between \\(0.73r\\) and \\(0.74r\\), making \\(0.73r\\) closer than either \\(0.75r\\) or \\(0.41r\\). The fixed numbers in B and C do not describe the dependence on the unspecified radius. The answer is approximately \\(0.73r\\), choice D.",
