"question": "If each of two intersecting lines intersects a hyperbola and neither line is tangent to the hyperbola, then the possible number of points of intersection with the hyperbola is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\) or \\(3\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\) or \\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(3\\) or \\(4\\)",
"E": "\\(2,\\) \\(3,\\) or \\(4\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "A nontangent line parallel to an asymptote can meet a hyperbola in just one point. Thus we cannot assume that each line contributes two points.\n\nEvery line meets a nondegenerate hyperbola in at most two points: substituting a line equation into the hyperbola's quadratic equation gives a nonzero equation of degree at most two. Each of the two given lines contributes one or two points. Their common point must be counted only once if it lies on the hyperbola. Consequently, the total is between one and four.\n\nWe can realize every total using the hyperbola \\(H:x^2-y^2=1\\).\n\nFor one point, take \\(y=x-1\\) and \\(y=1-x\\). Substituting either equation into \\(H\\) gives\n\\[\nx^2-(x-1)^2=1,\n\\qquad 2x-1=1.\n\\]\nThus both lines meet \\(H\\) only at \\(P=(1,0)\\). The tangent at this vertex is the vertical line \\(x=1\\), so neither of these sloping lines is tangent.\n\nFor two points, take \\(y=0\\) and \\(y=x-1\\). Their combined intersections are exactly \\((1,0)\\) and \\((-1,0)\\).\n\nFor three points, take \\(y=0\\) and \\(y=2x-2\\). The second line gives\n\\[\nx^2-(2x-2)^2=1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(x-1)(3x-5)=0.\n\\]\nIts intersections are \\((1,0)\\) and \\((5/3,4/3)\\). Together with \\((-1,0)\\) from \\(y=0\\), there are exactly three distinct points.\n\nFor four points, take \\(y=0\\) and \\(x=2\\). They give the four distinct points\n\\[\n(1,0),\\quad(-1,0),\\quad(2,\\sqrt3),\\quad(2,-\\sqrt3).\n\\]\nEvery pair of lines listed above intersects. Apart from the already checked one-point lines, each listed line has two distinct intersections and therefore is not tangent.\n\nHence the possible totals are \\(1,2,3,4\\). No offered choice is complete; E omits \\(1\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
