"year": 1956,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 31,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In our number system the base is ten. If the base were changed to four you would count as follows: \\(1,\\) \\(2,\\) \\(3,\\) \\(10,\\) \\(11,\\) \\(12,\\) \\(13,\\) \\(20,\\) \\(21,\\) \\(22,\\) \\(23,\\) \\(30,\\) \\(\\ldots\\) The twentieth number would be:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(20\\)",
"B": "\\(38\\)",
"C": "\\(44\\)",
"D": "\\(104\\)",
"E": "\\(110\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The counting starts at one and increases by one each time, so the twentieth number represents twenty.\n\nIn base four, the rightmost places represent ones, fours, and sixteens: each place is worth four times the place to its right. Since\n\\[\n20=1\\cdot16+1\\cdot4+0\\cdot1,\n\\]\nthe digits are \\(1,1,0\\). Thus the twentieth number is \\(\\boxed{110}\\) in base four, choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
