"year": 1956,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 33,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The number \\(\\sqrt2\\) is equal to:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "a rational fraction",
"B": "a finite decimal",
"C": "\\(1.41421\\)",
"D": "an infinite repeating decimal",
"E": "an infinite non-repeating decimal"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Terminating and eventually repeating decimals are rational numbers, so we can decide the question by testing whether \\(\\sqrt2\\) can be a fraction.\n\nSuppose \\(\\sqrt2=p/q\\), where \\(p,q\\) are positive integers and the fraction is in lowest terms. Squaring gives\n\\[\np^2=2q^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(p^2\\) is even. An odd integer has an odd square, so \\(p\\) must be even. Write \\(p=2r\\), where \\(r\\) is an integer. Substitution gives\n\\[\n4r^2=2q^2,\n\\qquad q^2=2r^2.\n\\]\nThe same reasoning shows that \\(q\\) is even. But then \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) share a factor of two, contradicting that the fraction was in lowest terms.\n\nTherefore \\(\\sqrt2\\) is irrational. Its decimal expansion is infinite and non-repeating, choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1956/solutions",
