"question": "Comparing the numbers \\(10^{-49}\\) and \\(2\\cdot10^{-50},\\) we may say:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "the first exceeds the second by \\(8\\cdot10^{-1}\\)",
"B": "the first exceeds the second by \\(2\\cdot10^{-1}\\)",
"C": "the first exceeds the second by\n\\(8\\cdot10^{-50}\\)",
"D": "the second is five times the first",
"E": "the first exceeds the second by \\(5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Write both numbers as multiples of the same power of \\(10\\). By the meaning of negative exponents,\n\\[\n10^{-49}=\\frac{1}{10^{49}}\n=\\frac{10}{10^{50}}\n=10\\cdot10^{-50}.\n\\]\nTheir difference is therefore\n\\[\n10^{-49}-2\\cdot10^{-50}\n=(10-2)\\cdot10^{-50}\n=8\\cdot10^{-50}.\n\\]\nThis difference is positive, so the first exceeds the second by \\(8\\cdot10^{-50}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
