"number": 38,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(r\\) be the distance from the origin to a point \\(P\\) with coordinates \\(x\\) and \\(y.\\) Designate the ratio \\(\\frac{y}{r}\\) by \\(s\\) and the ratio \\(\\frac{x}{r}\\) by \\(c.\\) Then the values of \\(s^2-c^2\\) are limited to the numbers:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "less than \\(-1\\) and greater than \\(+1,\\) both excluded",
"B": "less than \\(-1\\) and greater than \\(+1,\\) both included",
"C": "between \\(-1\\) and \\(+1,\\) both excluded",
"D": "between \\(-1\\) and \\(+1,\\) both included",
"E": "\\(-1\\) and \\(+1\\) only"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The ratios require \\(P\\ne(0,0)\\), so \\(r>0\\). By the Pythagorean theorem, \\(r^2=x^2+y^2\\). Dividing by \\(r^2\\) gives the useful relation\n\\[\ns^2+c^2=\\frac{y^2+x^2}{r^2}=1.\n\\]\nBoth squares are nonnegative, so \\(0\\le s^2\\le1\\). Therefore\n\\[\ns^2-c^2=2s^2-1,\n\\qquad -1\\le s^2-c^2\\le1.\n\\]\nTo check that every value in this interval is possible, let \\(u\\) be any number in \\([0,1]\\) and choose\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(\\sqrt{1-u},\\sqrt u\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe square roots are defined, this point has \\(r=1\\), and it gives \\(s^2-c^2=2u-1\\). As \\(u\\) ranges from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\), this expression takes every value from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\). In particular, \\(P=(1,0)\\) gives \\(-1\\), and \\(P=(0,1)\\) gives \\(1\\).\n\nThus the range is \\(\\boxed{[-1,1]}\\), with both endpoints included, choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1958/solutions",
