"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 6,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Given the true statement: If a quadrilateral is a square, then it is a rectangle. It follows that, of the converse and the inverse of this true statement:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "only the converse is true",
"B": "only the inverse is true",
"C": "both are true",
"D": "neither is true",
"E": "the inverse is true, but the converse is sometimes true"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The converse switches the hypothesis and conclusion: “If a quadrilateral is a rectangle, then it is a square.” A \\(1\\)-by-\\(2\\) rectangle has four right angles but does not have four equal sides, so it disproves the converse.\n\nThe inverse negates both parts without switching them: “If a quadrilateral is not a square, then it is not a rectangle.” The same \\(1\\)-by-\\(2\\) rectangle is not a square, yet it is a rectangle, so it also disproves the inverse.\n\nEach conditional would have to hold for every quadrilateral satisfying its hypothesis. These counterexamples show that neither is true, choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1959/solutions",
