"year": 1959,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 37,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "When simplified the product \\[ \\begin{aligned} &\\left(1-\\dfrac13\\right)\\left(1-\\dfrac14\\right)\\\\ &\\quad{}\\cdot\\left(1-\\dfrac15\\right)\\cdots\\left(1-\\dfrac1n\\right) \\end{aligned} \\] becomes:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac1n\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac2n\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{2(n-1)}n\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{n(n+1)}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{n(n+1)}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "For the consecutive product, \\(n\\) is an integer with \\(n\\ge3\\). Rewrite each factor as\n\\[\n1-\\frac1k=\\frac{k-1}{k}.\n\\]\nThe product becomes\n\\[\n\\frac23\\cdot\\frac34\\cdot\\frac45\\cdots\\frac{n-1}{n}.\n\\]\nEvery integer from \\(3\\) through \\(n-1\\) occurs once in a numerator and once in a denominator, so these nonzero factors cancel. Only the first numerator, \\(2\\), and the last denominator, \\(n\\), remain. Thus the product is \\(\\boxed{\\frac2n}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\). For \\(n=3\\), the single factor is already \\(2/3\\), agreeing with the formula.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1959/solutions",
