"year": 1960,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 6,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The circumference of a circle is \\(100\\) inches. The side of a square inscribed in this circle, expressed in inches, is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{25\\sqrt2}{\\pi}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{50\\sqrt2}{\\pi}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{100}{\\pi}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{100\\sqrt2}{\\pi}\\)",
"E": "\\(50\\sqrt2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "The circumference determines the circle's diameter \\(d\\). Since circumference equals \\(\\pi\\) times diameter,\n\\[\nd=\\frac{100}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nThe square's diagonals are equal and bisect each other, so their intersection is equidistant from all four vertices. It is therefore the center of the circle through those vertices. Each diagonal passes through the center and has endpoints on the circle, so it is a diameter.\n\nLet \\(s>0\\) be the square's side length. A diagonal forms a right triangle with two sides of length \\(s\\). By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\n2s^2=d^2,\n\\qquad s=\\frac{d}{\\sqrt2}\n=\\frac{50\\sqrt2}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{50\\sqrt2}{\\pi}}\\) inches, choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1960/solutions",
