"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Circle I passes through the center of, and is tangent to, circle II. The area of circle I is \\(4\\) square inches. Then the area of circle II, in square inches, is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(8\\)",
"B": "\\(8\\sqrt2\\)",
"C": "\\(8\\sqrt\\pi\\)",
"D": "\\(16\\)",
"E": "\\(16\\sqrt2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Let \\(r>0\\) and \\(R>0\\) be the radii of circles I and II, respectively, and let \\(d\\) be the distance between their centers. Circle I passes through the center of circle II, so \\(d=r\\).\n\nAt tangency, the centers and the contact point lie on one line. For external tangency the center distance is the sum of the radii, which here would give \\(r=r+R\\). This would force \\(R=0\\), so external tangency is impossible.\n\nFor internal tangency the center distance is the absolute difference of the radii. Thus\n\\[\n|R-r|=r.\n\\]\nThis gives \\(R=2r\\) or \\(R=0\\). A circle has positive radius, so \\(R=2r\\). Its area is therefore\n\\[\n\\pi R^2=\\pi(2r)^2=4\\pi r^2=4(4)=16.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{16}\\) square inches, choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
