"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 21,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The diagonal of square I is \\(a+b.\\) The perimeter of square II with twice the area of I is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\((a+b)^2\\)",
"B": "\\(\\sqrt2(a+b)^2\\)",
"C": "\\(2(a+b)\\)",
"D": "\\(\\sqrt8(a+b)\\)",
"E": "\\(4(a+b)\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Let \\(d=a+b\\), which is positive because it is a diagonal length, and let \\(s>0\\) be the side of square I. A diagonal divides the square into right triangles with legs \\(s,s\\), so the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nd^2=s^2+s^2=2s^2.\n\\]\nSince square I has area \\(s^2\\), twice its area is exactly \\(d^2\\). Thus square II has area \\(d^2\\), and its positive side length is \\(d\\). Its perimeter is therefore\n\\[\n4d=\\boxed{4(a+b)},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
