"year": 1961,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 2,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "An automobile travels \\(\\frac{a}{6}\\) feet in \\(r\\) seconds. If this rate is maintained for \\(3\\) minutes, how many yards does it travel in the \\(3\\) minutes?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{a}{1080r}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{30r}{a}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{30a}{r}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{10r}{a}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{10a}{r}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "First express the speed per second so that we can use the full travel time. Since \\(r>0\\) is an elapsed time, the speed is\n\\[\n\\frac{a/6}{r}=\\frac{a}{6r}\n\\quad\\text{feet per second}.\n\\]\nThree minutes is \\(3\\cdot60=180\\) seconds. Using distance equal to speed times time, the automobile travels\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{6r}\\cdot180=\\frac{30a}{r}\n\\quad\\text{feet}.\n\\]\nThere are \\(3\\) feet in a yard, so divide by \\(3\\). The distance is \\(\\boxed{10a/r}\\) yards, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
