"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Doubling both the base and the exponent gives\n\\[\nr=(2a)^{2b}.\n\\]\nFirst assume \\(a>0\\) and \\(x>0\\), with real \\(b\\ne0\\). These are additional assumptions, not printed conditions. Positive-base power laws give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=((2a)^2)^b\\\\\n&=(4a^2)^b\\\\\n&=a^b(4a)^b.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nComparing this with \\(r=a^bx^b\\), we may divide by \\(a^b>0\\), obtaining \\(x^b=(4a)^b\\). Raising both positive sides to the power \\(1/b\\) gives \\(x=4a\\), the intended choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).\n\nHowever, the printed assumptions do not determine \\(x\\) uniquely. If \\(a=1\\) and \\(b=2\\), the original equation becomes\n\\[\n2^4=1^2x^2,\n\\]\nso both \\(x=4\\) and \\(x=-4\\) satisfy it. Also, if \\(a=0\\) and \\(b=1\\), the equation becomes \\(0=0\\cdot x\\), which holds for every real \\(x\\). In this case the distinct listed values \\(0\\), \\(2\\), and \\(4\\) all work.\n\nThus there is no unique value as printed. Under the additional positive-base assumptions, the answer is \\(x=4a\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
