"year": 1961,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 14,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A rhombus is given with one diagonal twice the length of the other diagonal. Express the side of the rhombus in terms of \\(K,\\) where \\(K\\) is the area of the rhombus in square inches.",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\sqrt K\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac12\\sqrt{2K}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac13\\sqrt{3K}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac14\\sqrt{4K}\\)",
"E": "none of these are correct"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The diagonals of a rhombus bisect one another at right angles, so their half-lengths form a right triangle with a side of the rhombus as hypotenuse. Let the shorter diagonal have length \\(d>0\\); the longer one then has length \\(2d\\). The diagonal area formula gives\n\\[\nK=\\frac{d(2d)}{2}=d^2.\n\\]\nIf the side length is \\(s\\), the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\ns^2=\\left(\\frac d2\\right)^2+d^2\n=\\frac{5d^2}{4}=\\frac{5K}{4}.\n\\]\nSince a side length is positive,\n\\[\ns=\\frac{\\sqrt{5K}}{2}\\text{ inches}.\n\\]\nFor \\(K>0\\), this is greater than \\(\\sqrt K\\), whereas every expression in A-D is at most \\(\\sqrt K\\). Therefore none of those expressions is correct, and the answer is E.",
