"year": 1961,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If \\(x\\) men working \\(x\\) hours a day for \\(x\\) days produce \\(x\\) articles, then the number of articles (not necessarily an integer) produced by \\(y\\) men working \\(y\\) hours a day for \\(y\\) days is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{x^3}{y^2}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{y^2}{x^3}\\)",
"E": "\\(y\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Assume \\(x,y>0\\) and that all men work at the same constant production rate per worker-hour, so output is proportional to the number of men, daily hours, and days. Counting total worker-hours combines these three factors.\n\nThe first group supplies\n\\[\nx\\cdot x\\cdot x=x^3\n\\]\nworker-hours and produces \\(x\\) articles. Its production rate is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{x^3}=\\frac{1}{x^2}\n\\]\narticles per worker-hour; this division is valid because \\(x>0\\). The second group supplies \\(y\\cdot y\\cdot y=y^3\\) worker-hours, so it produces\n\\[\ny^3\\cdot\\frac{1}{x^2}=\\frac{y^3}{x^2}\n\\]\narticles. Under the stated production assumption, the answer is choice B.",
