"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let the bisectors of the exterior angles at \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) of triangle \\(ABC\\) meet at \\(D.\\) Then, if all measurements are in degrees, angle \\(BDC\\) equals:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac12(90-A)\\)",
"B": "\\(90-A\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac12(180-A)\\)",
"D": "\\(180-A\\)",
"E": "\\(180-2A\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Write \\(A,B,C\\) for the degree measures of the interior angles of triangle \\(ABC\\). Use the exterior angles formed by \\(BC\\) and the extension of \\(AB\\) past \\(B\\), and by \\(CB\\) and the extension of \\(AC\\) past \\(C\\). Their bisectors meet at \\(D\\) on the side of \\(BC\\) opposite \\(A\\).\n\nEach exterior angle supplements its adjacent interior angle. Bisecting gives the two angles of triangle \\(BDC\\) at the ends of its base:\n\\[\n\\angle CBD=\\frac{180-B}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\angle BCD=\\frac{180-C}{2}.\n\\]\nSubtracting these from the triangle's angle sum yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BDC\n&=180-\\frac{180-B}{2}-\\frac{180-C}{2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{B+C}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(A+B+C=180\\), the required angle is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{180-A}{2}}\\) degrees, choice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
