"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "First take out the common factor \\(x\\). The remaining expression is a difference of squares, and that pattern can be used repeatedly:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^9-x\n&=x(x^8-1)\\\\\n&=x(x^4-1)(x^4+1)\\\\\n&=x(x^2-1)(x^2+1)(x^4+1)\\\\\n&=x(x-1)(x+1)(x^2+1)(x^4+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTo count these as the final factors, we must check that the last two cannot split further with integer coefficients. The quadratic \\(x^2+1\\) is positive for every real \\(x\\), so it has no real root and cannot split into linear factors.\n\nLikewise, \\(x^4+1\\) has no real root, so it has no linear factor. Any factorization into nonconstant integer polynomials would therefore have to consist of two quadratics. Since the leading coefficient is \\(1\\), their signs can be chosen so both are monic. The zero coefficient of \\(x^3\\) would force the form\n\\[\nx^4+1=(x^2+ax+b)(x^2-ax+d),\n\\]\nwhere \\(a,b,d\\) are integers. The constant coefficient gives \\(bd=1\\), so either \\(b=d=1\\) or \\(b=d=-1\\). The coefficient of \\(x^2\\) must also be zero, giving\n\\[\nb+d-a^2=0,\n\\qquad a^2=2b.\n\\]\nFor \\(b=1\\), this requires the integer square \\(a^2\\) to equal \\(2\\); for \\(b=-1\\), it requires \\(a^2=-2\\). Both are impossible. Thus the quartic cannot split further.\n\nThe displayed product consequently contains exactly \\(\\boxed{5}\\) nonconstant factors, each already irreducible over the integers. The answer is choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1962/solutions",
