"year": 1964,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 9,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A jobber buys an article at \\(\\$24\\) less \\(12\\dfrac12\\%.\\) He then wishes to sell the article at a gain of \\(33\\dfrac13\\%\\) of his cost after allowing a \\(20\\%\\) discount on his marked price. At what price, in dollars, should the article be marked?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(25.20\\)",
"B": "\\(30.00\\)",
"C": "\\(33.60\\)",
"D": "\\(40.00\\)",
"E": "none of these"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The percentages refer to different prices, so first find the actual cost. Since \\(12\\frac12\\%=\\frac18\\), the jobber pays\n\\[\n24\\left(1-\\frac18\\right)=24\\cdot\\frac78=21\n\\]\ndollars. A gain of \\(33\\frac13\\%=\\frac13\\) of his cost requires a selling price of\n\\[\n21\\left(1+\\frac13\\right)=28\n\\]\ndollars.\n\nLet \\(M\\) be the marked price in dollars. A \\(20\\%\\) discount leaves \\(80\\%=\\frac45\\) of that price, so\n\\[\n\\frac45M=28,\n\\qquad M=28\\cdot\\frac54=35.\n\\]\nThe article should be marked at \\(\\boxed{\\$35.00}\\). This is not among the listed prices, so the answer is E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
