"number": 25,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The set of values of \\(m\\) for which \\(x^2+3xy+x+my-m\\) has two factors, with integer coefficients, which are linear in \\(x\\) and \\(y,\\) is precisely:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0,\\) \\(12,\\) \\(-12\\)",
"B": "\\(0,\\) \\(12\\)",
"C": "\\(12,\\) \\(-12\\)",
"D": "\\(12\\)",
"E": "\\(0\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "The missing \\(y^2\\) term strongly restricts the factors. Write a possible factorization as\n\\[\n(px+qy+r)(sx+ty+u),\n\\]\nwhere all six coefficients are integers. The coefficient of \\(x^2\\) gives \\(ps=1\\), so \\(p=s=1\\) or \\(p=s=-1\\). In the latter case, changing the sign of both factors makes both \\(x\\)-coefficients equal to \\(1\\).\n\nWith that normalization, the \\(y^2\\) and \\(xy\\) coefficients give \\(qt=0\\) and \\(q+t=3\\). Thus one factor has no \\(y\\) term and the other has \\(y\\)-coefficient \\(3\\). After swapping the factors if needed, every possible factorization has the form\n\\[\n(x+c)(x+3y+d),\\qquad c,d\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nExpanding and matching the remaining coefficients gives\n\\[\nc+d=1,\\qquad 3c=m,\\qquad cd=-m.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(d=1-c\\) and \\(m=3c\\) into the last equation:\n\\[\nc(1-c)=-3c,\n\\qquad c(4-c)=0.\n\\]\nHence \\(c=0\\) or \\(c=4\\), giving \\(m=0\\) or \\(m=12\\). We did not divide by \\(c\\), so the zero case remains included.\n\nBoth candidates give the required integer linear factors:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=0:&\\quad x(x+3y+1),\\\\\nm=12:&\\quad (x+4)(x+3y-3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe factor structure covered every possible integer linear factorization, so the set is precisely \\(\\{0,12\\}\\), choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1964/solutions",
