"year": 1964,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 26,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In a ten-mile race First beats Second by \\(2\\) miles and First beats Third by \\(4\\) miles. If the runners maintain constant speeds throughout the race, by how many miles does Second beat Third?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\dfrac14\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\dfrac12\\)",
"D": "\\(2\\dfrac34\\)",
"E": "\\(3\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "When First finishes the ten miles, Second has run \\(10-2=8\\) miles and Third has run \\(10-4=6\\) miles. These distances were traveled in the same time, so Third's speed is \\(\\frac68=\\frac34\\) of Second's speed.\n\nBecause both speeds remain constant, Third always travels \\(\\frac34\\) as far as Second in the same elapsed time. When Second reaches ten miles, Third has therefore run\n\\[\n10\\cdot\\frac34=7\\frac12\\text{ miles}.\n\\]\nThus Second beats Third by \\(10-7\\frac12=2\\frac12\\) miles, choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
