"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 2,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A regular hexagon is inscribed in a circle. The ratio of the length of a side of the hexagon to the length of the shorter of the arcs intercepted by the side is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1:1\\)",
"B": "\\(1:6\\)",
"C": "\\(1:\\pi\\)",
"D": "\\(3:\\pi\\)",
"E": "\\(6:\\pi\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Let the circle have radius \\(r>0\\). Joining its center to the six vertices divides the full turn into six equal central angles, each\n\\[\n\\frac{360^\\circ}{6}=60^\\circ.\n\\]\nThe triangle formed by two adjacent radii and a hexagon side is isosceles. Its remaining angles are also \\(60^\\circ\\), so it is equilateral and the side length is \\(r\\).\n\nThe intercepted shorter arc has central angle \\(60^\\circ<180^\\circ\\), so its length is one-sixth of the circumference:\n\\[\n\\frac16(2\\pi r)=\\frac{\\pi r}{3}.\n\\]\nThus the requested ratio is\n\\[\nr:\\frac{\\pi r}{3}=\\boxed{3:\\pi},\n\\]\nchoice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1965/solutions",
