"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 13,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(n\\) be the number of number-pairs \\((x,y)\\) which satisfy \\(5y-3x=15\\) and \\(x^2+y^2\\leq16.\\) Then \\(n\\) is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\)",
"D": "more than two, but finite",
"E": "greater than any finite number"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "No integer restriction is stated, so we interpret \\((x,y)\\) as real ordered pairs. To prove that there are infinitely many, it is enough to find an entire interval of allowable \\(x\\)-values.\n\nThe equation determines\n\\[\ny=3+\\frac35x.\n\\]\nChoose any real \\(x\\) with \\(-1\\le x\\le0\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac{12}{5}\\le y\\le3.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(x^2\\le1\\) and, since \\(y\\) is positive, \\(y^2\\le9\\). Therefore\n\\[\nx^2+y^2\\le1+9=10<16.\n\\]\nEvery such pair satisfies both original conditions. The interval \\([-1,0]\\) contains infinitely many real numbers, and different \\(x\\)-values produce different ordered pairs. Hence \\(n\\) is greater than any finite number, choice E.",
