"batch": "NA",
"number": 3,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If the arithmetic mean of two numbers is \\(6\\) and their geometric mean is \\(10,\\) then an equation with the given two numbers as roots is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(x^2+12x+100=0\\)",
"B": "\\(x^2+6x+100=0\\)",
"C": "\\(x^2-12x-10=0\\)",
"D": "\\(x^2-12x+100=0\\)",
"E": "\\(x^2-6x+100=0\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "First interpret the stated means as formal sum-and-product data. If the proposed numbers are \\(u\\) and \\(v\\), the arithmetic mean gives\n\\[\nu+v=2\\cdot6=12,\n\\]\nand the geometric-mean condition gives\n\\[\nuv=10^2=100.\n\\]\nTo construct a quadratic with these numbers as roots, expand its factors:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x-u)(x-v)\n&=x^2-(u+v)x+uv\\\\\n&=x^2-12x+100.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the formal coefficient calculation produces choice \\(\\mathrm D\\).\n\nWe must also check whether the proposed numbers can be real. For real \\(u,v\\), a square cannot be negative, but the stated sum and product imply\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u-v)^2\n&=(u+v)^2-4uv\\\\\n&=12^2-4\\cdot100=-256.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis is impossible. Under the usual real-number definition of geometric mean, no pair satisfies the question's premises. The equation \\(\\boxed{x^2-12x+100=0}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm D\\), is the formal intended answer, conditional on interpreting the means beyond their ordinary real-number setting.",
