"year": 1966,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Circle \\(\\mathrm{I}\\) is circumscribed about a given square and circle \\(\\mathrm{II}\\) is inscribed in the given square. If \\(r\\) is the ratio of the area of circle \\(\\mathrm{I}\\) to that of circle \\(\\mathrm{II},\\) then \\(r\\) equals:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\sqrt2\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(\\sqrt3\\)",
"D": "\\(2\\sqrt2\\)",
"E": "\\(2\\sqrt3\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Let \\(a>0\\) be the radius of circle \\(\\mathrm{II}\\). Its diameter spans the square between opposite sides, so the square has side length \\(2a\\). Both circles have the square's center as their center.\n\nThe center, a side midpoint, and an endpoint of that side form a right triangle. Its two legs both have length \\(a\\), and its hypotenuse is the radius \\(R\\) of circle \\(\\mathrm I\\). The Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nR^2=a^2+a^2=2a^2.\n\\]\nSince a circle's area is \\(\\pi\\) times its radius squared,\n\\[\nr=\\frac{\\pi R^2}{\\pi a^2}=2.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{r=2}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm B\\).",
