"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The length of the common chord of two intersecting circles is \\(16\\) feet. If the radii are \\(10\\) feet and \\(17\\) feet, a possible value for the distance between the centers of the circles, expressed in feet, is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(27\\)",
"B": "\\(21\\)",
"C": "\\(\\sqrt{389}\\)",
"D": "\\(15\\)",
"E": "undetermined"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Let \\(P,Q\\) be the common chord's endpoints and \\(M\\) its midpoint. Then \\(PM=8\\) feet. In each circle, the line from its center to the midpoint of this chord is perpendicular to the chord. Thus both centers lie on the same perpendicular through \\(M\\).\n\nJoining either center to \\(P\\) forms a right triangle with one leg \\(8\\) and hypotenuse equal to that circle's radius. The two distances from the centers to \\(M\\) are therefore\n\\[\n\\sqrt{10^2-8^2}=6,\n\\qquad\n\\sqrt{17^2-8^2}=15.\n\\]\nIf the centers lie on opposite sides of \\(M\\), their distance is \\(6+15=21\\) feet. If they lie on the same side, their distance is \\(15-6=9\\) feet. Both arrangements are possible: placing the centers at these distances along the perpendicular leaves \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) on both circles.\n\nThus \\(\\boxed{21\\text{ feet}}\\) is a possible center distance, choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1966/solutions",
