"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If \\(\\dfrac ab\\lt-\\dfrac cd,\\) where \\(a,\\) \\(b,\\) \\(c,\\) \\(d\\) are real numbers and \\(bd\\ne0,\\) then:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(a\\) must be negative",
"B": "\\(a\\) must be positive",
"C": "\\(a\\) must not be zero",
"D": "\\(a\\) can be negative or zero, but not positive",
"E": "\\(a\\) can be positive, negative, or zero"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "To determine whether a sign is forced, we can look for examples satisfying the inequality with each possible sign of \\(a\\).\n\nChoose \\(b=d=1\\) and \\(c=-2\\). These choices satisfy \\(bd\\ne0\\), and the inequality becomes\n\\[\na<2.\n\\]\nThis holds when \\(a=-1\\), when \\(a=0\\), and when \\(a=1\\). Thus a negative value, zero, and a positive value are all possible. These cover the three possible signs of a real number, so the answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
