"domain": [],
"skill": [],
"year": 1967,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 25,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For every odd number \\(p\\gt1\\) we have:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\((p-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}}-1\\) is divisible by\n\\(p-2\\)",
"B": "\\((p-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}}+1\\) is divisible by \\(p\\)",
"C": "\\((p-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}}\\) is divisible by \\(p\\)",
"D": "\\((p-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}}+1\\) is divisible by \\(p+1\\)",
"E": "\\((p-1)^{\\frac{p-1}{2}}-1\\) is divisible by \\(p-1\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Method 1: Track the remainder of the power\n\nIn choice A, the proposed divisor is one less than the base. This makes the remainder especially simple. Let\n\\[\nk=\\frac{p-1}{2},\\qquad d=p-2.\n\\]\nBecause \\(p\\) is an odd integer greater than \\(1\\), \\(k\\) is a positive integer and \\(d\\ge1\\). If \\(p=3\\), then \\(d=1\\), so A holds.\n\nFor \\(p\\ge5\\), the base \\(p-1=d+1\\) leaves remainder \\(1\\) upon division by \\(d\\). Multiplying two integers with this remainder preserves it: for integers \\(u,v\\),\n\\[\n(du+1)(dv+1)=d(duv+u+v)+1.\n\\]\nThus every positive integer power of \\(p-1\\) also leaves remainder \\(1\\). In particular, subtracting \\(1\\) from \\((p-1)^k\\) gives a multiple of \\(d=p-2\\). This proves A for every allowed \\(p\\).\n\nTo rule out the other universal claims, take \\(p=5\\), when the power is \\(4^2=16\\). B and C would require \\(5\\) to divide \\(17\\) and \\(16\\), respectively. D would require \\(6\\) to divide \\(17\\), and E would require \\(4\\) to divide \\(15\\). All four fail. Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\mathrm{A}}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Exhibit an integer factor\n\nChoice A suggests factoring because its proposed divisor, \\(p-2\\), is one less than the base \\(p-1\\). Let\n\\[\na=p-1,\\qquad k=\\frac{p-1}{2}.\n\\]\nHere \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(k\\) is a positive integer.\n\nLet \\(S\\) be the sum of \\(k\\) terms starting at \\(1\\), with each subsequent term obtained by multiplying by \\(a\\):\n\\[\nS=1+a+\\cdots+a^{k-1}.\n\\]\nWhen \\(k=1\\), this means simply \\(S=1\\). Multiplying \\(S\\) by \\(a\\) shifts every power up by one. Subtracting \\(S\\) then cancels all intermediate powers, leaving\n\\[\n(a-1)S=aS-S=a^k-1.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a=p-1\\) gives\n\\[\n(p-1)^k-1=(p-2)S.\n\\]\nSince \\(S\\) is an integer, this proves divisibility by \\(p-2\\). The smallest allowed value, \\(p=3\\), is included: then \\(k=1\\) and \\(S=1\\).\n\nThe other choices all fail at \\(p=5\\). The power is \\(16\\), but \\(17\\) and \\(16\\) are not divisible by \\(5\\), \\(17\\) is not divisible by \\(6\\), and \\(15\\) is not divisible by \\(4\\). Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\mathrm{A}}\\).",
