"batch": "NA",
"number": 40,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Located inside equilateral triangle \\(ABC\\) is a point \\(P\\) such that \\(PA=8,\\) \\(PB=6,\\) and \\(PC=10.\\) To the nearest integer the area of triangle \\(ABC\\) is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(159\\)",
"B": "\\(131\\)",
"C": "\\(95\\)",
"D": "\\(79\\)",
"E": "\\(50\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Method 1: Subtract distance equations\n\nCoordinate distance equations are useful here because subtracting them removes their shared squared terms. Let \\(s>0\\) be the equilateral triangle's side length, and place\n\\[\nA=(0,0),\\quad B=(s,0),\\quad\nC=\\left(\\frac{s}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt3s}{2}\\right).\n\\]\nThe height \\(\\sqrt3s/2\\) follows from the Pythagorean theorem after bisecting the equilateral triangle. Write \\(P=(u,v)\\). Since \\(P\\) is inside the triangle, \\(v>0\\).\n\nThe given distances yield\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^2+v^2&=64,\\\\\n(u-s)^2+v^2&=36,\\\\\n\\left(u-\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2+\n\\left(v-\\frac{\\sqrt3s}{2}\\right)^2&=100.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and third gives\n\\[\n2su=s^2+28,\n\\qquad\nsu+\\sqrt3sv=s^2-36.\n\\]\nBecause \\(s>0\\), we may solve these for\n\\[\nu=\\frac{s^2+28}{2s},\n\\qquad\nv=\\frac{s^2-100}{2\\sqrt3s}.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(v>0\\) forces \\(t=s^2>100\\). Substituting these expressions into \\(u^2+v^2=64\\) and multiplying by \\(12t>0\\) gives\n\\[\n3(t+28)^2+(t-100)^2=768t.\n\\]\nExpanding and completing the square produces\n\\[\nt^2-200t+3088=0,\n\\qquad\n(t-100)^2=6912.\n\\]\nThus \\(t=100\\pm48\\sqrt3\\). The minus sign would give \\(v<0\\), so it is excluded by the interior condition. Therefore \\(s^2=100+48\\sqrt3\\).\n\nThe area is\n\\[\n\\frac12s\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3s}{2}\\right)\n=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}(100+48\\sqrt3)\n=36+25\\sqrt3\\approx79.30.\n\\]\nTo the nearest integer, the area is \\(\\boxed{79}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Create a 6-8-10 right triangle\n\nThe distances \\(6,8,10\\) suggest constructing a right triangle, since \\(6^2+8^2=10^2\\). A \\(60^\\circ\\) rotation fits the equilateral triangle and creates the needed sides.\n\nLet \\(s\\) denote the side length. Rotate about \\(A\\) through \\(60^\\circ\\) in the direction taking \\(B\\) to \\(C\\), and let \\(Q\\) be the image of \\(P\\). Rotation preserves distances, so \\(AQ=AP=8\\) and \\(CQ=BP=6\\). Also, \\(\\angle PAQ=60^\\circ\\), making \\(APQ\\) equilateral and giving \\(PQ=8\\).\n\nTriangle \\(PQC\\) therefore has sides \\(8,6,10\\). By the converse of the Pythagorean theorem, \\(QP\\perp QC\\). Since \\(\\angle AQP=60^\\circ\\), the angle \\(\\angle AQC\\) is either \\(30^\\circ\\) or \\(150^\\circ\\).\n\nDrop a perpendicular from \\(C\\) to the line \\(AQ\\), with foot \\(N\\). The resulting \\(30^\\circ\\) right triangle has hypotenuse \\(QC=6\\), so\n\\[\nCN=3,\\qquad QN=3\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nThe foot \\(N\\) can lie toward \\(A\\) from \\(Q\\) or in the opposite direction. Since \\(AQ=8>3\\sqrt3\\), these possibilities give \\(AN=8-3\\sqrt3\\) or \\(AN=8+3\\sqrt3\\). Hence\n\\[\ns^2=AC^2=(8\\pm3\\sqrt3)^2+3^2\n=100\\pm48\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nTo choose the sign, use the fact that \\(P\\) is inside. Extend ray \\(CP\\) to meet \\(AB\\) at \\(T\\), and let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(AB\\). Then \\(CP<CT\\), while\n\\[\nCT^2=CM^2+MT^2\n\\leq\\frac{3s^2}{4}+\\frac{s^2}{4}=s^2.\n\\]\nHere \\(CM=\\sqrt3s/2\\) is the equilateral altitude and \\(MT\\leq s/2\\) because \\(T\\) lies on \\(AB\\). Thus \\(s>CP=10\\), which excludes \\(s^2=100-48\\sqrt3\\).\n\nConsequently the area is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt3s^2}{4}\n=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}(100+48\\sqrt3)\n=36+25\\sqrt3\\approx79.30.\n\\]\nThe nearest integer is \\(\\boxed{79}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
