"question": "A straight line passing through the point \\((0,4)\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(x-3y-7=0.\\) Its equation is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(y+3x-4=0\\)",
"B": "\\(y+3x+4=0\\)",
"C": "\\(y-3x-4=0\\)",
"D": "\\(3y+x-12=0\\)",
"E": "\\(3y-x-12=0\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "First rewrite the given equation to reveal its slope:\n\\[\ny=\\frac13x-\\frac73.\n\\]\nIts slope is finite and nonzero. A perpendicular line therefore has slope \\(m\\) satisfying \\(\\frac13m=-1\\), so \\(m=-3\\). Using the point \\((0,4)\\), the point-slope equation is\n\\[\ny-4=-3(x-0).\n\\]\nRearranging gives \\(y+3x-4=0\\), choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
