"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A positive number is mistakenly divided by \\(6\\) instead of being multiplied by \\(6.\\) Based on the correct answer, the error thus committed, to the nearest percent, is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(100\\)",
"B": "\\(97\\)",
"C": "\\(83\\)",
"D": "\\(17\\)",
"E": "\\(3\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "The phrase “based on the correct answer” tells us to divide the error by the correct result. Let the original number be \\(a>0\\). The correct result is \\(6a\\), and the mistaken result is \\(a/6\\). Since the mistaken result is smaller, the error is \\(6a-a/6\\).\n\nIts percentage of the correct result is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6a-a/6}{6a}\\cdot100\\%\n&=\\frac{35}{36}\\cdot100\\%\\\\\n&\\approx97.22\\%.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe denominator is nonzero because \\(a>0\\). Rounding to the nearest whole percent gives \\(97\\%\\), choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
