"year": 1968,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If an arc of \\(60^\\circ\\) on circle \\(\\mathrm{I}\\) has the same length as an arc of \\(45^\\circ\\) on circle \\(\\mathrm{II},\\) the ratio of the area of circle \\(\\mathrm{I}\\) to that of circle \\(\\mathrm{II}\\) is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(16:9\\)",
"B": "\\(9:16\\)",
"C": "\\(4:3\\)",
"D": "\\(3:4\\)",
"E": "none of these"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "An arc occupies the same fraction of its circle's circumference as its central angle occupies of \\(360^\\circ\\). Let the positive radii of circles I and II be \\(r_1\\) and \\(r_2\\). Equal arc lengths give\n\\[\n\\frac{60}{360}(2\\pi r_1)=\\frac{45}{360}(2\\pi r_2),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\frac{r_1}{r_2}=\\frac{45}{60}=\\frac34.\n\\]\nA circle's area is \\(\\pi r^2\\), so the area ratio is the square of the radius ratio:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi r_1^2}{\\pi r_2^2}=\\left(\\frac34\\right)^2=\\frac9{16}.\n\\]\nThus the requested ratio is \\(\\boxed{9:16}\\), choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1968/solutions",
