"year": 1968,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 12,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A circle passes through the vertices of a triangle with side-lengths \\(7\\dfrac12,\\) \\(10,\\) \\(12\\dfrac12.\\) The radius of the circle is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{15}{4}\\)",
"B": "\\(5\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{25}{4}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{35}{4}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{15\\sqrt2}{2}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "The side lengths are proportional to \\(3,4,5\\), suggesting a right triangle. Indeed,\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)^2+10^2\n=\\frac{225+400}{4}\n=\\frac{625}{4}\n=\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nBy the converse of the Pythagorean theorem, the triangle is right, with hypotenuse \\(25/2\\).\n\nThe midpoint of a right triangle's hypotenuse is equally distant from all three vertices, with each distance equal to half the hypotenuse. It is therefore the center of the circle through the vertices. The radius is\n\\[\nR=\\frac12\\cdot\\frac{25}{2}=\\boxed{\\frac{25}{4}},\n\\]\nchoice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
