"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(P\\) be the product of any three consecutive positive odd integers. The largest integer dividing all such \\(P\\) is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(15\\)",
"B": "\\(6\\)",
"C": "\\(5\\)",
"D": "\\(3\\)",
"E": "\\(1\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "We need a divisor that always works and a reason no larger common divisor can work. Write the three consecutive positive odd integers as \\(a,a+2,a+4\\). When \\(a\\) is divided by \\(3\\), its remainder is \\(0\\), \\(1\\), or \\(2\\). In those three cases, respectively, \\(a\\), \\(a+2\\), or \\(a+4\\) is divisible by \\(3\\). Thus every allowed product is divisible by \\(3\\).\n\nTo bound any universal positive divisor \\(d\\), use two allowed products:\n\\[\n1\\cdot3\\cdot5=15,\\qquad 7\\cdot9\\cdot11=693.\n\\]\nSince \\(d\\) divides both, it also divides\n\\[\n693-46\\cdot15=3.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(d\\le3\\). Since \\(3\\) always works, the largest integer dividing every such product is \\(\\boxed{3}\\), choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1968/solutions",
