"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If the points \\((1,y_1)\\) and \\((-1,y_2)\\) lie on the graph of \\(y=ax^2+bx+c,\\) and \\(y_1-y_2=-6,\\) then \\(b\\) equals:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(-3\\)",
"B": "\\(0\\)",
"C": "\\(3\\)",
"D": "\\(\\sqrt{ac}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{a+c}{2}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "At \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=-1\\), the squared terms agree. Subtracting the corresponding function values will therefore cancel the terms involving \\(a\\) and \\(c\\). Substitution gives\n\\[\ny_1=a+b+c,\\qquad y_2=a-b+c.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n-6=y_1-y_2\n=(a+b+c)-(a-b+c)=2b.\n\\]\nDividing by \\(2\\) yields \\(b=-3\\), choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
