"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Inside square \\(ABCD\\) with side \\(s,\\) quarter-circle arcs with radii \\(s\\) and centers at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are drawn. These arcs intersect at a point \\(X\\) inside the square. How far is \\(X\\) from side \\(CD?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\tfrac12s(\\sqrt3+4)\\)",
"B": "\\(\\tfrac12s\\sqrt3\\)",
"C": "\\(\\tfrac12s(1+\\sqrt3)\\)",
"D": "\\(\\tfrac12s(\\sqrt3-1)\\)",
"E": "\\(\\tfrac12s(2-\\sqrt3)\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Because \\(X\\) lies on both arcs, the radii give \\(AX=BX=s\\). Thus triangle \\(ABX\\) is isosceles with base \\(AB=s\\), which lets us split it into two right triangles.\n\nLet \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(AB\\). In an isosceles triangle, the median from the vertex is also an altitude, so \\(XM\\perp AB\\) and \\(AM=s/2\\). The Pythagorean theorem in triangle \\(AMX\\) gives\n\\[\nXM^2=s^2-\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2\n=\\frac{3s^2}{4}.\n\\]\nSince \\(s>0\\) and \\(XM\\) is a length, \\(XM=s\\sqrt3/2\\).\n\nExtend the ray \\(MX\\) to meet \\(CD\\) at \\(Y\\). Opposite sides of the square are parallel, so \\(MY\\perp CD\\), and the separation of \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) is \\(MY=s\\). Since \\(X\\) is inside the square, it lies between \\(M\\) and \\(Y\\). Therefore its distance from \\(CD\\) is\n\\[\nXY=MY-MX\n=s-\\frac{s\\sqrt3}{2}\n=\\frac{s}{2}(2-\\sqrt3).\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{s}{2}(2-\\sqrt3)}\\), choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
