"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 2,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If \\(b\\) men take \\(c\\) days to lay \\(f\\) bricks, then the number of days it will take \\(c\\) men working at the same rate to lay \\(b\\) bricks is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(fb^2\\)",
"B": "\\(\\frac{b}{f^2}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\frac{f^2}{b}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\frac{b^2}{f}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\frac{f}{b^2}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Finding one man's daily rate lets us use the same productivity for the new crew. Since \\(b\\) men lay \\(f\\) bricks in \\(c\\) days, one man lays\n\\[\n\\frac{f}{bc}\n\\]\nbricks per day. Therefore \\(c\\) men lay\n\\[\nc\\cdot\\frac{f}{bc}=\\frac{f}{b}\n\\]\nbricks per day.\n\nLet \\(d\\) be the number of days this crew needs to lay \\(b\\) bricks. Work equals daily rate times time, so\n\\[\n\\frac{f}{b}d=b,\n\\qquad\n d=\\frac{b}{f/b}=\\frac{b^2}{f}.\n\\]\nThe quantities \\(b,c,f\\) are positive in this work situation, so all divisions are valid. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{b^2}{f}}\\) days, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
