"year": 1972,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 1,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The lengths in inches of the three sides of each of four triangles \\(\\mathrm{I},\\) \\(\\mathrm{II},\\) \\(\\mathrm{III},\\) and \\(\\mathrm{IV}\\) are as follows: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\mathrm{I}\\quad&3,4,5\\\\ \\mathrm{II}\\quad&4,7\\frac12,8\\frac12\\\\ \\mathrm{III}\\quad&7,24,25\\\\ \\mathrm{IV}\\quad&3\\frac12,4\\frac12,5\\frac12 \\end{aligned} \\] Of these four given triangles, the only right triangles are:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\mathrm{I}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{II}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\mathrm{I}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{III}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\mathrm{I}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{IV}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\mathrm{I},\\) \\(\\mathrm{II},\\) and\n\\(\\mathrm{III}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\mathrm{I},\\) \\(\\mathrm{II},\\) and \\(\\mathrm{IV}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "For a triangle whose longest side is \\(c\\), the Pythagorean theorem and its converse say that it is right exactly when the other two sides satisfy \\(a^2+b^2=c^2\\). Testing each triangle gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{I}:\\quad&3^2+4^2=25=5^2,\\\\\n\\mathrm{II}:\\quad&4^2+7.5^2=72.25=8.5^2,\\\\\n\\mathrm{III}:\\quad&7^2+24^2=625=25^2,\\\\\n\\mathrm{IV}:\\quad&3.5^2+4.5^2=32.5\\ne30.25=5.5^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus exactly triangles \\(\\mathrm{I}\\), \\(\\mathrm{II}\\), and \\(\\mathrm{III}\\) are right triangles, choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
