"number": 33,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The minimum value of the quotient of a (base ten) number of three different nonzero digits divided by the sum of its digits is:",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(9.7\\)",
"B": "\\(10.1\\)",
"C": "\\(10.5\\)",
"D": "\\(10.9\\)",
"E": "\\(20.5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Method 1: Method 1: Prove a sharp lower bound\n\nA small hundreds digit and a large units digit are promising because place value makes the hundreds digit much more costly in the numerator. The allowed number \\(189\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{189}{1+8+9}=\\frac{189}{18}=10.5.\n\\]\nWe will prove that no allowed number has a smaller quotient.\n\nLet \\(a,b,c\\) be the hundreds, tens, and units digits. They are distinct integers from \\(1\\) to \\(9\\). Write\n\\[\nN=100a+10b+c,\\qquad S=a+b+c>0.\n\\]\nTo compare \\(N/S\\) with \\(10.5=21/2\\), clear the positive denominator:\n\\[\n2N-21S=179a-b-19c.\n\\]\nWe have \\(a\\ge1\\). Also, \\(b+19c\\le179\\): if \\(c=9\\), distinctness gives \\(b\\le8\\), so \\(b+19c\\le8+171=179\\). If \\(c\\le8\\), then \\(b+19c\\le9+152=161\\).\n\nIt follows that\n\\[\n2N-21S\\ge179-179=0,\n\\qquad \\frac NS\\ge\\frac{21}{2}.\n\\]\nThe digits of \\(189\\) are distinct and nonzero, and its quotient attains this bound. Thus the minimum is \\(\\boxed{10.5}\\), choice \\(\\text{C}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Method 2: Improve the digits one at a time\n\nLet the distinct nonzero digits be \\(a,b,c\\), and define\n\\[\nR(a,b,c)=\\frac{100a+10b+c}{a+b+c}.\n\\]\nFor any fixed three digits, rearranging them leaves the denominator unchanged. The numerator is smallest when the digits increase from left to right: swapping a larger digit in a higher place with a smaller digit in a lower place decreases the number by the positive difference in place values times the positive difference in digits. Thus a minimum must have \\(a<b<c\\).\n\nIf \\(a>1\\), replacing it by \\(1\\) keeps the digits distinct. Comparing the two quotients gives\n\\[\nR(a,b,c)-R(1,b,c)\n=\\frac{(a-1)(90b+99c)}{(a+b+c)(1+b+c)}>0.\n\\]\nAll factors in the denominator are positive. Therefore the minimum has \\(a=1\\).\n\nWith this digit fixed, rewrite the quotient as\n\\[\nR(1,b,c)=1+\\frac{99+9b}{1+b+c}.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(b\\), the numerator of the fraction is positive, so increasing \\(c\\) decreases the quotient. Because \\(1<b<c\\le9\\), choosing \\(c=9\\) is allowed and is best.\n\nNow \\(b\\) ranges from \\(2\\) to \\(8\\), and\n\\[\nR(1,b,9)=\\frac{109+10b}{b+10}\n=10+\\frac9{b+10}.\n\\]\nThis is smallest when \\(b=8\\), the largest allowed value. Hence \\(189\\) gives the minimum\n\\[\n10+\\frac9{18}=\\boxed{10.5},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
