"year": 1973,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If \\(n\\) is a real number, then the simultaneous system \\[ \\begin{cases} nx+y=1,\\\\ ny+z=1,\\\\ x+nz=1 \\end{cases} \\] has no solution if and only if \\(n\\) is equal to",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(-1\\)",
"B": "\\(0\\)",
"C": "\\(1\\)",
"D": "\\(0\\) or \\(1\\)",
"E": "\\(\\frac12\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The equations have the same cyclic pattern, so try equal values \\(x=y=z=t\\). Each equation then becomes\n\\[\n(n+1)t=1.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(n\\ne-1\\), the denominator \\(n+1\\) is nonzero, and choosing\n\\[\nx=y=z=\\frac1{n+1}\n\\]\nmakes every left side equal to \\((n+1)/(n+1)=1\\). Thus the system has a solution for every such \\(n\\).\n\nIt remains to test \\(n=-1\\). Adding the three original equations gives\n\\[\n(n+1)(x+y+z)=3.\n\\]\nWhen \\(n=-1\\), this says \\(0=3\\), which is impossible. Therefore the system has no solution if and only if \\(\\boxed{n=-1}\\), choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
