"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 12,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The average (arithmetic mean) age of a group consisting of doctors and lawyers is \\(40.\\) If the doctors average \\(35\\) and the lawyers \\(50\\) years old, then the ratio of the number of doctors to the number of lawyers is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(3:2\\)",
"B": "\\(3:1\\)",
"C": "\\(2:3\\)",
"D": "\\(2:1\\)",
"E": "\\(1:2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The group averages must be weighted by the numbers of people in the groups. Let \\(d>0\\) be the number of doctors and \\(\\ell>0\\) the number of lawyers.\n\nAn average multiplied by the number of people gives their total age. Thus the doctors' total age is \\(35d\\), the lawyers' total age is \\(50\\ell\\), and the combined average gives\n\\[\n35d+50\\ell=40(d+\\ell).\n\\]\nExpanding and collecting terms,\n\\[\n35d+50\\ell=40d+40\\ell,\n\\qquad 10\\ell=5d,\n\\qquad d=2\\ell.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\ell>0\\), the ratio of doctors to lawyers is \\(\\boxed{2:1}\\), choice D.",
