"number": 30,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\([t]\\) denote the greatest integer not exceeding \\(t,\\) where \\(t\\ge0,\\) and \\[ \\begin{aligned} S=\\{(x,y):{}&(x-T)^2+y^2\\\\ &\\le T^2\\},\\\\ T&=t-[t]. \\end{aligned} \\] Then we have",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "the point \\((0,0)\\) does not belong to \\(S\\) for any \\(t\\)",
"B": "\\(0\\le\\operatorname{Area} S\\le\\pi\\) for all\n\\(t\\)",
"C": "\\(S\\) is contained in the first quadrant for all \\(t\\ge5\\)",
"D": "the center of \\(S\\) for any \\(t\\) is on the line \\(y=x\\)",
"E": "none of the other statements is true"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "The definition of the greatest integer gives\n\\[\n[t]\\le t<[t]+1,\n\\]\nso \\(T=t-[t]\\) satisfies \\(0\\le T<1\\). The inequality \\((x-T)^2+y^2\\le T^2\\) describes the closed disk with center \\((T,0)\\) and radius \\(T\\). If \\(T=0\\), it consists only of the origin and has area zero. Otherwise, its area is \\(\\pi T^2\\). Therefore, in all cases,\n\\[\n0\\le \\operatorname{Area}(S)=\\pi T^2<\\pi.\n\\]\nThis proves statement B, whose upper bound allows equality even though equality is never attained.\n\nThe origin satisfies the defining inequality for every \\(t\\), so A is false. For \\(t=11/2\\), the center is \\((1/2,0)\\), which is not on \\(y=x\\), and the disk contains \\((1/2,-1/2)\\), which is outside the first quadrant. Thus C and D are also false. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1973/solutions",
