"batch": "NA",
"number": 3,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The coefficient of \\(x^7\\) in the polynomial expansion of \\[ (1+2x-x^2)^4 \\] is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(-8\\)",
"B": "\\(12\\)",
"C": "\\(6\\)",
"D": "\\(-12\\)",
"E": "none of these"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "View the expression as a product of four copies of \\(1+2x-x^2\\). Each contribution to the expansion comes from choosing one term from each copy.\n\nChoosing \\(-x^2\\) from every copy gives degree \\(8\\). To obtain degree \\(7\\), the total degree must decrease by exactly \\(1\\). Thus exactly one copy must supply \\(2x\\), and the other three must supply \\(-x^2\\). Choosing a constant would decrease the degree by at least \\(2\\), so there are no other possibilities.\n\nThere are four choices for the copy supplying \\(2x\\), and each contributes\n\\[\n(2x)(-x^2)^3=-2x^7.\n\\]\nThese four distinct selections include every degree-seven contribution. Therefore the coefficient is \\(4(-2)=\\boxed{-8}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
