"year": 1974,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 9,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The integers greater than one are arranged in five columns as follows:\n\n\\[\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5\\\\ \\hline 9 & 8 & 7 & 6 & \\\\ \\hline & 10 & 11 & 12 & 13\\\\ \\hline 17 & 16 & 15 & 14 & \\\\ \\hline & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots\\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\\]\n\n(Four consecutive integers appear in each row; in the first, third and other odd numbered rows, the integers appear in the last four columns and increase from left to right; in the second, fourth and other even numbered rows, the integers appear in the first four columns and increase from right to left.)\n\nIn which column will the number \\(1{,}000\\) fall?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "first",
"B": "second",
"C": "third",
"D": "fourth",
"E": "fifth"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Group the rows in odd-even pairs. Each pair contains eight consecutive integers, and the stated placement rule is the same for every pair.\n\nIn increasing numerical order, the first eight integers \\(2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\) occupy columns\n\\[\n2,3,4,5,4,3,2,1,\n\\]\nrespectively. This eight-column pattern repeats, so adding any whole number of blocks of \\(8\\) leaves a number in the same column.\n\nBecause\n\\[\n1{,}000-8=992=124\\cdot8,\n\\]\n\\(1{,}000\\) occupies the same column as \\(8\\). The displayed arrangement places \\(8\\) in the second column. Thus the answer is the second column, choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1974/solutions",
