"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 18,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A positive integer \\(N\\) with three digits in its base ten representation is chosen at random, with each three-digit number having an equal chance of being chosen. The probability that \\(\\log_2N\\) is an integer is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(\\frac3{899}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\frac1{225}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\frac1{300}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\frac1{450}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "By the definition of a logarithm, \\(\\log_2N\\) is an integer exactly when \\(N=2^k\\) for some integer \\(k\\). The neighboring powers\n\\[\n2^6=64<100,\\qquad 2^{10}=1024>999\n\\]\nshow where the three-digit powers begin and end. Since powers of two increase as the integer exponent increases, the complete list is\n\\[\n2^7=128,\\qquad 2^8=256,\\qquad 2^9=512.\n\\]\nThese are three distinct favorable numbers. There are \\(999-100+1=900\\) equally likely three-digit integers, so the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{900}=\\boxed{\\frac{1}{300}},\n\\]\nchoice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
