"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 2,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For how many real numbers \\(x\\) is \\(\\sqrt{-(x+1)^2}\\) a real number?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "none",
"B": "one",
"C": "two",
"D": "a finite number greater than two",
"E": "infinitely many"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "A square root is real exactly when its radicand is nonnegative. For real \\(x\\), the square \\((x+1)^2\\) is nonnegative, so \\(-(x+1)^2\\) is nonpositive. It can meet the square-root condition only by being zero:\n\\[\n-(x+1)^2=0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nx+1=0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nx=-1.\n\\]\nAt \\(x=-1\\), the expression equals \\(\\sqrt0=0\\), which is real. Every other real \\(x\\) gives a negative radicand. Thus there is exactly \\(\\boxed{1}\\) such number, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
