"number": 5,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many integers greater than ten and less than one hundred, written in base ten notation, are increased by nine when their digits are reversed?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(8\\)",
"D": "\\(9\\)",
"E": "\\(10\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Place value lets us calculate exactly how reversing the digits changes the number. Let the tens digit be \\(a\\) and the ones digit be \\(b\\). Every allowed number has the form \\(10a+b\\), where \\(1\\le a\\le9\\) and \\(0\\le b\\le9\\). Its reversal has value \\(10b+a\\).\n\nThe required increase is nine, so\n\\[\n(10b+a)-(10a+b)=9.\n\\]\nCombining terms gives \\(9(b-a)=9\\), hence \\(b=a+1\\). Since \\(b\\le9\\), the possible tens digits are exactly \\(a=1,2,\\ldots,8\\).\n\nThese give \\(12,23,34,45,56,67,78,89\\). Each lies strictly between 10 and 100 and satisfies the reversal condition. Every possible digit pair was included, and different tens digits give different integers. Therefore there are \\(8\\) integers, choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
