"year": 1978,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Opposite sides of a regular hexagon are \\(12\\) inches apart. The length of each side, in inches, is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(7.5\\)",
"B": "\\(6\\sqrt2\\)",
"C": "\\(5\\sqrt2\\)",
"D": "\\(\\frac92\\sqrt3\\)",
"E": "\\(4\\sqrt3\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Joining the center of a regular hexagon to its vertices divides it into six congruent isosceles triangles. Each central angle is \\(360^\\circ/6=60^\\circ\\), so these triangles are equilateral.\n\nLet \\(s>0\\) be the side length, let \\(O\\) be the center, and let \\(A,B\\) be adjacent vertices. Then \\(OA=AB=s\\). If \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\), the equilateral triangle's median \\(OM\\) is perpendicular to \\(AB\\), and \\(AM=s/2\\).\n\nThe center is equally far from the two opposite parallel sides, so their separation of \\(12\\) inches gives \\(OM=6\\). Applying the Pythagorean theorem in right triangle \\(OMA\\),\n\\[\n6^2+\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2=s^2.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\tfrac34s^2=36\\), so \\(s^2=48\\). Taking the positive root because \\(s\\) is a length gives \\(s=4\\sqrt3\\) inches, choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
