"number": 22,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The following four statements, and only these, are found on a card:\n\nOn this card exactly one statement is false.\n On this card exactly two statements are false.\n On this card exactly three statements are false.\n On this card exactly four statements are false.\n\n(Assume each statement on the card is either true or false.) Among them the number of false statements is exactly",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\)",
"D": "\\(3\\)",
"E": "\\(4\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The four statements claim four different exact numbers of false statements. Two different counts cannot both be correct, so at most one statement is true.\n\nCould all four statements be false? Then there would be exactly four false statements, making the fourth statement true. This contradiction rules out that possibility.\n\nTherefore exactly one statement is true, leaving exactly three false statements. This is consistent: the third statement is true, while the claims of one, two, and four false statements are false. Thus the answer is \\(3\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1978/solutions",
