"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The square of an integer is called a perfect square. If \\(x\\) is a perfect square, the next larger perfect square is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(x+1\\)",
"B": "\\(x^2+1\\)",
"C": "\\(x^2+2x+1\\)",
"D": "\\(x^2+x\\)",
"E": "\\(x+2\\sqrt x+1\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Let \\(m=\\sqrt{x}\\). Because \\(x\\) is a perfect square, \\(m\\) is a nonnegative integer and \\(x=m^2\\).\n\nEvery integer square is also the square of a nonnegative integer, since opposite integers have the same square. Squares increase as their nonnegative integer roots increase, so the next larger perfect square after \\(m^2\\) is \\((m+1)^2\\). Expanding gives\n\\[\n(m+1)^2=m^2+2m+1\n=\\boxed{x+2\\sqrt{x}+1}.\n\\]\nThis also covers \\(x=0\\), whose next larger perfect square is \\(1\\). The answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1979/solutions",
