"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Find a positive integral solution to the equation \\[ \\frac{\\substack{1+3+5+\\cdots\\\\{}+(2n-1)}} {\\substack{2+4+6+\\cdots\\\\{}+2n}}=\\frac{115}{116}. \\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(110\\)",
"B": "\\(115\\)",
"C": "\\(116\\)",
"D": "\\(231\\)",
"E": "The equation has no positive integral solutions."
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Each sum has \\(n\\) equally spaced terms, so its sum is the number of terms times the average of the first and last terms. Therefore\n\\[\n1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)\n=\\frac{n(1+2n-1)}2=n^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n2+4+\\cdots+2n\n=\\frac{n(2+2n)}2=n(n+1).\n\\]\nSince \\(n\\) is positive, the denominator is nonzero and we may cancel \\(n\\). The equation becomes\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{n+1}=\\frac{115}{116}.\n\\]\nMultiplying by the nonzero denominators gives\n\\[\n116n=115(n+1)=115n+115,\n\\]\nso \\(n=115\\). This is a positive integer and makes the original ratio \\(115^2/(115\\cdot116)=115/116\\). The answer is \\(115\\), choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
