"year": 1979,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 16,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A circle with area \\(A_1\\) is contained in the interior of a larger circle with area \\(A_1+A_2.\\) If the radius of the larger circle is \\(3,\\) and if \\(A_1,\\) \\(A_2,\\) \\(A_1+A_2\\) is an arithmetic progression, then the radius of the smaller circle is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(\\frac2{\\sqrt3}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\frac32\\)",
"E": "\\(\\sqrt3\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "In a three-term arithmetic progression, equal consecutive differences mean that twice the middle term equals the sum of the outer terms. Applying this to the areas in their stated order gives\n\\[\n2A_2=A_1+(A_1+A_2),\n\\]\nso \\(A_2=2A_1\\). Consequently, the larger circle has area \\(A_1+A_2=3A_1\\).\n\nIts radius is \\(3\\), so\n\\[\n3A_1=\\pi(3)^2=9\\pi,\n\\qquad A_1=3\\pi.\n\\]\nLet \\(r>0\\) be the smaller circle's radius. Its area satisfies \\(\\pi r^2=3\\pi\\), giving \\(r^2=3\\). The positive radius is therefore \\(r=\\sqrt3\\), which is less than \\(3\\) as required. The answer is \\(\\sqrt3\\), choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1979/solutions",
