"year": 1981,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For all positive numbers \\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) \\(z,\\) the product \\[ \\begin{aligned} &(x+y+z)^{-1}\\\\ &\\quad\\cdot(x^{-1}+y^{-1}+z^{-1})\\\\ &\\quad\\cdot(xy+yz+zx)^{-1}\\\\ &\\quad\\cdot\\big((xy)^{-1}+(yz)^{-1}+(zx)^{-1}\\big) \\end{aligned} \\] equals",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(x^{-2}y^{-2}z^{-2}\\)",
"B": "\\(x^{-2}+y^{-2}+z^{-2}\\)",
"C": "\\((x+y+z)^{-1}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\frac1{xyz}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\frac1{xy+yz+zx}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Use \\(a^{-1}=1/a\\) and give each reciprocal sum the common denominator \\(xyz\\). This exposes the sums appearing in the other factors:\n\\[\n\\frac1x+\\frac1y+\\frac1z\n=\\frac{xy+yz+zx}{xyz},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac1{xy}+\\frac1{yz}+\\frac1{zx}\n=\\frac{x+y+z}{xyz}.\n\\]\nIn the original product, these numerators cancel \\(xy+yz+zx\\) and \\(x+y+z\\) from the other two factors. Positivity of \\(x,y,z\\) ensures that every denominator and canceled factor is nonzero. What remains is\n\\[\n\\frac1{(xyz)^2}\n=\\frac1{x^2y^2z^2}\n=x^{-2}y^{-2}z^{-2}.\n\\]\nTherefore the answer is \\(x^{-2}y^{-2}z^{-2}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1981/solutions",
