"question": "The base three representation of \\(x\\) is \\[ 12112211122211112222. \\] The first digit (on the left) of the base nine representation of \\(x\\) is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(3\\)",
"D": "\\(4\\)",
"E": "\\(5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Because \\(9=3^2\\), moving one place in base nine has the same effect as moving two places in base three. We can therefore convert by grouping base-three digits in pairs from the right:\n\\[\n12\\mid11\\mid22\\mid11\\mid12\\mid22\\mid11\\mid11\\mid22\\mid22.\n\\]\nEach pair has a value from \\(0\\) through \\(8\\), so it becomes one valid base-nine digit. There are \\(20\\) base-three digits, and the leading pair is \\(12\\). Its value is\n\\[\n1\\cdot3+2=5.\n\\]\nThus the first base-nine digit is \\(5\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
