"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(\\lfloor z\\rfloor\\) denote the greatest integer not exceeding \\(z.\\) Let \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) satisfy the simultaneous equations \\[ \\begin{aligned} y&=2\\lfloor x\\rfloor+3,\\\\ y&=3\\lfloor x-2\\rfloor+5. \\end{aligned} \\] If \\(x\\) is not an integer, then \\(x+y\\) is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "an integer",
"B": "between \\(4\\) and \\(5\\)",
"C": "between \\(-4\\) and \\(4\\)",
"D": "between \\(15\\) and \\(16\\)",
"E": "\\(16.5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Subtracting an integer shifts the floor by that same integer. To see this here, let \\(n=\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\). Then\n\\[\nn\\le x<n+1,\n\\]\nso subtracting \\(2\\) gives \\(n-2\\le x-2<n-1\\). Hence \\(\\lfloor x-2\\rfloor=n-2\\).\n\nThe two expressions for \\(y\\) must agree, giving\n\\[\n2n+3=3(n-2)+5=3n-1.\n\\]\nThus \\(n=4\\), and \\(y=2(4)+3=11\\). Since \\(\\lfloor x\\rfloor=4\\), we have \\(4\\le x<5\\). The condition that \\(x\\) is not an integer removes the endpoint \\(x=4\\), leaving \\(4<x<5\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\boxed{15<x+y<16}.\n\\]\nConversely, every \\(x\\) in \\((4,5)\\), together with \\(y=11\\), satisfies both original equations, so this is the full range. The answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1982/solutions",
