"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-1}.\\) Then for \\(x^2\\ne1,\\) \\(f(-x)\\) is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\frac1{f(x)}\\)",
"B": "\\(-f(x)\\)",
"C": "\\(\\frac1{f(-x)}\\)",
"D": "\\(-f(-x)\\)",
"E": "\\(f(x)\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The condition \\(x^2\\ne1\\) means \\(x\\ne1\\) and \\(x\\ne-1\\). Consequently, both \\(f(x)\\) and \\(f(-x)\\) are defined, and \\(f(x)\\ne0\\). Replace every occurrence of the input by \\(-x\\), then multiply the numerator and denominator by \\(-1\\):\n\\[\nf(-x)=\\frac{-x+1}{-x-1}=\\frac{x-1}{x+1}.\n\\]\nThis fraction is the reciprocal of \\(f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-1}\\). Therefore\n\\[\nf(-x)=\\boxed{\\frac1{f(x)}},\n\\]\nchoice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
