"year": 1984,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 16,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The function \\(f(x)\\) satisfies \\(f(2+x)=f(2-x)\\) for all real numbers \\(x.\\) If the equation \\(f(x)=0\\) has exactly four distinct real roots, then the sum of these roots is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(6\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The identity compares inputs equally far from \\(2\\), suggesting that roots occur at reflected positions. If \\(r\\) is a root, substitute \\(x=r-2\\) into the identity to get\n\\[\nf(r)=f(4-r)=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(4-r\\) is also a root.\n\nWrite all four distinct roots in increasing order as \\(r_1<r_2<r_3<r_4\\). Their four reflected values are distinct roots too, so they form the same complete list in reverse order. Consequently\n\\[\n4-r_1=r_4,\n\\qquad\n4-r_2=r_3.\n\\]\nEach resulting pair sums to \\(4\\). Hence the sum of all four roots is\n\\[\n(r_1+r_4)+(r_2+r_3)=4+4=\\boxed{8},\n\\]\nchoice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
