"year": 1985,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A large bag of coins contains pennies, dimes and quarters. There are twice as many dimes as pennies and three times as many quarters as dimes. An amount of money which could be in the bag is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\$306\\)",
"B": "\\(\\$333\\)",
"C": "\\(\\$342\\)",
"D": "\\(\\$348\\)",
"E": "\\(\\$360\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Let \\(p\\) be the positive integer number of pennies. There are then \\(2p\\) dimes and \\(3(2p)=6p\\) quarters. Working in cents, their total value is\n\\[\np+10(2p)+25(6p)=171p.\n\\]\nEvery offered amount is a whole number of dollars, so \\(171p\\) must be divisible by \\(100\\). Since\n\\[\n171=3^2\\cdot19,\n\\qquad 100=2^2\\cdot5^2,\n\\]\n\\(p\\) must supply both factors of \\(2\\) and both factors of \\(5\\). Thus \\(p=100k\\) for a positive integer \\(k\\), making the total \\(171k\\) dollars.\n\nThe only multiple of \\(171\\) between \\(306\\) and \\(360\\) is \\(2\\cdot171=342\\). This amount is achieved with \\(200\\) pennies, \\(400\\) dimes, and \\(1200\\) quarters. The answer is \\(\\$342\\), choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1985/solutions",
