"year": 1985,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 5,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Which terms must be removed from the sum \\[ \\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac16+\\frac18+\\frac1{10}+\\frac1{12} \\] if the sum of the remaining terms is to equal \\(1?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\frac14\\) and \\(\\frac18\\)",
"B": "\\(\\frac14\\) and \\(\\frac1{12}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\frac18\\) and \\(\\frac1{12}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\frac16\\) and \\(\\frac1{10}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\frac18\\) and \\(\\frac1{10}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Using the common denominator \\(120\\) turns the question into matching integer numerators. The original sum is\n\\[\n\\frac{60+30+20+15+12+10}{120}\n=\\frac{147}{120}.\n\\]\nTo leave \\(1=120/120\\), we must remove \\(27/120\\). The only pair of these numerators adding to \\(27\\) is \\(15\\) and \\(12\\): \\(60\\) and \\(30\\) are too large, \\(20\\) would require an absent \\(7\\), and among \\(15,12,10\\), only \\(15+12\\) works.\n\nThese numerators correspond to \\(15/120=1/8\\) and \\(12/120=1/10\\). Remove \\(\\frac18\\) and \\(\\frac1{10}\\), choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1985/solutions",
