"year": 1985,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 25,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The volume of a certain rectangular solid is \\(8\\text{ cm}^3,\\) its total surface area is \\(32\\text{ cm}^2,\\) and its three dimensions are in geometric progression. The sum of the lengths in cm of all the edges of this solid is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(28\\)",
"B": "\\(32\\)",
"C": "\\(36\\)",
"D": "\\(40\\)",
"E": "\\(44\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Label the positive dimensions, in their geometric-progression order, \\(u,v,w\\), measured in centimeters. Equal successive ratios give \\(v^2=uw\\). This makes the volume especially useful:\n\\[\n8=uvw=v(uw)=v^3,\n\\]\nso \\(v=2\\).\n\nThe surface-area condition gives\n\\[\nuv+uw+vw=\\frac{32}{2}=16.\n\\]\nUsing \\(uw=v^2\\), we can turn this into the sum of the dimensions without finding them individually:\n\\[\n16=uv+v^2+vw=v(u+v+w).\n\\]\nSince \\(v=2\\), it follows that \\(u+v+w=8\\).\n\nA rectangular solid has four edges of each dimension. Their total length is therefore \\(4(u+v+w)=\\boxed{32\\text{ cm}}\\), choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
