"year": 1985,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 22,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Assume every \\(7\\)-digit whole number is a possible telephone number except those that begin with \\(0\\) or \\(1.\\) What fraction of telephone numbers begin with \\(9\\) and end with \\(0?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{63}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{80}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{81}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{90}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{100}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Count all permitted telephone numbers and then those with the required first and last digits. The first digit has \\(8\\) choices, from \\(2\\) through \\(9\\), and each of the remaining six digits has \\(10\\) choices. Therefore there are\n\\[\n8\\cdot 10^6=8{,}000{,}000\n\\]\npermitted numbers.\n\nFor a number beginning with \\(9\\) and ending with \\(0\\), those two digits are fixed. The five middle digits are unrestricted, giving \\(10^5=100{,}000\\) such numbers. Each ordered selection of digits determines exactly one number, so these counts include every eligible number once.\n\nThe requested fraction is\n\\[\n\\frac{100{,}000}{8{,}000{,}000}=\\frac{1}{80}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
