"year": 1986,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(S\\) be the statement\n\n“If the sum of the digits of the whole number \\(n\\) is divisible by \\(6,\\) then \\(n\\) is divisible by \\(6.\\)”\n\nA value of \\(n\\) which shows \\(S\\) to be false is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(30\\)",
"B": "\\(33\\)",
"C": "\\(40\\)",
"D": "\\(42\\)",
"E": "none of these"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "An if-then statement is false when its condition holds but its conclusion fails. Here we need a number whose digit sum is divisible by \\(6\\), while the number itself is not.\n\nFor \\(n=33\\), the digit sum is \\(3+3=6\\), which is divisible by \\(6\\). However, \\(33\\) is odd, and every multiple of \\(6\\) is even. Thus \\(33\\) is not divisible by \\(6\\), making it a counterexample.\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{33}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
