"year": 1986,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The \\(120\\) permutations of AHSME are arranged in dictionary order, as if each were an ordinary five-letter word. The last letter of the \\(86\\)th word in this list is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(A\\)",
"B": "\\(H\\)",
"C": "\\(S\\)",
"D": "\\(M\\)",
"E": "\\(E\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The letters in alphabetical order are \\(A,E,H,M,S\\). In dictionary order, all words with the same prefix occur consecutively, so we can locate the desired word by counting prefix blocks.\n\nFor each fixed first letter, the remaining letters can be arranged in \\(4\\cdot3\\cdot2\\cdot1=24\\) ways. Each arrangement is counted once by its successive letter choices. The blocks beginning with \\(A,E,H\\) therefore occupy positions \\(1\\) through \\(72\\). Position \\(86\\) is the \\(86-72=14\\)th word beginning with \\(M\\).\n\nWithin the \\(M\\) block, the second letters occur in the order \\(A,E,H,S\\). Each second-letter block contains \\(3\\cdot2\\cdot1=6\\) words. The \\(MA\\) and \\(ME\\) blocks account for the first \\(12\\), so our word is the second word beginning with \\(MH\\).\n\nThe remaining letters are \\(A,E,S\\). The first two suffixes in dictionary order are \\(AES\\) and \\(ASE\\), giving \\(MHAES\\) and \\(MHASE\\). Thus the last letter of the \\(86\\)th word is \\(E\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
