"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 24,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The \\(600\\) students at King Middle School are divided into three groups of equal size for lunch. Each group has lunch at a different time. A computer randomly assigns each student to one of the three lunch groups. The probability that three friends, Al, Bob, and Carol, will be assigned to the same lunch group is approximately",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{27}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac19\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac18\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac16\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac13\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Each lunch group contains \\(600\\div3=200\\) students. Model the assignment by randomly filling \\(600\\) distinct seats, with \\(200\\) seats belonging to each lunch group. This keeps every group at its required size.\n\nWhichever seat Al takes, \\(599\\) seats remain for Bob. After Bob takes a seat, \\(598\\) remain for Carol. Thus there are \\(599\\cdot598\\) equally likely ordered pairs of seats for Bob and Carol. Each pair is counted once because Bob's seat is specified first and Carol's second.\n\nFor all three friends to share Al's group, Bob must take one of its \\(199\\) remaining seats. Carol must then take one of the \\(198\\) seats still available in that group. These \\(199\\cdot198\\) pairs are exactly the favorable pairs. The counts are the same wherever Al sits, so the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{199\\cdot198}{599\\cdot598}\n=\\frac{39{,}402}{358{,}202}\n\\approx0.1100.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\frac19\\approx0.1111\\), the closest listed approximation is \\(\\frac19\\), choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
