"year": 1987,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 3,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many primes less than \\(100\\) have \\(7\\) as the ones digit? (Assume the usual base \\(10\\) representation.)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(4\\)",
"B": "\\(5\\)",
"C": "\\(6\\)",
"D": "\\(7\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "The positive integers below \\(100\\) with ones digit \\(7\\) are\n\\[\n7,17,27,37,47,57,67,77,87,97.\n\\]\nThe list includes each possible tens digit from \\(0\\) through \\(9\\), so no candidate is omitted or repeated. Four candidates are composite:\n\\[\n27=3\\cdot9,\\quad57=3\\cdot19,\\quad\n77=7\\cdot11,\\quad87=3\\cdot29.\n\\]\nTo verify the others, any composite number below \\(100\\) has a prime divisor below \\(10\\): at least one factor in a nontrivial factor pair must be below \\(10\\). Therefore it suffices to check divisibility by \\(2,3,5,7\\).\n\nThe remaining two-digit numbers are \\(17,37,47,67,97\\). Their ones digits rule out divisibility by \\(2\\) or \\(5\\). Their digit sums are \\(8,10,11,13,16\\), so none is divisible by \\(3\\). They also lie strictly between the following consecutive multiples of \\(7\\), respectively:\n\\[\n14<17<21,\\quad35<37<42,\\quad42<47<49,\n\\]\n\\[\n63<67<70,\\quad91<97<98.\n\\]\nThus all five are prime. Together with the prime \\(7\\), there are \\(6\\) qualifying primes, choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
