"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The conditions are\n\\[\na=bc,\\qquad b=ac,\\qquad c=ab.\n\\]\nUsing \\(c=ab\\) in the first two equations removes \\(c\\):\n\\[\na=ab^2,\\qquad b=a^2b.\n\\]\nSince \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are nonzero, division gives \\(b^2=1\\) and \\(a^2=1\\). Thus each of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must be \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\), and \\(c=ab\\) is then fixed.\n\nEvery such pair actually works: with \\(c=ab\\), we have \\(bc=ab^2=a\\) and \\(ac=a^2b=b\\). There are two choices for \\(a\\) and two for \\(b\\), giving \\(2\\cdot2=4\\) triples:\n\\[\n(1,1,1),\\quad (1,-1,-1),\\quad\n(-1,1,-1),\\quad (-1,-1,1).\n\\]\nAll solutions are included because the equations forced these sign choices. Different ordered pairs \\((a,b)\\) produce different triples, so none is counted twice. The answer is \\(\\boxed{4}\\), choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1987/solutions",
