"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In the addition problem below, \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are nonzero digits:\n\n\\[\\begin{array}{r} 9876 \\\\ A32 \\\\ +B1 \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}\\]\n\nHow many digits (not necessarily different) are in the sum of the three whole numbers?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(4\\)",
"B": "\\(5\\)",
"C": "\\(6\\)",
"D": "\\(9\\)",
"E": "depends on the values of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "We only need the number of digits, so it is enough to find the smallest and largest possible sums. Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are nonzero digits, each is between \\(1\\) and \\(9\\).\n\nThe smallest sum uses \\(A=B=1\\):\n\\[9876+132+11=10019.\\]\nThe largest sum uses \\(A=B=9\\):\n\\[9876+932+91=10899.\\]\nEvery possible sum lies between these bounds. Both bounds are at least \\(10000\\) and less than \\(100000\\), so every sum has exactly \\(5\\) digits, choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
