"year": 1989,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 17,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The perimeter of an equilateral triangle exceeds the perimeter of a square by \\(1989\\) cm. The length of each side of the triangle exceeds the length of each side of the square by \\(d\\) cm. The square has perimeter greater than \\(0.\\) How many positive integers are not possible values for \\(d?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(9\\)",
"C": "\\(221\\)",
"D": "\\(663\\)",
"E": "infinitely many"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Let \\(s\\) be the square's side length in centimeters. Its positive perimeter means \\(s>0\\), and each triangle side has length \\(s+d\\). The perimeter difference gives\n\\[\n3(s+d)-4s=1989,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\ns=3d-1989.\n\\]\nConsequently the square has positive side length exactly when\n\\[\n3d-1989>0,\\qquad d>663.\n\\]\nThe endpoint \\(d=663\\) is excluded because it would give a square of perimeter zero.\n\nConversely, every integer \\(d>663\\) gives positive lengths \\(s=3d-1989\\) and \\(s+d\\), and these lengths satisfy the original perimeter difference. Thus the impossible positive integers are exactly \\(1,2,\\ldots,663\\). Their number is \\(\\boxed{663}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1989/solutions",
