"batch": "NA",
"number": 28,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Find the sum of the roots of \\(\\tan^2x-9\\tan x+1=0\\) that are between \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=2\\pi\\) radians.",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\frac\\pi2\\)",
"B": "\\(\\pi\\)",
"C": "\\(\\frac{3\\pi}2\\)",
"D": "\\(3\\pi\\)",
"E": "\\(4\\pi\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The constant term \\(1\\) suggests looking for reciprocal tangent values, because reciprocal positive tangents correspond to complementary acute angles. Set \\(t=\\tan x\\). We need\n\\[\nt^2-9t+1=0.\n\\]\nIts discriminant is \\(81-4=77>0\\), so it has two distinct real roots, say \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Expanding\n\\[\n(t-a)(t-b)=t^2-(a+b)t+ab\n\\]\nand comparing coefficients gives\n\\[\na+b=9,\\qquad ab=1.\n\\]\nThe positive product means the roots have the same sign, and their positive sum means both are positive. Also, \\(b=1/a\\).\n\nLet \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in(0,\\pi/2)\\) be the unique acute angles with tangents \\(a,b\\), respectively. Complementary acute angles exchange the opposite and adjacent legs in a right triangle, so\n\\[\n\\tan\\left(\\frac\\pi2-\\alpha\\right)\n=\\frac1{\\tan\\alpha}=b.\n\\]\nUniqueness on the acute-angle interval therefore gives\n\\[\n\\beta=\\frac\\pi2-\\alpha,\n\\qquad \\alpha+\\beta=\\frac\\pi2.\n\\]\nTangent has period \\(\\pi\\). For each positive tangent value, its only solutions between \\(0\\) and \\(2\\pi\\) are its acute angle and that angle plus \\(\\pi\\). Hence the four distinct roots are\n\\[\n\\alpha,\\quad\\beta,\\quad\\alpha+\\pi,\\quad\\beta+\\pi.\n\\]\nThe angles \\(\\pi/2\\) and \\(3\\pi/2\\) are excluded because tangent is undefined there. Neither endpoint \\(0\\) nor \\(2\\pi\\) satisfies the equation.\n\nThe required sum is\n\\[\n2(\\alpha+\\beta)+2\\pi\n=2\\left(\\frac\\pi2\\right)+2\\pi\n=\\boxed{3\\pi},\n\\]\nchoice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1989/solutions",
