"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Distance to a line is measured perpendicularly. Since both required distances are positive, neither point lies on an eligible line \\(\\ell\\). Thus \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) must lie either on opposite sides of \\(\\ell\\) or on the same side.\n\nIf they lie on opposite sides, \\(\\ell\\) crosses segment \\(AB\\) at a point \\(C\\). The perpendicular is the shortest segment from a point to a line, so\n\\[\nAC\\ge2,\\qquad BC\\ge3.\n\\]\nBut \\(AC+BC=AB=5\\), so both inequalities must be equalities. Therefore \\(AC=2\\), \\(BC=3\\), and \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\). There is exactly one such line: the perpendicular to \\(AB\\) at that point \\(C\\).\n\nNow suppose the points lie on the same side. Shift \\(\\ell\\) parallel to itself by \\(2\\) units toward them. The resulting line \\(k\\) passes through \\(A\\) and is \\(3-2=1\\) unit from \\(B\\). Conversely, any line \\(k\\) through \\(A\\) at distance \\(1\\) from \\(B\\) gives exactly one eligible \\(\\ell\\), by shifting \\(k\\) by \\(2\\) units away from \\(B\\).\n\nTo count these lines \\(k\\), let \\(T\\) be the perpendicular foot from \\(B\\) onto \\(k\\). Then triangle \\(ABT\\) is right, with hypotenuse \\(AB=5\\) and leg \\(BT=1\\). Its other leg is\n\\[\nAT=\\sqrt{5^2-1^2}=\\sqrt{24}.\n\\]\nThese lengths fix the right triangle's shape. With hypotenuse \\(AB\\) fixed, it has exactly two placements, reflected across \\(AB\\), and these give two different lines \\(k\\). Their parallel shifts also have different directions, so they give two distinct eligible lines \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThe two side cases are disjoint and exhaustive. There are therefore \\(1+2=\\boxed{3}\\) lines, choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
