"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many positive integers less than \\(50\\) have an odd number of positive integer divisors?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(3\\)",
"B": "\\(5\\)",
"C": "\\(7\\)",
"D": "\\(9\\)",
"E": "\\(11\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Divisors naturally come in factor pairs. For a positive integer \\(n\\), pair each positive divisor \\(d\\) with \\(n/d\\). Pairing again returns \\(d\\), so the distinct divisors are grouped into pairs unless the two members coincide.\n\nThey coincide exactly when\n\\[\nd=\\frac nd,\n\\qquad d^2=n.\n\\]\nThus a nonsquare has an even number of divisors. A square has one unpaired divisor, \\(\\sqrt n\\), and all its other divisors occur in pairs, giving an odd total. This also includes \\(1\\), whose only divisor is \\(1\\).\n\nSince \\(7^2=49<50<64=8^2\\), the eligible integers are\n\\[\n1,4,9,16,25,36,49.\n\\]\nThere are \\(7\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
