"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Which of the following could not be the units digit [one’s digit] of the square of a whole number?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1 \\)",
"B": "\\(4 \\)",
"C": "\\(5 \\)",
"D": "\\(6 \\)",
"E": "\\(8 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The units digit of a product depends only on the units digits of its factors: every contribution from a tens place or higher is a multiple of \\(10\\). Thus the units digit of a square depends only on the original number's units digit.\n\nEvery whole number ends in one of the digits \\(0\\) through \\(9\\). Squaring these ten digits gives, respectively,\n\\[\n0,\\ 1,\\ 4,\\ 9,\\ 16,\\ 25,\\ 36,\\ 49,\\ 64,\\ 81.\n\\]\nTheir units digits are therefore only \\(0,1,4,5,6,9\\). This checks every possible final digit, and all the other listed choices occur in the list. The digit that cannot occur is \\(\\boxed{8}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
