"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "When three different numbers from the set \\(\\{ -3, -2, -1, 4, 5 \\}\\) are multiplied, the largest possible product is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(10 \\)",
"B": "\\(20 \\)",
"C": "\\(30 \\)",
"D": "\\(40 \\)",
"E": "\\(60 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "A positive product is possible, so any negative product can be ruled out. There are only two positive numbers available, so a positive product of three different numbers must use two negatives and one positive. Choosing one or three negatives would give a negative product.\n\nThe possible products of two different negative numbers are\n\\[\n(-3)(-2)=6,\\qquad (-3)(-1)=3,\\qquad (-2)(-1)=2.\n\\]\nThe largest is \\(6\\). Since these pair products are positive, using \\(5\\) as the third factor gives a larger result than using \\(4\\). Therefore the largest possible product is\n\\[\n(-3)(-2)(5)=6\\cdot5=30,\n\\]\nwhich is choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
