"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 17,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A straight concrete sidewalk is to be \\(3\\) feet wide, \\(60\\) feet long, and \\(3\\) inches thick. How many cubic yards of concrete must a contractor order for the sidewalk if concrete must be ordered in a whole number of cubic yards?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2 \\)",
"B": "\\(5 \\)",
"C": "\\(12 \\)",
"D": "\\(20 \\)",
"E": "more than \\(20 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "First express all three dimensions in feet. Since \\(1\\) foot equals \\(12\\) inches, the thickness is\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{12}=\\frac14\\text{ foot}.\n\\]\nThe sidewalk is a rectangular prism, so its volume is length times width times thickness:\n\\[\n60\\cdot3\\cdot\\frac14=45\\text{ cubic feet}.\n\\]\nOne yard equals \\(3\\) feet. Thus a cubic yard is a cube measuring \\(3\\) feet along each edge and contains\n\\[\n3\\cdot3\\cdot3=27\\text{ cubic feet}.\n\\]\nOne cubic yard is insufficient because \\(27<45\\). Two cubic yards provide \\(2\\cdot27=54\\) cubic feet, which is enough. Therefore the smallest sufficient whole-number order is \\(2\\) cubic yards, choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/1990/solutions",
