"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "What is the largest quotient that can be formed using two numbers chosen from the set \\(\\{-24, -3, -2, 1, 2, 8\\}?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(-24\\)",
"B": "\\(-3\\)",
"C": "\\(8\\)",
"D": "\\(12\\)",
"E": "\\(24\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Positive quotients are possible, so a negative quotient cannot be the largest. This eliminates pairs with opposite signs. None of the available numbers is zero, so every possible denominator is allowed.\n\nFor two positive numbers, the numerator is at most \\(8\\) and the denominator is at least \\(1\\), giving a quotient at most\n\\[\n\\frac81=8.\n\\]\nFor two negative numbers, dividing gives the same positive result as dividing their magnitudes. The numerator's magnitude is at most \\(24\\), and the denominator's magnitude is at least \\(2\\), so the quotient is at most \\(24/2=12\\). This bound is attained by\n\\[\n\\frac{-24}{-2}=12.\n\\]\nThese cases cover every pair. The largest quotient is \\(\\boxed{12}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/1991/solutions",
