"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "There are several sets of three different numbers whose sum is \\(15\\) which can be chosen from \\(\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9\\}.\\) How many of these sets contain a \\(5?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(3\\)",
"B": "\\(4\\)",
"C": "\\(5\\)",
"D": "\\(6\\)",
"E": "\\(7\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Every set being counted contains \\(5\\), so its other two numbers must sum to \\(15-5=10\\). List each pair with its smaller number first. That smaller number must be \\(1,2,3,\\) or \\(4\\): two different numbers both at least \\(5\\) would have a sum greater than \\(10\\).\n\nThese four possible smaller numbers give exactly the pairs\n\\[\n\\{1,9\\},\\quad\\{2,8\\},\\quad\\{3,7\\},\\quad\\{4,6\\}.\n\\]\nEach pair consists of different allowed numbers, neither equal to \\(5\\). Adding \\(5\\) to each pair gives a valid set. Every possible smaller number has been checked, and listing smaller numbers first prevents counting a pair twice. Thus there are \\(\\boxed{4}\\) sets, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
