"skill": [],
"year": 1992,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(i=\\sqrt{-1}.\\) For \\(n\\ge1,\\) define a sequence of complex numbers by \\[ \\begin{aligned} z_1&=0,\\\\ z_{n+1}&=z_n^2+i. \\end{aligned} \\] In the complex plane, how far from the origin is \\(z_{111}?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(\\sqrt2\\)",
"C": "\\(\\sqrt3\\)",
"D": "\\(\\sqrt{110}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\sqrt{2^{55}}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Computing the first few terms can reveal whether the recurrence repeats. Starting from \\(z_1=0\\), we obtain\n\\[z_2=i,\\qquad z_3=i^2+i=-1+i.\\]\nNow check what happens to each of the next two values:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-1+i)^2+i&=1-2i-1+i=-i,\\\\\n(-i)^2+i&=-1+i.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the recurrence sends \\(-1+i\\) to \\(-i\\), and then sends \\(-i\\) back to \\(-1+i\\). These two transitions guarantee that the terms alternate forever from \\(z_3\\) onward.\n\nSince \\(111=3+2\\cdot54\\), we have \\(z_{111}=-1+i\\). This complex number corresponds to the point \\((-1,1)\\), whose distance from the origin is\n\\[\\sqrt{(-1)^2+1^2}=\\boxed{\\sqrt2}.\\]\nThe answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1992/solutions",
