"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "What is the value of the following expression?\n\n\\[ \\begin{gathered} \\small (1901 + 1902 + 1903 + \\cdots + 1993) \\\\ \\small {}- (101 + 102 + 103 + \\cdots + 193) \\end{gathered} \\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(167{,}400\\)",
"B": "\\(172{,}050\\)",
"C": "\\(181{,}071\\)",
"D": "\\(199{,}300\\)",
"E": "\\(362{,}142\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The two sums have matching patterns: \\(1901\\) is \\(1800\\) more than \\(101\\), \\(1902\\) is \\(1800\\) more than \\(102\\), and this continues through the last terms. Pairing corresponding terms therefore avoids calculating either large sum separately.\n\nThere are \\(193-101+1=93\\) terms in each sum, including both endpoints. Subtracting the second sum removes each of its terms once, so the difference is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1901-101)+\\cdots+(1993-193)\\\\\n&\\qquad=93\\times1800=167{,}400.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe value is \\(167{,}400\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
