"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Replacing every die face \\(r\\) by \\(7-r\\) gives a useful symmetry. This replacement is its own inverse, and it changes a sum \\(u\\) into \\(7n-u\\). Thus sums \\(u\\) and \\(7n-u\\) have the same probability.\n\nSince \\(332\\cdot6=1992<1994\\), positive probability for a sum of \\(1994\\) requires \\(n\\ge333\\). When \\(n=333\\), the reflected sum is\n\\[\n7\\cdot333-1994=337.\n\\]\nA sum of \\(337\\) is possible with \\(332\\) ones and one five. Its reflected roll has sum \\(1994\\), so \\(337\\) is an attainable value of \\(S\\).\n\nTo prove that it is smallest, suppose an integer \\(S<337\\) had the same positive probability as \\(1994\\). Every die contributes at least \\(1\\), so\n\\[\n333\\le n\\le S\\le336.\n\\]\nSet \\(T=7n-1994\\). By reflection, \\(T\\) has the same probability as \\(1994\\), and the four possible values of \\(n\\) give\n\\[\n337\\le T\\le358.\n\\]\nWe will show that there are strictly more rolls with sum \\(T\\) than with sum \\(S\\).\n\nLabel the dice so that a roll is an ordered list. In any roll with sum \\(S\\), each face is at most\n\\[\nS-(n-1)\\le336-332=4.\n\\]\nThere is therefore room to increase any chosen face by \\(1\\). Let \\(k=T-S\\). We have \\(1\\le k\\le25<n\\). Add \\(1\\) to each of the first \\(k\\) dice. The resulting roll is valid and has sum \\(T\\). Different original rolls give different resulting rolls, because subtracting \\(1\\) from those same dice recovers the original roll.\n\nEvery resulting roll has its first die at least \\(2\\). However, there is also a roll of sum \\(T\\) whose first die is \\(1\\): let\n\\[\nd=T-n=6n-1994.\n\\]\nHere \\(4\\le d\\le22<n\\), so we may set \\(d\\) of the other dice to \\(2\\) and all remaining dice to \\(1\\). Its sum is \\(n+d=T\\), and it is outside the constructed image.\n\nThus strictly more ordered rolls have sum \\(T\\) than sum \\(S\\). All ordered rolls for the same \\(n\\) are equally likely, so \\(P(T)>P(S)\\), contradicting their required equality. Therefore the smallest possible value is \\(\\boxed{337}\\), choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
