"question": "The unit’s digit (one’s digit) of the product of any six consecutive positive whole numbers is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(6\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "A product with a factor of \\(10\\) ends in \\(0\\), so look for factors of \\(5\\) and \\(2\\).\n\nMultiples of \\(5\\) occur every five numbers, so the first five of the six consecutive numbers contain a multiple of \\(5\\). Also, odd and even numbers alternate, so the six numbers contain three even numbers. We can therefore choose an even number different from the chosen multiple of \\(5\\).\n\nMultiply these two chosen numbers first. One supplies a factor of \\(5\\), and the other supplies a factor of \\(2\\), so their product is a multiple of \\(5\\times2=10\\). Multiplying by the remaining four whole numbers keeps the ones digit \\(0\\). Thus the required digit is \\(\\boxed{0}\\), choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
