"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For how many three-digit whole numbers does the sum of the digits equal \\(25?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2\\)",
"B": "\\(4\\)",
"C": "\\(6\\)",
"D": "\\(8\\)",
"E": "\\(10\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "The largest possible sum of three digits is \\(9+9+9=27\\). To obtain a sum of \\(25\\), the digits must have a total reduction of \\(2\\) from these three nines.\n\nThere are only two ways to distribute this reduction:\n\n- Reduce one digit by \\(2\\). The digits are \\(7,9,9\\), giving \\(799,979,997\\).\n- Reduce two digits by \\(1\\) each. The digits are \\(8,8,9\\), giving \\(889,898,988\\).\n\nThese cases exhaust the possibilities because the reductions are nonnegative whole numbers totaling \\(2\\). Each listed number is distinct, and the two cases cannot overlap because only the first contains a \\(7\\). Thus there are \\(3+3=6\\) numbers, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/1994/solutions",
