"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The area of the triangle bounded by the lines \\(y=x,\\) \\(y=-x\\) and \\(y=6\\) is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(12\\)",
"B": "\\(12\\sqrt2\\)",
"C": "\\(24\\)",
"D": "\\(24\\sqrt2\\)",
"E": "\\(36\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The horizontal line \\(y=6\\) provides a convenient base, so first find where the other two lines meet it. Substituting \\(y=6\\) into \\(y=x\\) gives \\((6,6)\\), and substituting into \\(y=-x\\) gives \\((-6,6)\\). The lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=-x\\) meet where \\(x=-x\\), namely at \\((0,0)\\).\n\nThe horizontal base from \\((-6,6)\\) to \\((6,6)\\) has length \\(6-(-6)=12\\). Its perpendicular height from \\((0,0)\\) is the vertical distance to \\(y=6\\), which is \\(6\\). Thus the triangle's area is\n\\[\n\\frac12\\cdot12\\cdot6=36.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{36}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
