"question": "What is the \\(100\\)th digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal form of \\(\\dfrac{4}{37}?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\)",
"D": "\\(7\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Use long division until a remainder repeats, because the same remainder will produce the same later digits. Starting with remainder \\(4\\), multiply each remainder by \\(10\\) to obtain the next decimal digit:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n40&=37\\times1+3,\\\\\n30&=37\\times0+30,\\\\\n300&=37\\times8+4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe digits produced are \\(1,0,8\\), and the final remainder is the original \\(4\\). Thus these steps repeat forever, giving\n\\[\n\\frac4{37}=0.\\overline{108}.\n\\]\nEach block has three digits. Since \\(99=33\\times3\\), the first \\(99\\) digits form \\(33\\) complete blocks. The \\(100\\)th digit is the first digit of the next block, namely \\(1\\), choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
