"question": "How many four-digit whole numbers are there such that the leftmost digit is odd, the second digit is even, and all four digits are different?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1120\\)",
"B": "\\(1400\\)",
"C": "\\(1800\\)",
"D": "\\(2025\\)",
"E": "\\(2500\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Choose the digits in order so that we can exclude any digit already used. The first digit can be any of \\(1,3,5,7,9\\), giving \\(5\\) choices. Each is nonzero, so the number will have four digits.\n\nThe second digit can be any of \\(0,2,4,6,8\\), also giving \\(5\\) choices. Zero is allowed here, and every even digit differs from the odd first digit.\n\nTwo different digits have now been used, leaving \\(8\\) choices for the third digit and then \\(7\\) choices for the fourth. Multiplying gives\n\\[\n5\\cdot5\\cdot8\\cdot7=\\boxed{1400}.\n\\]\nEvery valid number is produced by exactly one sequence of these choices, so none are omitted or counted twice. The answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
