"year": 1996,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 1,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The addition below is incorrect. What is the largest digit that can be changed to make the addition correct? \\[ \\begin{array}{r} 641\\\\ 852\\\\ {}+973\\\\ \\hline 2456 \\end{array} \\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(4\\)",
"B": "\\(5\\)",
"C": "\\(6\\)",
"D": "\\(7\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "First calculate the actual sum:\n\\[\n641+852+973=2466.\n\\]\nThis is \\(10\\) greater than the printed result, \\(2456\\). Thus a single-digit correction must either decrease an addend by \\(10\\) or increase the result by \\(10\\).\n\nChanging a units digit changes its number by at most \\(9\\). Changing a hundreds or thousands digit changes its number by a nonzero multiple of \\(100\\). Therefore the correction must be in the tens place.\n\nWe can lower an addend's tens digit by \\(1\\): change the \\(4\\) in \\(641\\) to \\(3\\), the \\(5\\) in \\(852\\) to \\(4\\), or the \\(7\\) in \\(973\\) to \\(6\\). Alternatively, we can change the result's tens digit from \\(5\\) to \\(6\\).\n\nThe largest original digit among these possibilities is \\(\\boxed{7}\\). Indeed,\n\\[\n641+852+963=2456.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1996/solutions",
