"skill": [],
"year": 1996,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 9,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Triangle \\(PAB\\) and square \\(ABCD\\) are in perpendicular planes. Given that \\(PA=3,\\) \\(PB=4,\\) and \\(AB=5,\\) what is \\(PD?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(5\\)",
"B": "\\(\\sqrt{34}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\sqrt{41}\\)",
"D": "\\(2\\sqrt{13}\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Triangle \\(PAD\\) contains the desired length. The square gives \\(AD=AB=5\\), so we first show that \\(AD\\perp AP\\).\n\nThe two given planes meet along \\(AB\\). Draw a line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(A\\) in plane \\(PAB\\), perpendicular to \\(AB\\). Also \\(AD\\perp AB\\), because \\(ABCD\\) is a square. The angle between the planes is measured in a cross-section perpendicular to their common line \\(AB\\). The two lines in that cross-section are \\(AD\\) and \\(\\ell\\); since the planes are perpendicular, \\(AD\\perp\\ell\\).\n\nThus \\(AD\\) is perpendicular to the two intersecting lines \\(AB\\) and \\(\\ell\\) in plane \\(PAB\\). It is therefore perpendicular to that plane, and in particular to \\(AP\\). Applying the Pythagorean theorem to the right triangle \\(PAD\\),\n\\[\nPD^2=PA^2+AD^2=3^2+5^2=34.\n\\]\nSince a length is positive, \\(PD=\\sqrt{34}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
