"year": 1996,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are \\(10\\) units apart. Points \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) are \\(4\\) units apart. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are \\(3\\) units apart. If \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) are as close as possible, then the number of units between them is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(3\\)",
"C": "\\(9\\)",
"D": "\\(11\\)",
"E": "\\(17\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "The straight segment from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) is no longer than any path joining them. In particular, the path from \\(A\\) through \\(D\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(B\\) gives\n\\[\n10=AB\\le AD+DC+CB=AD+3+4.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(AD\\ge3\\).\n\nTo attain this bound, place the points on one line in the order \\(A,D,C,B\\), with successive gaps \\(3,3,4\\). Then \\(AB=3+3+4=10\\), \\(BC=4\\), and \\(CD=3\\), as required, while \\(AD=3\\).\n\nThus the smallest possible distance is \\(\\boxed{3}\\) units, choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/1996/solutions",
