"year": 1997,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 12,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "If \\(m\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers and \\(mb\\gt0,\\) then the line whose equation is \\(y=mx+b\\) cannot contain the point",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\((0,1997)\\)",
"B": "\\((0,-1997)\\)",
"C": "\\((19,97)\\)",
"D": "\\((19,-97)\\)",
"E": "\\((1997,0)\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The condition \\(mb>0\\) says that \\(m\\) and \\(b\\) are nonzero and have the same sign. At the horizontal intercept, \\(y=0\\), so\n\\[\n0=mx+b,\n\\qquad x=-\\frac bm<0.\n\\]\nDivision by \\(m\\) is valid because \\(m\\ne0\\). Thus every allowed line crosses the horizontal axis at a negative coordinate, making \\((1997,0)\\) impossible.\n\nThe other options really are possible. The lines\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=x+1997,\\\\\ny&=-x-1997,\\\\\ny&=x+78,\\\\\ny&=-x-78\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\ncontain the points in choices A, B, C, and D, respectively, and each has \\(mb>0\\). Therefore the excluded point is \\(\\boxed{(1997,0)}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm E\\).",
