"year": 1997,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 20,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Which one of the following integers can be expressed as the sum of \\(100\\) consecutive positive integers?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1{,}627{,}384{,}950\\)",
"B": "\\(2{,}345{,}678{,}910\\)",
"C": "\\(3{,}579{,}111{,}300\\)",
"D": "\\(4{,}692{,}581{,}470\\)",
"E": "\\(5{,}815{,}937{,}260\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Let \\(a\\) be the first of the 100 consecutive positive integers. Pairing the first with the last, the second with the next-to-last, and so on gives 50 pairs, each with sum \\(2a+99\\). Therefore the total is\n\\[\nS=50(2a+99)=100a+4950.\n\\]\nSince \\(100a\\) ends in 00, every such sum ends in 50. Only choice A has those last two digits.\n\nTo verify that A really works, solve for its first term:\n\\[\na=\\frac{1{,}627{,}384{,}950-4950}{100}\n=16{,}273{,}800.\n\\]\nThis is a positive integer, so the 100 consecutive integers beginning there have the required sum. The answer is \\(\\boxed{1{,}627{,}384{,}950}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
