"year": 1997,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 20,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A pair of \\(8\\)-sided dice has sides numbered \\(1\\) through \\(8.\\) Each side has the same probability (chance) of landing face up. The probability that the product of the two numbers that land face-up exceeds \\(36\\) is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{32}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{11}{64}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{16}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Distinguish the dice as first and second. Each of the \\(8\\) results on the first die can occur with each of the \\(8\\) results on the second, giving \\(8\\cdot8=64\\) equally likely ordered outcomes.\n\nIf the first die shows at most \\(4\\), the product is at most \\(4\\cdot8=32\\), so none of those outcomes qualify. For the remaining first-die values, the qualifying second-die values are:\n\nFor \\(5\\): only \\(8\\), giving \\(1\\) outcome.\n\nFor \\(6\\): \\(7,8\\), giving \\(2\\) outcomes. In particular, \\(6\\cdot6=36\\) is excluded.\n\nFor \\(7\\): \\(6,7,8\\), giving \\(3\\) outcomes.\n\nFor \\(8\\): \\(5,6,7,8\\), giving \\(4\\) outcomes.\n\nThese cases cover every first-die value, and each ordered outcome appears in just one case. Thus the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{1+2+3+4}{64}=\\frac{10}{64}=\\boxed{\\frac5{32}}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/1997/solutions",
