"year": 1998,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Define \\([a,b,c]\\) to mean \\(\\frac{a+b}{c},\\) where \\(c\\ne0.\\) What is the value of \\[ [[60,30,90],[2,1,3],[10,5,15]]? \\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(0.5\\)",
"C": "\\(1\\)",
"D": "\\(1.5\\)",
"E": "\\(2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The entries of the outer bracket are themselves bracket values, so first apply the given rule to the three inner brackets:\n\\[\n[60,30,90]=\\frac{60+30}{90}=1,\n\\]\n\\[\n[2,1,3]=\\frac{2+1}{3}=1,\n\\]\n\\[\n[10,5,15]=\\frac{10+5}{15}=1.\n\\]\nThe divisors \\(90\\), \\(3\\), and \\(15\\) are all nonzero. Substituting these three results into the outer bracket gives\n\\[\n[1,1,1]=\\frac{1+1}{1}=\\boxed{2}.\n\\]\nIts divisor is also nonzero, so the expression is defined. The answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1998/solutions",
