"batch": "NA",
"number": 12,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many different prime numbers are factors of \\(N\\) if \\[ \\log_2(\\log_3(\\log_5(\\log_7N)))=11? \\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(3\\)",
"D": "\\(4\\)",
"E": "\\(7\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Use the definition \\(\\log_b x=y\\) if and only if \\(x=b^y\\). Removing the logarithms from outside inward gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_3(\\log_5(\\log_7N))&=2^{11},\\\\\n\\log_5(\\log_7N)&=3^{2^{11}},\\\\\n\\log_7N&=5^{3^{2^{11}}},\\\\\nN&=7^{5^{3^{2^{11}}}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll bases exceed \\(1\\), and the resulting logarithm arguments are positive, so these conversions respect the domains of the original logarithms.\n\nThe exponent \\(5^{3^{2^{11}}}\\) is a positive integer. Hence \\(N\\) is a positive integer power of \\(7\\). Since \\(7\\) is prime, its powers have no prime divisor other than \\(7\\). There is exactly \\(1\\) different prime factor, choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
