"year": 1998,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A regular hexagon and an equilateral triangle have equal areas. What is the ratio of the length of a side of the triangle to the length of a side of the hexagon?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\sqrt3\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(\\sqrt6\\)",
"D": "\\(3\\)",
"E": "\\(6\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Let the hexagon's side length be \\(s>0\\), and let the triangle's side length be \\(t>0\\). Joining the hexagon's center to its vertices divides it into six congruent triangles. Each has two equal radii and a central angle of \\(360^\\circ/6=60^\\circ\\), so each is equilateral with side length \\(s\\).\n\nThe given equilateral triangle thus has six times the area of one small equilateral triangle. These triangles are similar, and their area ratio is the square of their side-length ratio. Therefore\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{t}{s}\\right)^2=6.\n\\]\nSince side lengths are positive, the requested ratio is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt6}\\), choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
