"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 16,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "What is the radius of a circle inscribed in a rhombus with diagonals of length \\(10\\) and \\(24?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(4\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{58}{13}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{60}{13}\\)",
"D": "\\(5\\)",
"E": "\\(6\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Method 1: Express the area using the radius\n\nThe diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles, so each side is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs \\(5\\) and \\(12\\). Thus the side length is\n\\[\n\\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=13.\n\\]\nThe area of the rhombus is half the product of its diagonals:\n\\[\nK=\\frac{10\\cdot24}{2}=120.\n\\]\nLet \\(I\\) be the center of the inscribed circle and let its radius be \\(r\\). Joining \\(I\\) to all four vertices partitions the rhombus into four triangles. Each has base \\(13\\) and height \\(r\\), because the radius to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the corresponding side. Therefore\n\\[\n120=4\\left(\\frac{13r}{2}\\right)=26r.\n\\]\nIt follows that \\(r=\\dfrac{60}{13}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Use similar right triangles\n\nLabel the rhombus \\(ABCD\\) in order so that \\(AC=24\\) and \\(BD=10\\), and let the diagonals meet at \\(O\\). They bisect each other at right angles, so\n\\[\nOA=12,\\qquad OB=5,\\qquad AB=\\sqrt{12^2+5^2}=13.\n\\]\nThe circle's center is equidistant from the two sides meeting at each vertex, so it lies on each internal angle bisector. A rhombus's diagonals bisect its vertex angles; consequently the circle's center is \\(O\\).\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the point where the circle touches \\(AB\\), and let its radius be \\(r\\). Then \\(OT=r\\) and \\(OT\\perp AB\\). Triangles \\(AOT\\) and \\(ABO\\) are similar: they share the angle between \\(AO\\) and \\(AB\\), and their angles at \\(T\\) and \\(O\\), respectively, are right angles. Matching corresponding sides gives\n\\[\n\\frac{OT}{OB}=\\frac{AO}{AB},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{r}{5}=\\frac{12}{13}.\n\\]\nHence \\(r=\\dfrac{60}{13}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/1999/solutions",
