"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 17,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Problems \\(17,\\) \\(18,\\) and \\(19\\) refer to the following:\n\nCookies For a Crowd\n\nAt Central Middle School the \\(108\\) students who take the AMC \\(8\\) meet in the evening to talk about problems and eat an average of two cookies apiece. Walter and Gretel are baking Bonnie’s Best Bar Cookies this year. Their recipe, which makes a pan of \\(15\\) cookies, lists these items: \\(1\\dfrac{1}{2}\\) cups of flour, \\(2\\) eggs, \\(3\\) tablespoons butter, \\(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\) cups sugar, and \\(1\\) package of chocolate drops. They will make only full recipes, not partial recipes.\n\nWalter can buy eggs by the half-dozen. How many half-dozens should he buy to make enough cookies? (Some eggs and some cookies may be left over.)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(5\\)",
"D": "\\(7\\)",
"E": "\\(15\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "The students need a total of\n\\[\n108\\cdot2=216\\text{ cookies}.\n\\]\nBecause only full recipes are allowed, compare nearby whole numbers of recipes. Fourteen recipes make \\(14\\cdot15=210\\) cookies, which is too few. Fifteen recipes make \\(15\\cdot15=225\\) cookies, which is enough. Thus \\(15\\) is the least number of full recipes needed.\n\nEach recipe uses \\(2\\) eggs, so these recipes require \\(15\\cdot2=30\\) eggs. A half-dozen contains \\(6\\) eggs, and\n\\[\n30\\div6=5.\n\\]\nWalter should buy \\(5\\) half-dozens, choice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
