"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Two non-zero real numbers, \\(a\\) and \\(b,\\) satisfy \\(ab = a - b.\\) Find a possible value of \\[\\dfrac{a}{b} + \\dfrac{b}{a} - ab.\\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(-2\\)",
"B": "\\(-\\dfrac12\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac13\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac12\\)",
"E": "\\(2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Method 1: Divide the constraint and substitute\n\nThe expression contains \\(a/b\\) and \\(b/a\\), so divide the given equation by each denominator to obtain useful replacements. Both divisions are valid because \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are nonzero.\n\nDividing \\(ab=a-b\\) by \\(b\\) gives\n\\[\na=\\frac ab-1,\\qquad \\frac ab=a+1.\n\\]\nDividing by \\(a\\) gives\n\\[\nb=1-\\frac ba,\\qquad \\frac ba=1-b.\n\\]\nSubstituting these into the requested expression yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac ab+\\frac ba-ab\n&=(a+1)+(1-b)-ab\\\\\n&=2+(a-b)-ab\\\\\n&=2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nsince \\(a-b=ab\\). For example, \\(a=1\\) and \\(b=\\tfrac12\\) satisfy the condition, so this value is possible. The answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Use the square of the known difference\n\nCombining the two fractions produces \\(a^2+b^2\\), which can be rewritten using the known difference \\(a-b\\). Since \\(ab\\ne0\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac ab+\\frac ba\n&=\\frac{a^2+b^2}{ab}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(a-b)^2+2ab}{ab}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere we used \\((a-b)^2=a^2-2ab+b^2\\). Now substitute \\(a-b=ab\\):\n\\[\n\\frac ab+\\frac ba\n=\\frac{(ab)^2+2ab}{ab}\n=ab+2.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(ab\\) gives \\(2\\). The pair \\(a=1\\), \\(b=\\tfrac12\\) satisfies the original condition, so \\(\\boxed{2}\\) is possible, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
