"year": 2000,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 25,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In year \\(N,\\) the \\(300\\)th day of the year is a Tuesday. In year \\(N + 1,\\) the \\(200\\)th day is also a Tuesday. On what day of the week did the \\(100\\)th day of year \\(N - 1\\) occur?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "Thursday",
"B": "Friday",
"C": "Saturday",
"D": "Sunday",
"E": "Monday"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Two dates have the same weekday exactly when the number of days between them is a multiple of \\(7\\). Let \\(L\\) be the number of days in year \\(N\\). From its \\(300\\)th day to the \\(200\\)th day of the next year, the elapsed time is\n\\[\n(L-300)+200=L-100.\n\\]\nFor an ordinary year this would be \\(265=7\\cdot37+6\\) days, which cannot connect two Tuesdays. For a leap year it is \\(266=7\\cdot38\\) days. Thus year \\(N\\) is a leap year.\n\nLeap years are not consecutive, so year \\(N-1\\) has \\(365\\) days. From its \\(100\\)th day to the \\(300\\)th day of year \\(N\\), the elapsed time is\n\\[\n(365-100)+300=565=7\\cdot80+5.\n\\]\nThe desired day is therefore five weekdays before Tuesday. Counting backward gives Monday, Sunday, Saturday, Friday, Thursday. Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{Thursday}}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
