"year": 2000,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many positive integers \\(b\\) have the property that \\(\\log_b 729\\) is a positive integer?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\)",
"D": "\\(3\\)",
"E": "\\(4\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "A logarithm base must be positive and different from \\(1\\), so an integer base here must satisfy \\(b\\ge2\\). Let \\(k=\\log_b729\\), where \\(k\\) is a positive integer. By the definition of a logarithm,\n\\[\nb^k=729=3^6.\n\\]\nIf \\(b\\) had any prime factor other than \\(3\\), that prime would also divide \\(b^k\\), which is impossible. Therefore \\(b=3^d\\) for some positive integer \\(d\\).\n\nSubstituting this form gives\n\\[\n(3^d)^k=3^{dk}=3^6,\n\\]\nso \\(dk=6\\). The positive factor pairs of \\(6\\) give exactly\n\\[\n(b,k)=(3,6),(9,3),(27,2),(729,1).\n\\]\nEach pair satisfies the original logarithm condition, and the four bases are distinct. The prime-factor argument excludes all other integer bases. Thus there are \\(\\boxed{4}\\) possibilities, choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
