"number": 3,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many whole numbers lie in the interval between \\(\\frac{5}{3}\\) and \\(2\\pi?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2\\)",
"B": "\\(3\\)",
"C": "\\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(5\\)",
"E": "infinitely many"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "We only need to know which consecutive whole numbers surround each endpoint. Since \\(5/3=1\\frac23\\), and \\(3<\\pi<3.5\\),\n\\[\n1<\\frac53<2,\n\\qquad 6<2\\pi<7.\n\\]\nThus every whole number from \\(2\\) through \\(6\\) lies between the endpoints, while \\(1\\) is too small and \\(7\\) is too large. The complete list is \\(2,3,4,5,6\\), containing \\(5\\) numbers. Neither endpoint is a whole number, so including or excluding the endpoints does not change the count. The answer is choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2000/solutions",
