"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Work in cents. Nickels, dimes, and quarters are worth \\(5\\), \\(10\\), and \\(25\\) cents, so their combined value is a multiple of \\(5\\). There can be at most \\(6\\) pennies, because at least three of the nine coins must be the other types. Among \\(1\\) through \\(6\\) pennies, only \\(2\\) pennies leave a multiple of \\(5\\) when subtracted from \\(102\\).\n\nThus the other \\(7\\) coins total \\(100\\) cents. Imagine starting with seven nickels, worth \\(35\\) cents. Replacing a nickel with a dime adds \\(5\\) cents, and replacing one with a quarter adds \\(20\\) cents. We need \\(100-35=65\\) extra cents.\n\nFour quarters would already add \\(80\\) cents, too much. Since at least one quarter is required, there are just three cases:\n\n- One quarter adds \\(20\\) cents, leaving \\(45\\) extra cents, which would require \\(9\\) dimes. There are too few coins.\n- Two quarters add \\(40\\) cents, leaving \\(25\\) extra cents, which requires \\(5\\) dimes. Those seven coins leave no nickel.\n- Three quarters add \\(60\\) cents, leaving \\(5\\) extra cents, which requires \\(1\\) dime. The remaining three coins are nickels.\n\nThe resulting collection has two pennies, three nickels, one dime, and three quarters. It has nine coins and value\n\\[\n2+15+10+75=102\\text{ cents}.\n\\]\nTherefore you must have \\(\\boxed{1}\\) dime, choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2000/solutions",
