"year": 2001,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 16,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The mean of three numbers is \\(10\\) more than the least of the numbers and \\(15\\) less than the greatest. The median of the three numbers is \\(5.\\) What is their sum?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(5\\)",
"B": "\\(20\\)",
"C": "\\(25\\)",
"D": "\\(30\\)",
"E": "\\(36\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The mean is a balance point: the total amount above it equals the total amount below it. This follows because replacing all three numbers by their mean preserves their sum.\n\nThe greatest number is \\(15\\) above the mean, while the least is \\(10\\) below it. To balance the remaining difference of \\(15-10=5\\), the middle number must be \\(5\\) below the mean.\n\nFor three ordered numbers, the median is the middle number. Since that number is \\(5\\), the mean is \\(5+5=10\\). The resulting ordered numbers are \\(0,5,25\\), which satisfy both stated gaps. Their sum is\n\\[\n3\\cdot10=30,\n\\]\nchoice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
