"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(768\\)",
"B": "\\(801\\)",
"C": "\\(934\\)",
"D": "\\(1067\\)",
"E": "\\(1167\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "First count all multiples of \\(3\\) or \\(4\\), then remove those that are also multiples of \\(5\\). In each count, common multiples must be counted only once.\n\nThe number of positive multiples of a positive integer \\(d\\) through \\(2001\\) is the whole-number quotient when \\(2001\\) is divided by \\(d\\). There are \\(667\\) multiples of \\(3\\) and \\(500\\) multiples of \\(4\\). Their overlap consists exactly of the \\(166\\) multiples of \\(12\\). Thus the initial count is\n\\[\n667+500-166=1001.\n\\]\nSubtracting the overlap once corrects the fact that each multiple of \\(12\\) was initially counted twice.\n\nAmong these \\(1001\\) numbers, a multiple of \\(5\\) must be a multiple of \\(15\\) or \\(20\\). There are \\(133\\) multiples of \\(15\\) and \\(100\\) multiples of \\(20\\). The \\(33\\) multiples of \\(60\\) belong to both groups, so the number to remove is\n\\[\n133+100-33=200.\n\\]\nThese are exactly the forbidden multiples of \\(5\\), each counted once. The required count is therefore\n\\[\n1001-200=\\boxed{801},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
