"question": "The polynomial \\(P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c\\) has the property that the mean of its zeros, the product of its zeros, and the sum of its coefficients are all equal. If the \\(y\\)-intercept of the graph of \\(y = P(x)\\) is \\(2,\\) what is \\(b?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(-11\\)",
"B": "\\(-10\\)",
"C": "\\(-9\\)",
"D": "\\(1\\)",
"E": "\\(5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The \\(y\\)-intercept is \\(P(0)\\), so \\(c=2\\). To connect the other conditions to the coefficients, let \\(r,s,t\\) be the three zeros, counted with multiplicity. Expanding their linear factors gives\n\\[\nP(x)=(x-r)(x-s)(x-t)\n=x^3-(r+s+t)x^2+(rs+rt+st)x-rst.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(x^2\\) comes from choosing one root term from the factors, while the constant term comes from choosing all three. Comparing coefficients therefore gives\n\\[\nr+s+t=-a,\\qquad rst=-c=-2.\n\\]\nSince the mean of the zeros equals their product,\n\\[\n\\frac{-a}{3}=-2,\n\\]\nso \\(a=6\\). The sum of the coefficients also equals \\(-2\\), so\n\\[\n1+6+b+2=-2.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(\\boxed{b=-11}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2001/solutions",
