"year": 2001,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 17,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For the game show Who Wants To Be a Millionaire? the dollar values of each question are shown in the following table (where \\(\\text{K}=1000\\)). \\[ \\begin{array}{ccccc} \\boldsymbol{\\#} & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ \\boldsymbol{\\$} & 100 & 200 & 300 \\end{array} \\]\\[ \\begin{array}{cccccc} 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 500 & 1\\text{K} & 2\\text{K} & 4\\text{K} & 8\\text{K} \\end{array} \\] \\[ \\begin{array}{ccccc} 9 & 10 & 11 & 12 \\\\ 16\\text{K} & 32\\text{K} & 64\\text{K} & 125\\text{K} \\end{array} \\] \\[ \\begin{array}{ccc} 13 & 14 & 15 \\\\ 250\\text{K} & 500\\text{K} & 1000\\text{K} \\end{array} \\] Between which two questions is the percent increase of the value the smallest?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "From \\(1\\) to \\(2\\)",
"B": "From \\(2\\) to \\(3\\)",
"C": "From \\(3\\) to \\(4\\)",
"D": "From \\(11\\) to \\(12\\)",
"E": "From \\(14\\) to \\(15\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "A percent increase compares the gain with the original value, so use\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{new value}-\\text{original value}}{\\text{original value}}\\times100\\%.\n\\]\nEvery adjacent step in the table doubles the value except the steps from \\(2\\) to \\(3\\), from \\(3\\) to \\(4\\), and from \\(11\\) to \\(12\\). Doubling is a \\(100\\%\\) increase. For the three exceptions, the increases are\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\text{ to }3 &: \\frac{300-200}{200}\\times100\\%=50\\%,\\\\\n3\\text{ to }4 &: \\frac{500-300}{300}\\times100\\%=66\\tfrac23\\%,\\\\\n11\\text{ to }12 &: \\frac{125-64}{64}\\times100\\%=95.3125\\%.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIn the last calculation, measuring both values in thousands leaves the ratio unchanged. The smallest increase is \\(\\boxed{50\\%}\\), from question \\(2\\) to question \\(3\\), choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2001/solutions",
