"year": 2001,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 21,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The mean of a set of five different positive integers is \\(15.\\) The median is \\(18.\\) The maximum possible value of the largest of these five integers is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(19\\)",
"B": "\\(24\\)",
"C": "\\(32\\)",
"D": "\\(35\\)",
"E": "\\(40\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The mean fixes the sum of the five integers at \\(5\\times15=75\\). To make the largest integer as large as possible, we need the other four to use as little of this sum as the conditions allow.\n\nWrite the numbers in increasing order as \\(a<b<c<d<e\\). The median gives \\(c=18\\). Because the numbers are different positive integers, \\(a\\ge1\\), \\(b\\ge2\\), and \\(d\\ge19\\). Consequently,\n\\[\ne=75-(a+b+18+d)\n\\le75-(1+2+18+19)=35.\n\\]\nThis bound can be reached: \\(1,2,18,19,35\\) are different positive integers with sum \\(75\\) and median \\(18\\). Thus the maximum possible largest integer is \\(\\boxed{35}\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2001/solutions",
