"number": 22,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "On a twenty-question test, each correct answer is worth \\(5\\) points, each unanswered question is worth \\(1\\) point and each incorrect answer is worth \\(0\\) points. Which of the following scores is NOT possible?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(90\\)",
"B": "\\(91\\)",
"C": "\\(92\\)",
"D": "\\(95\\)",
"E": "\\(97\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "A perfect test earns \\(20\\times5=100\\) points. Replacing a correct answer with an unanswered question loses \\(5-1=4\\) points; replacing it with an incorrect answer loses \\(5\\) points. Thus any test that is not perfect loses at least \\(4\\) points and scores at most \\(96\\). A score of \\(97\\), which loses only \\(3\\) points, is impossible.\n\nTo check that no other listed choice is impossible, here are valid ways to obtain each one:\n\n- \\(90\\): \\(18\\) correct and \\(2\\) incorrect.\n- \\(91\\): \\(18\\) correct, \\(1\\) unanswered, and \\(1\\) incorrect.\n- \\(92\\): \\(18\\) correct and \\(2\\) unanswered.\n- \\(95\\): \\(19\\) correct and \\(1\\) incorrect.\n\nEach case uses exactly \\(20\\) questions. Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{97}\\), choice \\(\\text{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2001/solutions",
