"year": 2002,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The digits \\(1,\\) \\(2,\\) \\(3,\\) \\(4,\\) \\(5,\\) \\(6,\\) \\(7,\\) and \\(9\\) are used to form four two-digit prime numbers, with each digit used exactly once. What is the sum of these four primes?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(150\\)",
"B": "\\(160\\)",
"C": "\\(170\\)",
"D": "\\(180\\)",
"E": "\\(190\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "A two-digit prime cannot end in an even digit or in \\(5\\), since it would then have \\(2\\) or \\(5\\) as a proper factor. Among the supplied digits, only \\(1,3,7,9\\) can therefore be units digits.\n\nThere are four primes and exactly four available units digits, each used once. Consequently, the tens digits must be \\(2,4,5,6\\). Whatever valid pairings are chosen, the sum is\n\\[\n10(2+4+5+6)+(1+3+7+9)\n=170+20=190.\n\\]\nA valid arrangement exists: \\(29,41,53,67\\) are prime and use every supplied digit exactly once. Thus the required sum is \\(\\boxed{190}\\), choice E.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
