"batch": "B",
"number": 3,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The arithmetic mean of the nine numbers in the set \\(\\{9, 99, 999, 9999, \\ldots, 999999999\\}\\) is a \\(9\\)-digit number \\(M,\\) all of whose digits are distinct. Which digit does the number \\(M\\) not contain?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(6\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The mean is the sum of the nine numbers divided by \\(9\\). Dividing each term by \\(9\\) first makes the addition simpler:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nM&=\\frac99+\\frac{99}{9}+\\cdots+\\frac{999999999}{9}\\\\\n &=1+11+111+\\cdots+111111111.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEach quotient consists entirely of ones. In their sum, the ones column contains nine ones, the tens column contains eight ones, and each successive column contains one fewer. No column sum exceeds \\(9\\), so there are no carries. Reading the resulting digits from left to right gives\n\\[\nM=123456789.\n\\]\nIts digits are \\(1\\) through \\(9\\), so the missing digit is \\(0\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
