"batch": "B",
"number": 6,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For how many positive integers \\(n\\) is \\(n^2 - 3n + 2\\) a prime number?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "none",
"B": "one",
"C": "two",
"D": "more than two, but finitely many",
"E": "infinitely many"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "A prime number is greater than \\(1\\) and has no positive factors other than \\(1\\) and itself. Factoring the expression lets us determine when it has additional factors:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^2-3n+2&=n(n-1)-2(n-1)\\\\\n&=(n-1)(n-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor \\(n=1\\) or \\(n=2\\), the value is \\(0\\), which is not prime. For \\(n=3\\), the value is \\(2\\), which is prime. For every \\(n\\ge4\\), both factors are greater than \\(1\\), so their product is composite.\n\nThese cases cover all positive integers. Exactly \\(\\boxed{1}\\) value, namely \\(n=3\\), works, so the answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
