"year": 2002,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 14,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For all positive integers \\(n,\\) let \\(f(n) = \\log_{2002} n^2.\\) Let \\[N = f(11) + f(13) + f(14).\\] Which of the following relations is true?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(N \\gt 1\\)",
"B": "\\(N = 1\\)",
"C": "\\(1 \\lt N \\lt 2\\)",
"D": "\\(N = 2\\)",
"E": "\\(N \\gt 2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The useful observation is that \\(11\\cdot13\\cdot14=2002\\), the base of the logarithms. Combining the three logarithms therefore produces a power of their base. All arguments are positive, and the base \\(2002\\) is positive and different from \\(1\\), so the product rule applies:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN&=\\log_{2002}(11^2\\cdot13^2\\cdot14^2)\\\\\n&=\\log_{2002}\\bigl((11\\cdot13\\cdot14)^2\\bigr)\\\\\n&=\\log_{2002}(2002^2)=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus D gives the exact value. Since \\(2>1\\), choice A is also true. As displayed, the question has two true choices, A and D, although D is the intended keyed answer.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
