"number": 20,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(\\triangle XOY\\) be a right-angled triangle with \\(m\\angle XOY=90^\\circ.\\) Let \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) be the midpoints of legs \\(OX\\) and \\(OY,\\) respectively. Given that \\(XN=19\\) and \\(YM=22,\\) find \\(XY.\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(24\\)",
"B": "\\(26\\)",
"C": "\\(28\\)",
"D": "\\(30\\)",
"E": "\\(32\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "The Pythagorean theorem expresses \\(XY^2\\) as the sum of the two leg squares, so we can aim directly for that sum instead of finding each leg separately.\n\nLet \\(a=OX>0\\) and \\(b=OY>0\\). Because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are midpoints, \\(OM=a/2\\) and \\(ON=b/2\\). The triangles \\(XON\\) and \\(YOM\\) are right triangles at \\(O\\), so\n\\[\na^2+\\frac{b^2}{4}=19^2=361,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{a^2}{4}+b^2=22^2=484.\n\\]\nAdding makes the coefficients of \\(a^2\\) and \\(b^2\\) equal:\n\\[\n\\frac54(a^2+b^2)=845.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\na^2+b^2=\\frac45\\cdot845=676.\n\\]\nApplying the Pythagorean theorem to \\(XOY\\) gives \\(XY^2=676\\). A length is positive, so \\(XY=\\boxed{26}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2002B/solutions",
