"batch": "NA",
"number": 1,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A circle and two distinct lines are drawn on a sheet of paper. What is the largest possible number of points of intersection of these figures?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2\\)",
"B": "\\(3\\)",
"C": "\\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(5\\)",
"E": "\\(6\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Each line can intersect the circle at most twice, and two distinct lines can intersect each other at most once. Every intersection belongs to one of these three pairs of figures, so there can be at most\n\\[\n2+2+1=5\n\\]\ndistinct intersection points.\n\nTo attain five, draw two perpendicular lines through the circle's center. They meet the circle at four different points and meet each other at the center, giving one additional point. Thus the largest possible number is \\(\\boxed{5}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
