"year": 2002,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 2,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many different combinations of \\(\\$5\\) bills and \\(\\$2\\) bills can be used to make a total of \\(\\$17?\\) Order does not matter in this problem.",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2\\)",
"B": "\\(3\\)",
"C": "\\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(5\\)",
"E": "\\(6\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The two-dollar bills contribute an even amount. To reach the odd total \\(17\\), the five-dollar bills must contribute an odd amount, so their number must be odd.\n\nFour five-dollar bills already exceed \\(17\\), leaving only one or three five-dollar bills as possibilities:\n\nWith one five-dollar bill, the remaining \\(17-5=12\\) dollars require six two-dollar bills.\n\nWith three five-dollar bills, the remaining \\(17-15=2\\) dollars require one two-dollar bill.\n\nThese exhaust the possible numbers of five-dollar bills, and each fixes the number of two-dollar bills. Since order does not matter, each is counted once. There are \\(\\boxed{2}\\) combinations, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
