"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "What is the units digit of \\(13^{2003}?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(3\\)",
"C": "\\(7\\)",
"D": "\\(8\\)",
"E": "\\(9\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "The units digit of a product depends only on the units digits of its factors, because all other place-value contributions are multiples of \\(10\\). Thus multiplying by \\(13\\) changes the units digit just as multiplying by \\(3\\) does.\n\nStarting with \\(13^1\\), the successive units digits are\n\\[\n3,\\quad9,\\quad7,\\quad1.\n\\]\nThe next multiplication gives units digit \\(3\\) again. Since each next digit is determined by the preceding digit, the same four-digit cycle repeats indefinitely.\n\nNow\n\\[\n2003=4\\cdot500+3.\n\\]\nAfter \\(500\\) complete cycles, the exponent is three positions into the next cycle. The third digit is \\(\\boxed{7}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
