"question": "A base-\\(10\\) three-digit number \\(n\\) is selected at random. Which of the following is closest to the probability that the base-\\(9\\) representation and the base-\\(11\\) representation of \\(n\\) are both three-digit numerals?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0.3\\)",
"B": "\\(0.4\\)",
"C": "\\(0.5\\)",
"D": "\\(0.6\\)",
"E": "\\(0.7\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "A three-digit numeral in base \\(b\\) starts at \\(100_b=b^2\\) and ends just before \\(1000_b=b^3\\). Thus it represents exactly the integers satisfying \\(b^2\\le n<b^3\\).\n\nFor base \\(9\\), this requires\n\\[\n81\\le n\\le728.\n\\]\nFor base \\(11\\), it requires\n\\[\n121\\le n\\le1330.\n\\]\nThe original choice also satisfies \\(100\\le n\\le999\\). All three requirements therefore hold exactly when\n\\[\n121\\le n\\le728.\n\\]\nIncluding both endpoints gives \\(728-121+1=608\\) favorable integers. There are \\(999-100+1=900\\) equally likely choices in total, so the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{608}{900}\\approx0.676.\n\\]\nThe closest listed value is \\(0.7\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
