"year": 2003,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "There are \\(100\\) players in a singles tennis tournament. The tournament is single elimination, meaning that a player who loses a match is eliminated. In the first round, the strongest \\(28\\) players are given a bye, and the remaining \\(72\\) players are paired off to play. After each round, the remaining players play in the next round. The match continues until only one player remains unbeaten. The total number of matches played is",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "a prime number",
"B": "divisible by \\(2\\)",
"C": "divisible by \\(5\\)",
"D": "divisible by \\(7\\)",
"E": "divisible by \\(11\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Each match has exactly one loser, and that player is immediately eliminated. Thus no player can be the loser of two matches. Conversely, every player except the unbeaten champion must lose one match.\n\nPairing each match with its loser therefore counts every match exactly once. Since \\(100-1=99\\) players are eliminated, there are \\(99\\) matches. A bye eliminates nobody and does not affect this count.\n\nBecause \\(99=9\\cdot11\\), the total is divisible by \\(11\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2003B/solutions",
