"id": "2003_AMC_12A_Q18",
"bank": "amc",
"responseType": "choice",
"domain": [],
"skill": [],
"year": 2003,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 18,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(n\\) be a \\(5\\)-digit number, and let \\(q\\) and \\(r\\) be the quotient and remainder, respectively, when \\(n\\) is divided by \\(100.\\) For how many values of \\(n\\) is \\(q + r\\) divisible by \\(11?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(8180\\)",
"B": "\\(8181\\)",
"C": "\\(8182\\)",
"D": "\\(9000\\)",
"E": "\\(9090\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Method 1: Method 1: Count five-digit multiples\n\nThe quotient-remainder relation is\n\\[\nn=100q+r,\n\\qquad 100\\le q\\le999,\n\\qquad 0\\le r\\le99.\n\\]\nTo connect the condition on \\(q+r\\) directly to \\(n\\), subtract:\n\\[\nn-(q+r)=99q=11(9q).\n\\]\nTheir difference is a multiple of \\(11\\), so \\(q+r\\) is divisible by \\(11\\) if and only if \\(n\\) is divisible by \\(11\\).\n\nThe smallest five-digit multiple is \\(10010=11\\cdot910\\), since \\(11\\cdot909=9999\\). The largest is \\(99990=11\\cdot9090\\), since \\(11\\cdot9091=100001\\). Thus every qualifying number has the form \\(11k\\) for exactly one integer \\(k\\) from \\(910\\) through \\(9090\\). Every such multiplier gives a qualifying number, and different multipliers give different numbers. The count is therefore\n\\[\n9090-910+1=\\boxed{8181}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice B.\n\nMethod 2: Method 2: Count remainders in blocks\n\nEach five-digit number corresponds uniquely to a pair of integers\n\\[\n100\\le q\\le999,\n\\qquad 0\\le r\\le99,\n\\]\nthrough \\(n=100q+r\\). Conversely, every pair in these ranges gives a five-digit number. We can therefore count qualifying pairs without omission or repetition.\n\nFix any of the \\(900\\) possible quotients \\(q\\). Split the remainders from \\(0\\) through \\(98\\) into nine blocks:\n\\[\n0\\text{ through }10,\\quad\n11\\text{ through }21,\\quad\\ldots,\\quad\n88\\text{ through }98.\n\\]\nWithin each block, the values of \\(q+r\\) are \\(11\\) consecutive integers, so exactly one is divisible by \\(11\\). Thus these blocks give exactly nine successful remainders for every quotient, accounting for \\(900\\cdot9\\) numbers.\n\nThe only remaining remainder is \\(r=99\\). Since \\(99\\) is divisible by \\(11\\), it works exactly when \\(q\\) is divisible by \\(11\\). Such quotients are \\(11k\\) with \\(10\\le k\\le90\\), giving \\(90-10+1=81\\) extra numbers. These have remainder \\(99\\), so none was counted in the earlier blocks. The total is\n\\[\n900\\cdot9+81=\\boxed{8181},\n\\]\nchoice B.",
