"number": 25,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(f(x) = \\sqrt{ax^2 + bx}.\\) For how many real values of \\(a\\) is there at least one positive value of \\(b\\) for which the domain of \\(f\\) and the range of \\(f\\) are the same set?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\)",
"D": "\\(3\\)",
"E": "infinitely many"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Method 1: Find the domain and the quadratic maximum\n\nThe square root makes every output nonnegative, while an input belongs to the domain exactly when \\(ax^2+bx\\ge0\\). This lets us separate the possibilities according to the sign of \\(a\\), always keeping \\(b>0\\).\n\nIf \\(a>0\\), the negative number \\(x=-b/a\\) is in the domain because its radicand is zero. The range contains no negative numbers, so the domain and range cannot be equal.\n\nIf \\(a=0\\), then \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{bx}\\) has domain \\([0,\\infty)\\). Its range is also \\([0,\\infty)\\): every \\(y\\ge0\\) is obtained by taking \\(x=y^2/b\\). Thus \\(a=0\\) works.\n\nFor \\(a<0\\), write \\(c=-a>0\\). The radicand is \\(x(b-cx)\\), which is nonnegative exactly when\n\\[\n0\\le x\\le\\frac bc.\n\\]\nTo find all outputs on this interval, complete the square:\n\\[\nbx-cx^2\n=\\frac{b^2}{4c}\n-c\\left(x-\\frac{b}{2c}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nOn the domain, the squared term takes every value from zero to \\(b^2/(4c^2)\\). Hence the radicand takes every value from zero to \\(b^2/(4c)\\), and the range of \\(f\\) is\n\\[\n\\left[0,\\frac{b}{2\\sqrt c}\\right].\n\\]\nThe domain and range are closed intervals starting at zero, so they are equal exactly when their upper endpoints agree:\n\\[\n\\frac bc=\\frac{b}{2\\sqrt c}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nc=2\\sqrt c.\n\\]\nSince \\(b>0\\) and \\(c>0\\), the cancellations are valid. Dividing the last equation by \\(\\sqrt c\\) gives \\(\\sqrt c=2\\), so \\(c=4\\) and \\(a=-4\\). In that case both sets are \\([0,b/4]\\) for every \\(b>0\\).\n\nAll three sign cases have been covered. Exactly \\(a=0\\) and \\(a=-4\\) work, giving \\(\\boxed{2}\\) values, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Test which outputs can occur\n\nEvery output of a square root is nonnegative. If \\(a>0\\), the negative input \\(-b/a\\) is in the domain and gives output zero, so the domain cannot equal the range. If \\(a=0\\), both sets are \\([0,\\infty)\\), since \\(f(x)=\\sqrt{bx}\\) and every \\(y\\ge0\\) is obtained at \\(x=y^2/b\\).\n\nIt remains to consider \\(a<0\\). Put \\(c=-a>0\\). Factoring the radicand as \\(x(b-cx)\\) gives the domain\n\\[\nD=\\left[0,\\frac bc\\right].\n\\]\nInstead of finding a vertex, test whether a proposed output \\(y\\ge0\\) is attainable. The equation \\(f(x)=y\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\ncx^2-bx+y^2=0.\n\\]\nSquaring introduces no extraneous outputs here: \\(y\\) is nonnegative, and any real solution has radicand \\(y^2\\ge0\\), so it automatically belongs to the domain and satisfies the original equation.\n\nBecause \\(c>0\\), this is a genuine quadratic in \\(x\\). It has a real solution exactly when its discriminant is nonnegative:\n\\[\nb^2-4cy^2\\ge0.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(y\\ge0\\) and \\(b>0\\), this says\n\\[\n0\\le y\\le\\frac{b}{2\\sqrt c}.\n\\]\nThus the range is exactly \\([0,b/(2\\sqrt c)]\\), including both endpoints. It equals \\(D\\) precisely when\n\\[\n\\frac bc=\\frac{b}{2\\sqrt c},\n\\]\nwhich, using \\(b,c>0\\), gives \\(\\sqrt c=2\\) and \\(c=4\\). Therefore \\(a=-4\\) works, and no other negative \\(a\\) does.\n\nIncluding \\(a=0\\), there are exactly \\(\\boxed{2}\\) values, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2003A/solutions",
