"year": 2003,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 16,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Ali, Bonnie, Carlo and Dianna are going to drive together to a nearby theme park. The car they are using has four seats: one driver’s seat, one front passenger seat and two back seats. Bonnie and Carlo are the only two who can drive the car. How many possible seating arrangements are there?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(2\\)",
"B": "\\(4\\)",
"C": "\\(6\\)",
"D": "\\(12\\)",
"E": "\\(24\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Count by filling the driver’s seat first, because it has a restriction. There are \\(2\\) choices: Bonnie or Carlo. Once the driver is chosen, any of the remaining \\(3\\) people can take the front passenger seat. Then there are \\(2\\) choices for the left back seat and \\(1\\) for the right back seat. Thus the number of arrangements is\n\\[\n2\\cdot3\\cdot2\\cdot1=12.\n\\]\nEvery allowed arrangement is included exactly once, since it specifies one person for each of these four distinct seats. The answer is \\(\\boxed{12}\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2003/solutions",
