"year": 2004,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 6,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Bertha has \\(6\\) daughters and no sons. Some of her daughters have \\(6\\) daughters, and the rest have none. Bertha has a total of \\(30\\) daughters and granddaughters, and no great-granddaughters. How many of Bertha’s daughters and granddaughters have no daughters?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(22\\)",
"B": "\\(23\\)",
"C": "\\(24\\)",
"D": "\\(25\\)",
"E": "\\(26\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "First separate Bertha’s daughters from her granddaughters. Since she has \\(6\\) daughters, she has\n\\[\n30-6=24\n\\]\ngranddaughters. Each daughter who has daughters has exactly \\(6\\), so \\(24\\div6=4\\) of Bertha’s daughters have daughters. The remaining \\(6-4=2\\) have none.\n\nAll \\(24\\) granddaughters have no daughters, because any such daughter would be Bertha’s great-granddaughter. Thus the required count is\n\\[\n2+24=\\boxed{26},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2004A/solutions",
