"domain": [],
"skill": [],
"year": 2004,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A company sells peanut butter in cylindrical jars. Marketing research suggests that using wider jars will increase sales. If the diameter of the jars is increased by \\(25\\%\\) without altering the volume, by what percent must the height be decreased?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(10\\)",
"B": "\\(25\\)",
"C": "\\(36\\)",
"D": "\\(50\\)",
"E": "\\(60\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "A cylinder’s volume is its base area times its height, so a larger base requires a smaller height when the volume stays fixed.\n\nIncreasing the diameter by \\(25\\%\\) multiplies both the diameter and radius by \\(5/4\\). Since the circular base area is \\(\\pi r^2\\), its area is multiplied by\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac54\\right)^2=\\frac{25}{16}.\n\\]\nThe original dimensions are positive, so preserving the product of base area and height requires the height to be multiplied by the reciprocal, \\(16/25\\). The fraction of the original height removed is therefore\n\\[\n1-\\frac{16}{25}=\\frac9{25}=36\\%.\n\\]\nThe required decrease is \\(\\boxed{36\\%}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2004A/solutions",
