"year": 2004,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 18,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A sequence of three real numbers forms an arithmetic progression with a first term of \\(9.\\) If \\(2\\) is added to the second term and \\(20\\) is added to the third term, the three resulting numbers form a geometric progression. What is the smallest possible value for the third term of the geometric progression?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(4\\)",
"C": "\\(36\\)",
"D": "\\(49\\)",
"E": "\\(81\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The equal differences in the original progression let us describe all three terms with one number. Let \\(d\\) be its common difference. The original terms are\n\\[9,\\quad 9+d,\\quad 9+2d,\\]\nand the modified terms are\n\\[9,\\quad 11+d,\\quad 29+2d.\\]\nIf a geometric progression begins with \\(9\\) and has common ratio \\(r\\), its terms are \\(9,9r,9r^2\\). Thus its middle term squared equals \\(9\\) times its last term. Applying this necessary condition gives\n\\[(11+d)^2=9(29+2d).\\]\nExpanding and collecting terms yields\n\\[d^2+4d-140=0,\\]\nso\n\\[(d+14)(d-10)=0.\\]\nConsequently, \\(d=-14\\) or \\(d=10\\), and there are no other candidates.\n\nFor \\(d=-14\\), the modified terms are \\(9,-3,1\\), a geometric progression with ratio \\(-1/3\\). For \\(d=10\\), they are \\(9,21,49\\), a geometric progression with ratio \\(7/3\\). Both candidates therefore satisfy the original requirements. Negative common ratios are allowed, so the smallest possible third term is \\(\\boxed{1}\\), choice \\(\\text{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2004A/solutions",
