"batch": "B",
"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A standard six-sided die is rolled, and \\(P\\) is the product of the five numbers that are visible. What is the largest number that is certain to divide \\(P?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(6\\)",
"B": "\\(12\\)",
"C": "\\(24\\)",
"D": "\\(144\\)",
"E": "\\(720\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Only the bottom face is hidden. Since the product of all six face numbers is \\(1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5\\cdot6=720\\), a hidden number \\(h\\) gives \\(P=720/h\\). The hidden number can be any of \\(1,2,3,4,5,6\\), so the complete list of possible products is\n\\[\n720,\\ 360,\\ 240,\\ 180,\\ 144,\\ 120.\n\\]\nA number certain to divide \\(P\\) must divide every number on this list. All six are divisible by \\(12\\). To show that no larger common divisor works, consider just three of them:\n\\[\n\\gcd(144,120)=24,\\qquad \\gcd(24,180)=12.\n\\]\nThus every guaranteed divisor must divide \\(12\\), and \\(12\\) itself always works. The largest such number is \\(\\boxed{12}\\), choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
