"year": 2004,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 9,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A square has sides of length \\(10,\\) and a circle centered at one of its vertices has radius \\(10.\\) What is the area of the union of the regions enclosed by the square and the circle?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(200 + 25\\pi\\)",
"B": "\\(100 + 75\\pi\\)",
"C": "\\(75 + 100\\pi\\)",
"D": "\\(100 + 100\\pi\\)",
"E": "\\(100 + 125\\pi\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Let \\(O\\) be the square vertex at the circle's center. The two square sides meeting at \\(O\\) form a \\(90^\\circ\\) angle, and each has length equal to the circle's radius, \\(10\\).\n\nThe overlap is exactly the quarter disk inside this angle. To see why the entire quarter disk fits, note that each of the square's opposite sides is \\(10\\) units from \\(O\\). A point at most \\(10\\) units from \\(O\\) cannot extend past either opposite side. The rest of the disk lies outside the angle containing the square.\n\nThe square has area \\(10^2=100\\), the disk has area \\(\\pi\\cdot10^2=100\\pi\\), and their overlap has area \\(25\\pi\\). Adding the two areas counts the overlap twice, so subtract it once:\n\\[\n100+100\\pi-25\\pi=\\boxed{100+75\\pi}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\text{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2004B/solutions",
