"year": 2004,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A grocer makes a display of cans in which the top row has one can and each lower row has two more cans than the row above it. If the display contains \\(100\\) cans, how many rows does it contain?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(5\\)",
"B": "\\(8\\)",
"C": "\\(9\\)",
"D": "\\(10\\)",
"E": "\\(11\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The row sizes are \\(1,3,5,\\ldots\\). If there are \\(n\\) rows, where \\(n\\) is a positive integer, the bottom row has \\(1+2(n-1)=2n-1\\) cans. Let \\(S\\) be the total number of cans:\n\\[\nS=1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1).\n\\]\nWrite the same sum backward and add corresponding terms. Every pair sums to \\(2n\\): as one term increases by \\(2\\), its partner decreases by \\(2\\). There are \\(n\\) pairs, so\n\\[\n2S=n(2n),\\qquad S=n^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(n^2=100\\). Since the number of rows is positive, \\(n=\\boxed{10}\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
