"year": 2004,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 2,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "On the AMC \\(12,\\) each correct answer is worth \\(6\\) points, each incorrect answer is worth \\(0\\) points, and each problem left unanswered is worth \\(2.5\\) points. If Charlyn leaves \\(8\\) of the \\(25\\) problems unanswered, how many of the remaining problems must she answer correctly in order to score at least \\(100?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(11\\)",
"B": "\\(13\\)",
"C": "\\(14\\)",
"D": "\\(16\\)",
"E": "\\(17\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "The \\(8\\) unanswered problems already earn \\(8\\cdot2.5=20\\) points. Let \\(c\\) be the number of correct answers among the remaining \\(25-8=17\\) problems, so \\(c\\) is an integer with \\(0\\le c\\le17\\). Incorrect answers add no points, so the requirement is\n\\[\n6c+20\\ge100.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(20\\) and dividing by \\(6\\) gives\n\\[\nc\\ge\\frac{80}{6}=13\\frac13.\n\\]\nThe smallest allowed integer is \\(14\\), which is feasible among the \\(17\\) answered problems. Indeed, \\(13\\) correct answers give \\(98\\) points, while \\(14\\) give \\(104\\). She must answer at least \\(14\\) correctly, choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
