"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(U = 2 \\cdot 2004^{2005},\\) \\(V = 2004^{2005},\\) \\(W = 2003 \\cdot 2004^{2004},\\) \\(X = 2 \\cdot 2004^{2004},\\) \\(Y = 2004^{2004}\\) and \\(Z = 2004^{2003}.\\) Which of the following is largest?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(U - V\\)",
"B": "\\(V - W\\)",
"C": "\\(W - X\\)",
"D": "\\(X - Y\\)",
"E": "\\(Y - Z\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Method 1: Compare multiples of one positive power\n\nThe large powers become manageable if we express every choice using the same positive factor. Let \\(T=2004^{2004}>0\\). Then \\(2004^{2005}=2004T\\) and \\(2004^{2003}=T/2004\\), so\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU-V&=2004T,\\\\\nV-W&=(2004-2003)T=T,\\\\\nW-X&=(2003-2)T=2001T,\\\\\nX-Y&=(2-1)T=T,\\\\\nY-Z&=\\left(1-\\frac1{2004}\\right)T.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBecause \\(T\\) is positive, comparing these quantities is the same as comparing their coefficients. The coefficient \\(2004\\) exceeds \\(2001\\), \\(1\\), and \\(1-1/2004<1\\). Therefore \\(U-V\\) is largest, choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Bound every other choice by V\n\nSince \\(U=2V\\), the first choice is \\(U-V=V\\). It is enough to show that each other choice is smaller than \\(V\\).\n\nAll six quantities are positive. Also,\n\\[\nW=2003\\cdot2004^{2004}\n<2004\\cdot2004^{2004}=V,\n\\]\nand \\(Y=2004^{2004}<V\\). Using these facts and \\(X=2Y\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV-W&<V,\\\\\nW-X&<W<V,\\\\\nX-Y&=Y<V,\\\\\nY-Z&<Y<V.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus every other choice is less than \\(U-V\\). The largest is \\(U-V\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2004A/solutions",
