"year": 2004,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The numbers \\(-2,\\) \\(4,\\) \\(6,\\) \\(9\\) and \\(12\\) are rearranged according to these rules:\n\n\\(1.\\) The largest isn’t first, but it is in one of the first three places.\n\n\\(2.\\) The smallest isn’t last, but it is in one of the last three places.\n\n\\(3.\\) The median isn’t first or last.\n\nWhat is the average of the first and last numbers?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(3.5\\)",
"B": "\\(5\\)",
"C": "\\(6.5\\)",
"D": "\\(7.5\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "The numbers are already listed in increasing order, so the largest is \\(12\\), the smallest is \\(-2\\), and the median is the middle number, \\(6\\).\n\nRule 1 puts \\(12\\) in position \\(2\\) or \\(3\\). Rule 2 puts \\(-2\\) in position \\(3\\) or \\(4\\). Rule 3 also keeps \\(6\\) away from both ends. These three distinct numbers therefore fill the three interior positions, leaving \\(4\\) and \\(9\\) as the first and last numbers.\n\nThe restrictions are consistent: \\(4,12,6,-2,9\\) satisfies all three rules. Regardless of the order of the endpoints, their average is\n\\[\n\\frac{4+9}{2}=6.5.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(6.5\\), choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2004/solutions",
