"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Method 1: Count by the first friend's share\n\nAlthough the pencils are identical, the friends are distinct, so a distribution is determined by how many pencils each friend receives.\n\nThe first friend can receive only \\(1,2,3,\\) or \\(4\\) pencils, because at least two pencils must remain for the other friends. These cases leave \\(5,4,3,\\) or \\(2\\) pencils, respectively. The second friend can receive any positive number less than the remaining total, giving \\(4,3,2,\\) or \\(1\\) choices, respectively. Once the second friend's share is chosen, the third friend's share is determined.\n\nEvery allowed distribution occurs in exactly one of these cases and is counted once. Thus there are\n\\[\n4+3+2+1=10\n\\]\nways, choice D.\n\nMethod 2: Divide a row into three groups\n\nRepresent the six pencils by six marks in a row. There are five gaps between consecutive marks. Choosing two different gaps divides the row into three nonempty groups, which give the pencil counts for the first, second, and third friends in that order.\n\nEvery allowed distribution gives exactly one pair of dividing gaps: one after the first friend's pencils and one after the first two friends' pencils. Conversely, every pair of dividing gaps gives an allowed distribution. Thus counting gap pairs counts the distributions without omission or repetition.\n\nThere are five choices for one gap and four for another. This counts each pair twice, once in each order, so the number of distributions is\n\\[\n\\frac{5\\cdot4}{2}=10.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(10\\), choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2004/solutions",
"figureSources": {},
"editorialNote": "",
