"number": 20,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Two-thirds of the people in a room are seated in three-fourths of the chairs. The rest of the people are standing. If there are \\(6\\) empty chairs, how many people are in the room?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(12\\)",
"B": "\\(18\\)",
"C": "\\(24\\)",
"D": "\\(27\\)",
"E": "\\(36\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Since \\(\\frac34\\) of the chairs are occupied, the \\(6\\) empty chairs are the remaining \\(\\frac14\\). Each quarter of the chairs therefore contains \\(6\\) chairs, so the three occupied quarters seat\n\\[\n3\\cdot6=18\n\\]\npeople.\n\nThese \\(18\\) seated people make up \\(\\frac23\\) of all the people. One-third of the people is therefore \\(18\\div2=9\\), and all three thirds contain\n\\[\n3\\cdot9=27\n\\]\npeople. The answer is \\(27\\), choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
