"batch": "A",
"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A wooden cube \\(n\\) units on a side is painted red on all six faces and then cut into \\(n^3\\) unit cubes. Exactly one-fourth of the total number of faces of the unit cubes are red. What is \\(n?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(3\\)",
"B": "\\(4\\)",
"C": "\\(5\\)",
"D": "\\(6\\)",
"E": "\\(7\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Count faces of the small cubes separately, including the faces where two cubes touch. Each of the \\(n^3\\) small cubes has six faces, so there are \\(6n^3\\) faces altogether.\n\nEach original face of the large cube splits into \\(n^2\\) red unit faces. All newly exposed cut faces are unpainted, so the number of red faces is \\(6n^2\\). Since \\(n\\) is a positive integer, the red fraction is\n\\[\n\\frac{6n^2}{6n^3}=\\frac1n.\n\\]\nThe given fraction is \\(\\frac14\\), so \\(n=4\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{4}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
