"year": 2005,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 13,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many positive integers \\(n\\) satisfy the following condition:\n\n\\[ (130n)^{50} \\gt n^{100} \\gt 2^{200}? \\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(7\\)",
"C": "\\(12\\)",
"D": "\\(65\\)",
"E": "\\(125\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The exponents are all multiples of \\(50\\), so rewrite the three expressions as fiftieth powers:\n\\[\n(130n)^{50}>(n^2)^{50}>16^{50}.\n\\]\nFor positive bases, taking the same positive whole-number power preserves their order. Thus the original condition is equivalent to\n\\[\n130n>n^2>16.\n\\]\nBecause \\(n>0\\), dividing the first inequality by \\(n\\) gives \\(n<130\\). The second inequality gives \\(n>4\\).\n\nThese steps are reversible for positive \\(n\\), so precisely the integers \\(5,6,\\ldots,129\\) work. The endpoints \\(4\\) and \\(130\\) are excluded because the inequalities are strict. There are\n\\[\n129-5+1=125\n\\]\nsuch integers. The answer is \\(\\boxed{125}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
