"year": 2005,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 14,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many three-digit numbers satisfy the property that the middle digit is the average of the first and the last digits?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(41\\)",
"B": "\\(42\\)",
"C": "\\(43\\)",
"D": "\\(44\\)",
"E": "\\(45\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Choosing the first and last digits determines the middle digit. Let these digits be \\(a\\) and \\(c\\), where \\(1\\le a\\le9\\) and \\(0\\le c\\le9\\). The middle digit must be\n\\[\n\\frac{a+c}{2}.\n\\]\nThis is an integer exactly when \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) are both odd or both even. Since an average lies between the two digits, this integer is automatically a valid digit.\n\nFor odd outside digits, there are five choices for each digit, giving \\(5\\cdot5=25\\) numbers. For even outside digits, the first digit has four choices, \\(2,4,6,8\\), and the last has five choices, including \\(0\\). This gives \\(4\\cdot5=20\\) numbers.\n\nThe two cases are disjoint and cover every valid pair. Each pair produces exactly one three-digit number, so the total is\n\\[\n25+20=\\boxed{45},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
