"year": 2005,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A circle is inscribed in a square, then a square is inscribed in this circle, and finally, a circle is inscribed in this square. What is the ratio of the area of the smaller circle to the area of the larger square?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{16}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Let \\(s>0\\) be the side length of the larger square and \\(t>0\\) the side length of the smaller square. The larger circle has diameter \\(s\\). A diagonal of the smaller square passes through that circle's center and joins two points on the circle, so its length is also \\(s\\).\n\nThe diagonal splits the smaller square into right triangles with legs \\(t\\) and \\(t\\). By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\nt^2+t^2=s^2,\n\\qquad t^2=\\frac{s^2}{2}.\n\\]\nThe smaller circle has diameter \\(t\\), hence radius \\(t/2\\). Therefore the requested area ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi(t/2)^2}{s^2}\n=\\frac{\\pi t^2}{4s^2}\n=\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{8}},\n\\]\nwhich is choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2005B/solutions",
