"number": 16,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The quadratic equation \\(x^2 + mx + n = 0\\) has roots that are twice those of \\(x^2 + px + m = 0,\\) and none of \\(m,\\) \\(n,\\) and \\(p\\) is zero. What is the value of \\(\\dfrac{n}{p}?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(4\\)",
"D": "\\(8\\)",
"E": "\\(16\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Doubling both roots doubles their sum and quadruples their product. This lets us compare the coefficients without finding the roots.\n\nLet \\(r,s\\) be the roots of \\(x^2+px+m=0\\), listing a repeated root twice if necessary. Expanding\n\\[\n(x-r)(x-s)=x^2-(r+s)x+rs\n\\]\nshows that \\(r+s=-p\\) and \\(rs=m\\). The first equation has roots \\(2r,2s\\), so its coefficients give\n\\[\n-m=2r+2s=-2p,\n\\qquad n=(2r)(2s)=4m.\n\\]\nThus \\(m=2p\\) and \\(n=8p\\). Since \\(p\\ne0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac np=8.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(8\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2005B/solutions",
