"year": 2005,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A store normally sells windows at \\(\\$100\\) each. This week the store is offering one free window for each purchase of four. Dave needs seven windows and Doug needs eight windows. How many dollars will they save if they purchase the windows together rather than separately?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(100\\)",
"B": "\\(200\\)",
"C": "\\(300\\)",
"D": "\\(400\\)",
"E": "\\(500\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The offer gives a group of five windows for the price of four. Count complete groups of five, then any extra windows.\n\nDave needs \\(7=5+2\\) windows, so he pays for \\(4+2=6\\). Doug needs \\(8=5+3\\) windows, so he pays for \\(4+3=7\\). Separately, their total cost is\n\\[\n(6+7)\\cdot\\$100=\\$1300.\n\\]\nTogether, they need \\(7+8=15\\) windows, exactly three groups of five. They pay for \\(3\\cdot4=12\\) windows, costing \\(\\$1200\\).\n\nThese payments are minimal: paying for one fewer window in the three cases, namely \\(5\\), \\(6\\), or \\(11\\), provides at most \\(6\\), \\(7\\), or \\(13\\) windows, respectively, after the free windows are added.\n\nTheir saving is \\(\\$1300-\\$1200=\\boxed{\\$100}\\), choice \\(\\text{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2005A/solutions",
