"choices": {
"A": "\\(4\\)",
"B": "\\(5\\)",
"C": "\\(6\\)",
"D": "\\(7\\)",
"E": "\\(8\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Distance equals rate times time, so both the rate comparison and the riding-time comparison affect the distances. Josh rides at \\(\\frac45\\) of Mike’s rate for twice as long. His distance is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac45\\cdot2=\\frac85\n\\]\nof Mike’s distance. Thus Josh’s and Mike’s distances are in the ratio \\(8:5\\).\n\nBecause they ride toward each other and meet between their houses, their distances add to \\(13\\) miles. The ratio has \\(8+5=13\\) equal parts, so each part represents \\(1\\) mile. Mike’s five parts give \\(\\boxed{5\\text{ miles}}\\), choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
