"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Method 1: Use the discriminant\n\nCombine the linear terms to write the equation as\n\\[4x^2+(a+8)x+9=0.\\]\nThe leading coefficient is always \\(4\\), so the equation remains quadratic for every \\(a\\). A quadratic has exactly one distinct real solution when its discriminant is zero; a positive discriminant gives two distinct real solutions, and a negative one gives none. Therefore\n\\[(a+8)^2-4\\cdot4\\cdot9=0,\\]\nso \\((a+8)^2=144\\). Taking both square roots gives \\(a+8=12\\) or \\(a+8=-12\\), hence \\(a=4\\) or \\(a=-20\\).\n\nFor these values, the original equation becomes respectively \\((2x+3)^2=0\\) or \\((2x-3)^2=0\\), confirming one real solution in each case. The requested sum is \\(4+(-20)=-16\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Complete the square\n\nA square equation makes the number of real solutions easy to see. First combine \\(ax+8x=(a+8)x\\). To complete the square without fractions, multiply the equation by \\(16\\), move the constant term to the right, and add \\((a+8)^2\\) to both sides. This gives\n\\[64x^2+16(a+8)x+(a+8)^2=(a+8)^2-144,\\]\nor\n\\[(8x+a+8)^2=(a+8)^2-144.\\]\nThese transformations preserve all solutions. As \\(x\\) varies over the real numbers, \\(8x+a+8\\) takes every real value exactly once. Thus a positive right-hand side gives two solutions, zero gives one, and a negative right-hand side gives none.\n\nConsequently, exactly one solution occurs precisely when \\((a+8)^2=144\\). Therefore \\(a+8=12\\) or \\(-12\\), giving \\(a=4\\) or \\(-20\\). Their sum is \\(-16\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2005A/solutions",
