"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Bill walks \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) mile south, then \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) mile east, and finally \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) mile south. How many miles is he, in a direct line, from his starting point?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(1 \\frac{1}{4}\\)",
"C": "\\(1 \\frac{1}{2}\\)",
"D": "\\(1 \\frac{3}{4}\\)",
"E": "\\(2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "The two southward walks together put Bill \\(\\frac12+\\frac12=1\\) mile south of his start. He is also \\(\\frac34\\) mile east. South and east are perpendicular, so these two distances form the legs of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the direct distance sought.\n\nMeasure lengths in units of \\(\\frac14\\) mile. The legs then have lengths \\(4\\) and \\(3\\). If \\(c>0\\) is the direct distance in these units, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nc^2=4^2+3^2=16+9=25.\n\\]\nTaking the positive square root gives \\(c=5\\). Thus the distance is\n\\[\n5\\times\\frac14=\\frac54=\\boxed{1\\frac14}\\text{ miles},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
