"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Suppose \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are positive odd integers. Which of the following must also be an odd integer?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(m + 3n\\)",
"B": "\\(3m - n\\)",
"C": "\\(3m^2 + 3n^2\\)",
"D": "\\((nm + 3)^2\\)",
"E": "\\(3mn\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Two odd integers add or subtract to an even integer: their unpaired units either form a pair or cancel. A product of odd integers is odd, since pairing the odd-sized groups leaves one odd-sized group. A product with an even factor is even.\n\nApply these rules to each choice:\n\n- A: \\(3n\\) is odd, so \\(m+3n\\) is even.\n- B: \\(3m\\) is odd, so \\(3m-n\\) is even.\n- C: Both squares \\(m^2\\) and \\(n^2\\) are odd. Thus \\(3m^2+3n^2\\) is the sum of two odd integers and is even.\n- D: \\(nm\\) is odd, so \\(nm+3\\) is even. Its square is therefore even.\n- E: \\(3\\), \\(m\\), and \\(n\\) are all odd, so \\(3mn\\) is odd.\n\nTherefore the expression that must be odd is \\(\\boxed{3mn}\\), choice \\(\\text{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
