"year": 2005,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 12,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Big Al, the ape, ate \\(100\\) bananas from May \\(1\\) through May \\(5.\\) Each day he ate six more bananas than on the previous day. How many bananas did Big Al eat on May \\(5?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(20\\)",
"B": "\\(22\\)",
"C": "\\(30\\)",
"D": "\\(32\\)",
"E": "\\(34\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Method 1: Balance around the middle day\n\nBecause the count rises by \\(6\\) each day, the earlier and later days balance around May \\(3\\). May \\(1\\) has \\(12\\) fewer bananas than May \\(3\\), while May \\(5\\) has \\(12\\) more. Similarly, May \\(2\\) has \\(6\\) fewer and May \\(4\\) has \\(6\\) more.\n\nThese differences cancel in the five-day total, so the May \\(3\\) count equals the average:\n\\[\n100\\div5=20.\n\\]\nMay \\(5\\) is two daily increases later, giving\n\\[\n20+2\\times6=\\boxed{32}\n\\]\nbananas, choice \\(\\text{D}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Remove the increases above the first day's amount\n\nCompared with May \\(1\\), the extra bananas eaten on the five days are \\(0,6,12,18,24\\). These extras total\n\\[\n0+6+12+18+24=60.\n\\]\nRemoving them from the \\(100\\) bananas leaves \\(100-60=40\\). This remainder consists of five copies of the May \\(1\\) amount, so Big Al ate \\(40\\div5=8\\) bananas on May \\(1\\).\n\nMay \\(5\\) comes after four increases of \\(6\\), so its count is\n\\[\n8+4\\times6=\\boxed{32}\n\\]\nbananas, choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2005/solutions",
