"number": 16,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A five-legged Martian has a drawer full of socks, each of which is red, white or blue, and there are at least five socks of each color. The Martian pulls out one sock at a time without looking. How many socks must the Martian remove from the drawer to be certain there will be \\(5\\) socks of the same color?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(6\\)",
"B": "\\(9\\)",
"C": "\\(12\\)",
"D": "\\(13\\)",
"E": "\\(15\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "To avoid having five socks of one color, the Martian can have at most four red, four white, and four blue socks. Thus any collection without five matching socks contains at most\n\\[\n4+4+4=12\n\\]\nsocks. A thirteenth sock must make the total for some color at least five.\n\nTwelve socks do not guarantee success: the Martian could draw exactly four of each color. This is possible because the drawer contains at least five of each. Therefore \\(13\\) socks are both sufficient and necessary, giving \\(\\boxed{13}\\), choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
