"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many three-digit numbers are divisible by \\(13?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(7\\)",
"B": "\\(67\\)",
"C": "\\(69\\)",
"D": "\\(76\\)",
"E": "\\(77\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Every positive number divisible by \\(13\\) can be written as \\(13k\\), where \\(k\\) is a positive integer. Finding the first and last eligible multipliers lets us count all the numbers without listing them.\n\nAt the lower end,\n\\[\n13\\cdot7=91,\\qquad 13\\cdot8=104,\n\\]\nso \\(8\\) is the first multiplier producing a three-digit number. At the upper end,\n\\[\n13\\cdot76=988,\\qquad 13\\cdot77=1001,\n\\]\nso \\(76\\) is the last.\n\nEvery integer multiplier from \\(8\\) through \\(76\\) produces a different three-digit multiple, and every three-digit multiple occurs this way. Including both endpoints, the count is\n\\[\n76-8+1=\\boxed{69},\n\\]\nchoice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
