"year": 2006,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 18,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Let \\(a_1,\\) \\(a_2,\\) \\(\\ldots\\) be a sequence for which \\(a_1=2,\\) \\(a_2=3,\\) and \\(a_n=\\dfrac{a_{n-1}}{a_{n-2}}\\) for each positive integer \\(n\\ge 3.\\) What is \\(a_{2006}?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)",
"D": "\\(2\\)",
"E": "\\(3\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Because the requested index is large, first compute a few terms to look for repetition. The initial terms are positive, and each new term is a quotient of positive terms, so all terms remain positive and no denominator is zero.\n\nSuccessive use of the recurrence gives the first eight terms:\n\\[\n2,\\quad 3,\\quad \\frac32,\\quad \\frac12,\\quad \\frac13,\\quad \\frac23,\\quad 2,\\quad 3.\n\\]\nThus \\((a_7,a_8)=(a_1,a_2)\\). Each next term is determined by the preceding two terms, so repeating this pair forces every subsequent term to repeat as well. The sequence therefore repeats in blocks of six terms.\n\nSince\n\\[\n2006=6\\cdot334+2,\n\\]\nthe requested term occupies the second position in its block. Hence \\(a_{2006}=a_2=\\boxed{3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2006B/solutions",
