"year": 2006,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "B",
"number": 14,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Elmo makes \\(N\\) sandwiches for a fundraiser. For each sandwich he uses \\(B\\) globs of peanut butter at \\(4\\)¢ per glob and \\(J\\) blobs of jam at \\(5\\)¢ per blob. The cost of the peanut butter and jam to make all the sandwiches is \\(\\$2.53.\\) Assume that \\(B,\\) \\(J,\\) and \\(N\\) are positive integers with \\(N \\gt 1.\\) What is the cost of the jam Elmo uses to make the sandwiches?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\$1.05\\)",
"B": "\\(\\$1.25\\)",
"C": "\\(\\$1.45\\)",
"D": "\\(\\$1.65\\)",
"E": "\\(\\$1.85\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Method 1: Factor the total cost\n\nWork in cents so that all quantities are integers. Each sandwich costs \\(4B+5J\\) cents, giving\n\\[\nN(4B+5J)=253=11\\cdot23.\n\\]\nSince \\(11\\) and \\(23\\) are prime, the positive divisors of \\(253\\) are \\(1,11,23,253\\). We know \\(N>1\\), and \\(B,J\\ge1\\) makes each sandwich cost at least \\(9\\) cents. These conditions leave only \\(N=11\\) or \\(N=23\\).\n\nIf \\(N=23\\), then \\(4B+5J=11\\). Positivity forces \\(J=1\\), but that leaves \\(4B=6\\), impossible for an integer \\(B\\).\n\nIf \\(N=11\\), then \\(4B+5J=23\\). Since \\(4B\\ge4\\), we have \\(5J\\le19\\), so \\(J\\) is \\(1,2\\), or \\(3\\). The corresponding values of \\(4B\\) are \\(18,13\\), and \\(8\\). Only \\(8\\) is divisible by \\(4\\), giving \\(B=2\\) and \\(J=3\\).\n\nThese exhaust the possible sandwich counts and ingredient amounts. The total jam cost is\n\\[\n5NJ=5\\cdot11\\cdot3=165\\text{ cents},\n\\]\nor \\(\\boxed{\\$1.65}\\), choice (D).\n\nMethod 2: Test common sandwich counts\n\nEvery sandwich uses the same number of globs and blobs. Therefore the total peanut butter glob count and the total jam blob count must both be divisible by the sandwich count \\(N>1\\).\n\nIf a proposed jam cost is \\(c\\) cents, the two total ingredient counts are\n\\[\n\\frac{253-c}{4}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\frac{c}{5}.\n\\]\nFor choices (A) through (E), respectively, these ordered pairs are\n\\[\n(37,21),\\ (32,25),\\ (27,29),\\ (22,33),\\ (17,37).\n\\]\nTheir greatest common factors are, respectively,\n\\[\n1,\\ 1,\\ 1,\\ 11,\\ 1.\n\\]\nThus only choice (D) permits a common sandwich count greater than \\(1\\).\n\nIt also works: take \\(N=11\\), giving \\(22/11=2\\) peanut butter globs and \\(33/11=3\\) jam blobs per sandwich. The total cost is \\(11(4\\cdot2+5\\cdot3)=253\\) cents. Hence the jam costs \\(\\boxed{\\$1.65}\\), choice (D).",
