"year": 2006,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 18,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A cube with \\(3\\)-inch edges is made using \\(27\\) cubes with \\(1\\)-inch edges. Nineteen of the smaller cubes are white and eight are black. If the eight black cubes are placed at the corners of the larger cube, what fraction of the surface area of the larger cube is white?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{9}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{9}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{19}{27}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Count the exposed squares, since only the outside faces contribute to the surface area. Each face of the large cube is a \\(3\\)-by-\\(3\\) grid of nine squares, each with area \\(1\\) square inch. The four corner squares are black, so the remaining \\(9-4=5\\) squares are white.\n\nThere are six faces, so the total surface area is \\(6\\cdot9=54\\) square inches, and the white surface area is \\(6\\cdot5=30\\) square inches. Therefore, the fraction that is white is\n\\[\n\\frac{30}{54}=\\frac{5}{9}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{9}}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2006/solutions",
