"year": 2006,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 21,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "An aquarium has a rectangular base that measures \\(100\\) cm by \\(40\\) cm and has a height of \\(50\\) cm. The aquarium is filled with water to a depth of \\(37\\) cm. A rock with volume \\(1000\\text{ cm}^3\\) is then placed in the aquarium and completely submerged. By how many centimeters does the water level rise?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0.25\\)",
"B": "\\(0.5\\)",
"C": "\\(1\\)",
"D": "\\(1.25\\)",
"E": "\\(2.5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The completely submerged rock occupies \\(1000\\text{ cm}^3\\) below the water surface. The amount of water stays the same, so the volume below the surface must increase by that amount.\n\nThe aquarium's base area is\n\\[\n100\\cdot40=4000\\text{ cm}^2.\n\\]\nA \\(1\\)-cm rise creates an additional rectangular layer of volume \\(4000\\text{ cm}^3\\). Therefore, the water rises\n\\[\n\\frac{1000}{4000}=0.25\\text{ cm}.\n\\]\nThe resulting depth is \\(37+0.25=37.25\\) cm, below the \\(50\\)-cm aquarium height, so no water overflows. The answer is \\(\\boxed{0.25\\text{ cm}}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2006/solutions",
