"number": 24,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In the multiplication problem below \\(A,\\) \\(B,\\) \\(C,\\) \\(D\\) are different digits. What is \\(A+B?\\) \\[ \\begin{array}{cccc}& A & B & A\\\\ \\times & & C & D\\\\ \\hline C & D & C & D\\\\ \\end{array} \\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(3\\)",
"D": "\\(4\\)",
"E": "\\(9\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The result consists of two copies of the two-digit multiplier. Write \\(n=\\overline{CD}=10C+D\\) for that multiplier. Its leading digit \\(C\\) is nonzero, so \\(n>0\\).\n\nMoving the block two decimal places to the left multiplies it by \\(100\\). Therefore,\n\\[\n\\overline{CDCD}=100n+n=101n.\n\\]\nThe given multiplication now says\n\\[\n\\overline{ABA}\\cdot n=101n.\n\\]\nDividing both sides by the positive number \\(n\\) gives \\(\\overline{ABA}=101\\). Hence \\(A=1\\) and \\(B=0\\).\n\nThese values allow four different digits: for example, \\(101\\cdot23=2323\\), using \\(1,0,2,3\\). Thus \\(A+B=1+0=\\boxed{1}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
