"year": 2007,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 14,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A triangle with side lengths in the ratio \\(3 : 4 : 5\\) is inscribed in a circle of radius \\(3.\\) What is the area of the triangle?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(8.64\\)",
"B": "\\(12\\)",
"C": "\\(5\\pi\\)",
"D": "\\(17.28\\)",
"E": "\\(18\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Write the side lengths as \\(3k,4k,5k\\), where \\(k>0\\). Since\n\\[\n(3k)^2+(4k)^2=(5k)^2,\n\\]\nthe converse of the Pythagorean theorem shows that the triangle is right. This connects its side lengths to the given circle: a chord opposite an inscribed right angle is a diameter.\n\nThe hypotenuse therefore has length \\(2\\cdot3=6\\), so \\(5k=6\\) and \\(k=\\frac65\\). The perpendicular legs have lengths \\(\\frac{18}{5}\\) and \\(\\frac{24}{5}\\). Using them as the base and height gives the area\n\\[\n\\frac12\\cdot\\frac{18}{5}\\cdot\\frac{24}{5}\n=\\frac{216}{25}\n=\\boxed{8.64}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\text{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
