"year": 2007,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "B",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "All sides of the convex pentagon \\(ABCDE\\) are of equal length, and \\(\\angle A=\\angle B=90^\\circ.\\) What is the degree measure of \\(\\angle E?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(90\\)",
"B": "\\(108\\)",
"C": "\\(120\\)",
"D": "\\(144\\)",
"E": "\\(150\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Join \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) to separate the pentagon into a quadrilateral and a triangle. The right angles at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) make \\(AE\\) and \\(BC\\) perpendicular to \\(AB\\). Convexity puts \\(E\\) and \\(C\\) on the same side of \\(AB\\). Since \\(AE=BC=AB\\), these segments form the square \\(ABCE\\).\n\nThe square gives \\(CE=AB\\) and \\(\\angle AEC=90^\\circ\\). All pentagon sides are equal, so \\(CD=DE=CE\\). Thus triangle \\(CDE\\) is equilateral, and \\(\\angle CED=60^\\circ\\).\n\nBecause the pentagon is convex, diagonal \\(EC\\) lies inside the interior angle \\(AED\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle E=\\angle AEC+\\angle CED\n=90^\\circ+60^\\circ=150^\\circ.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{150^\\circ}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
