"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For any positive integer \\(n,\\) define \\(\\boxed{n}\\) to be the sum of the positive factors of \\(n.\\) For example, \\[ \\boxed{6} = 1 + 2 + 3 + 6 = 12. \\] Find \\(\\boxed{\\boxed{11}}.\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(13\\)",
"B": "\\(20\\)",
"C": "\\(24\\)",
"D": "\\(28\\)",
"E": "\\(30\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Work from the inner box outward, because the outer box uses the result of the inner one. Since \\(11\\) is prime, its positive factors are \\(1\\) and \\(11\\), so \\(\\boxed{11}=12\\).\n\nThe factor pairs of \\(12\\) are \\(1\\cdot12\\), \\(2\\cdot6\\), and \\(3\\cdot4\\). These include every pair: a pair with both factors at least \\(4\\) would have product at least \\(16\\). Thus the distinct positive factors of \\(12\\) are \\(1,2,3,4,6,12\\), and\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\boxed{11}}=\\boxed{12}\n=1+2+3+4+6+12=28.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(28\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2007/solutions",
