"year": 2008,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 1,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A bakery owner turns on his doughnut machine at \\(8{:}30\\) am. At \\(11{:}10\\) am the machine has completed one third of the day’s job. At what time will the doughnut machine complete the job?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1{:}50\\) pm",
"B": "\\(3{:}00\\) pm",
"C": "\\(3{:}30\\) pm",
"D": "\\(4{:}30\\) pm",
"E": "\\(5{:}50\\) pm"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Assume the machine maintains a constant production rate throughout the job. Then equal thirds of the job take equal amounts of time. From \\(8{:}30\\) am to \\(11{:}10\\) am is \\(2\\) hours \\(40\\) minutes, or \\(160\\) minutes. Since this completes one third, the whole job takes\n\\[\n160\\div\\frac13=480\\text{ minutes}=8\\text{ hours}.\n\\]\nEight hours after \\(8{:}30\\) am is \\(4{:}30\\) pm.\n\nThe constant-rate assumption is necessary. For example, if the machine doubles its rate after \\(11{:}10\\) am, the remaining two thirds take only another \\(160\\) minutes, giving a completion time of \\(1{:}50\\) pm. Thus the displayed conditions alone do not determine a unique time. Under the intended constant-rate assumption, the answer is \\(4{:}30\\) pm, choice \\(D\\).",
