"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 2,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A square is drawn inside a rectangle. The ratio of the width of the rectangle to a side of the square is \\(2:1.\\) The ratio of the rectangle’s length to its width is \\(2:1.\\) What percent of the rectangle’s area is inside the square?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(12.5\\)",
"B": "\\(25\\)",
"C": "\\(50\\)",
"D": "\\(75\\)",
"E": "\\(87.5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "The given ratios let us express every length using the square's side. Let that side be \\(s>0\\). The rectangle's width is \\(2s\\), and its length is twice its width, or \\(4s\\). The square has area \\(s^2\\), while the rectangle has area\n\\[\n(2s)(4s)=8s^2.\n\\]\nBecause the square lies inside the rectangle, the required percentage is its area divided by the rectangle's area:\n\\[\n\\frac{s^2}{8s^2}\\times100\\%=12.5\\%.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(12.5\\%\\), choice \\(A\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
