"batch": "A",
"number": 3,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For the positive integer \\(n,\\) let \\(\\langle n \\rangle\\) denote the sum of all the positive divisors of \\(n\\) with the exception of \\(n\\) itself. For example, \\(\\langle 4 \\rangle = 1 + 2 = 3\\) and \\(\\langle 12 \\rangle = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 6 = 16.\\) What is \\(\\langle\\langle\\langle 6 \\rangle\\rangle\\rangle?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(6\\)",
"B": "\\(12\\)",
"C": "\\(24\\)",
"D": "\\(32\\)",
"E": "\\(36\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Start with the innermost brackets. The factor pairs of \\(6\\) are \\(1\\cdot6\\) and \\(2\\cdot3\\), so its positive divisors other than itself are exactly \\(1,2,3\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\langle6\\rangle=1+2+3=6.\n\\]\nThe rule returns \\(6\\), so applying it a second and a third time returns \\(6\\) again. Hence\n\\[\n\\langle\\langle\\langle6\\rangle\\rangle\\rangle=6,\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(A\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
