"year": 2008,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "An equilateral triangle of side length \\(10\\) is completely filled in by non-overlapping equilateral triangles of side length \\(1.\\) How many small triangles are required?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(10\\)",
"B": "\\(25\\)",
"C": "\\(100\\)",
"D": "\\(250\\)",
"E": "\\(1000\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "All equilateral triangles are similar. The large triangle has sides \\(10\\) times as long as those of a small triangle, so its altitude is also \\(10\\) times as long. Since a triangle's area is half its base times its height, its area is \\(10\\cdot10=100\\) times the area of a small triangle.\n\nThe small triangles completely cover the large triangle without overlapping interiors, so their areas add to exactly its area. Consequently, the number required is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{large triangle's area}}{\\text{one small triangle's area}}\n=100.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{100}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
