"batch": "A",
"number": 13,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on a circle centered at \\(O,\\) and \\(\\angle AOB = 60^\\circ.\\) A second circle is internally tangent to the first and tangent to both \\(OA\\) and \\(OB.\\) What is the ratio of the area of the smaller circle to that of the larger circle?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{16}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{9}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{6}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Connecting the centers to a tangency point will relate the two radii. Let the larger radius be \\(R\\), and let the smaller circle have center \\(I\\) and radius \\(r\\), where \\(0<r<R\\).\n\nThe smaller circle touches both segments \\(OA\\) and \\(OB\\), so its center is on the internal bisector of \\(\\angle AOB\\). Let \\(T\\) be its tangency point on \\(OA\\). A radius to a tangency point is perpendicular to the tangent, so \\(IT\\perp OA\\), \\(IT=r\\), and \\(\\angle IOT=30^\\circ\\). In right triangle \\(OIT\\), the side opposite \\(30^\\circ\\) is half the hypotenuse. Thus\n\\[\nOI=2r.\n\\]\nInternal tangency of the circles also gives \\(OI=R-r\\). Combining these relations yields \\(R=3r\\). Therefore the requested area ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi r^2}{\\pi R^2}=\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2=\\boxed{\\frac{1}{9}},\n\\]\nchoice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2008A/solutions",
