"year": 2008,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "B",
"number": 3,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A semipro baseball league has teams with \\(21\\) players each. League rules state that a player must be paid at least \\(\\$15{,}000,\\) and that the total of all players’ salaries for each team cannot exceed \\(\\$700{,}000.\\) What is the maximum possible salary, in dollars, for a single player?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(270{,}000\\)",
"B": "\\(385{,}000\\)",
"C": "\\(400{,}000\\)",
"D": "\\(430{,}000\\)",
"E": "\\(700{,}000\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "To maximize one player's salary, give each of the other \\(20\\) players the smallest permitted salary. Those players must receive at least\n\\[\n20\\cdot15{,}000=300{,}000\n\\]\ndollars altogether.\n\nIf the selected player's salary is \\(s\\) dollars, the team limit therefore requires\n\\[\ns+300{,}000\\le700{,}000,\n\\]\nso \\(s\\le400{,}000\\). This bound is attainable: pay that player \\(400{,}000\\) dollars and each of the other players \\(15{,}000\\) dollars. Every salary meets the minimum, and the total is exactly \\(700{,}000\\) dollars.\n\nThe maximum salary is \\(\\boxed{\\$400{,}000}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
