"year": 2009,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 6,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Kiana has two older twin brothers. The product of their three ages is \\(128.\\) What is the sum of their three ages?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(10\\)",
"B": "\\(12\\)",
"C": "\\(16\\)",
"D": "\\(18\\)",
"E": "\\(24\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Use the recorded ordinary convention that the ages are positive whole numbers of years. Let each twin be \\(t\\) years old and Kiana be \\(k\\) years old. The twins are older, so\n\\[\nkt^2=128,\\qquad 0<k<t.\n\\]\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, \\(t^2\\) must divide \\(128\\). Now \\(128=2^7\\), so its square divisors are \\(1,4,16,64\\). Consequently the complete list of possibilities is\n\\[\n(t,k)=(1,128),(2,32),(4,8),(8,2).\n\\]\nOnly \\((8,2)\\) makes Kiana younger than the twins.\n\nThe whole-number convention matters. If fractional ages were allowed, ages \\(\\tfrac{32}{9},6,6\\) would also satisfy the product and age ordering, but their sum would be \\(\\tfrac{140}{9}\\). Thus the unrestricted wording would not determine a unique sum.\n\nUnder the recorded convention, the required sum is\n\\[\n2+8+8=\\boxed{18},\n\\]\nchoice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2009B/solutions",
"figureSources": {},
