"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Method 1: Use place values and digit bounds\n\nWrite the number as \\(N=100a+10b+c\\), where \\(a,b,c\\) are digits from \\(0\\) through \\(9\\). Allowing leading zeros includes numbers with fewer than three digits. The required condition becomes\n\\[\n100a+10b+c=6(a+b+c),\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n94a+4b=5c.\n\\]\nBecause \\(5c\\le45\\) and \\(4b\\ge0\\), we have \\(94a\\le45\\). Thus \\(a=0\\), leaving \\(4b=5c\\).\n\nThe common value \\(4b=5c\\) must be a multiple of both \\(4\\) and \\(5\\), hence a multiple of \\(20\\). Since \\(0\\le4b\\le36\\), it can only be \\(0\\) or \\(20\\). These give \\((b,c)=(0,0)\\) or \\((5,4)\\), corresponding to \\(N=0\\) or \\(N=54\\).\n\nZero is excluded because the number must be positive, while \\(54=6(5+4)\\) satisfies the condition. Every number below \\(1000\\) has a unique digit representation, so the exhaustive list counts exactly one positive integer. The answer is \\(1\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Restrict the possible digit sums\n\nLet \\(N\\) be a positive integer below \\(1000\\), and let \\(S\\) be its digit sum. There are at most three digits, each at most \\(9\\), so \\(1\\le S\\le27\\). We want \\(N=6S\\).\n\nA decimal number and its digit sum differ by a multiple of \\(9\\). To see this here, write \\(N=100a+10b+c\\), allowing leading zeros. Then \\(S=a+b+c\\), and\n\\[\nN-S=99a+9b=9(11a+b).\n\\]\nSince \\(N=6S\\), this says that \\(5S\\) is divisible by \\(9\\). It is also divisible by \\(5\\), so it must be a multiple of \\(45\\), the least common multiple of \\(5\\) and \\(9\\).\n\nThe bound \\(1\\le S\\le27\\) gives \\(5\\le5S\\le135\\). Thus\n\\[\n5S\\in\\{45,90,135\\},\n\\qquad S\\in\\{9,18,27\\}.\n\\]\nConsequently the only possible numbers \\(N=6S\\) are \\(54,108,162\\). Their digit sums are \\(9,9,9\\), respectively. Therefore only \\(54=6\\cdot9\\) satisfies the original condition.\n\nThese three distinct candidates exhaust all possible digit sums, so exactly \\(1\\) positive integer works, choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
