"number": 20,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Convex quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) has \\(AB = 9\\) and \\(CD = 12.\\) Diagonals \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\) intersect at \\(E,\\) \\(AC = 14,\\) and \\(\\triangle AED\\) and \\(\\triangle BEC\\) have equal areas. What is \\(AE?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{2}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{50}{11}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{21}{4}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{17}{3}\\)",
"E": "\\(6\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Method 1: Add a common triangle\n\nWrite \\([XYZ]\\) for the area of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Convexity places \\(E\\) inside both diagonals and places \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the same side of line \\(AB\\).\n\nAdding \\([ABE]\\) to the given equal areas produces two larger triangles with the same base:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n[ABD]&=[ABE]+[AED]\\\\\n&=[ABE]+[BEC]=[ABC].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince these triangles share base \\(AB\\), they have equal perpendicular heights. Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are on the same side of \\(AB\\), so their equal distances from that line place them on the same parallel to it. Therefore \\(CD\\parallel AB\\).\n\nNow \\(\\angle ABE=\\angle CDE\\) by parallel lines, and \\(\\angle AEB=\\angle CED\\) because they are vertical angles. Thus \\(\\triangle AEB\\sim\\triangle CED\\), giving\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{EC}=\\frac{AB}{CD}=\\frac9{12}=\\frac34.\n\\]\nBecause \\(AE+EC=AC=14\\), the segment \\(AE\\) is three of seven equal parts of \\(AC\\). Hence\n\\[\nAE=\\frac37\\cdot14=\\boxed{6},\n\\]\nchoice E.\n\nMethod 2: Compare areas using common heights\n\nWrite \\([XYZ]\\) for the area of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Since the quadrilateral is convex, all four diagonal segments and the areas of the triangles around \\(E\\) are positive.\n\nCompare both given equal areas with \\([AEB]\\). Triangles \\(AED\\) and \\(AEB\\) have bases \\(ED\\) and \\(EB\\) on line \\(BD\\), with the same altitude from \\(A\\). Similarly, triangles \\(BEC\\) and \\(BEA\\) have bases \\(EC\\) and \\(EA\\) on line \\(AC\\), with the same altitude from \\(B\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{[AED]}{[AEB]}=\\frac{ED}{EB},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{[BEC]}{[AEB]}=\\frac{EC}{EA}.\n\\]\nThe given area equality implies\n\\[\n\\frac{ED}{EB}=\\frac{EC}{EA},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{AE}{EC}=\\frac{BE}{ED}.\n\\]\nAll denominators are positive. These are the ratios of the two sides surrounding \\(E\\) in triangles \\(AEB\\) and \\(CED\\). Their included angles are equal vertical angles, so the triangles are similar by side-angle-side similarity.\n\nCorresponding sides now give\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{EC}=\\frac{AB}{CD}=\\frac9{12}=\\frac34.\n\\]\nSince \\(AE+EC=14\\),\n\\[\nAE=\\frac3{3+4}\\cdot14=\\boxed{6},\n\\]\nchoice E.",
