"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "B",
"number": 1,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Each morning of her five-day workweek, Jane bought either a \\(50\\)-cent muffin or a \\(75\\)-cent bagel. Her total cost for the week was a whole number of dollars. How many bagels did she buy?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(3\\)",
"D": "\\(4\\)",
"E": "\\(5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Five muffins would cost \\(5\\cdot50=250\\) cents, and five bagels would cost \\(5\\cdot75=375\\) cents. Every possible weekly purchase has a cost between these amounts. The only whole-dollar total in that interval is \\(300\\) cents.\n\nReplacing a muffin with a bagel increases the cost by \\(75-50=25\\) cents. To increase the all-muffin cost from \\(250\\) to \\(300\\) cents requires\n\\[\n\\frac{300-250}{25}=2\n\\]\nbagels. Two bagels and three muffins do cost \\(300\\) cents, so the answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
