"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 9,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A palindrome, such as \\(83438,\\) is a number that remains the same when its digits are reversed. The numbers \\(x\\) and \\(x + 32\\) are three-digit and four-digit palindromes, respectively. What is the sum of the digits of \\(x?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(20\\)",
"B": "\\(21\\)",
"C": "\\(22\\)",
"D": "\\(23\\)",
"E": "\\(24\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The change from three digits to four digits puts \\(x+32\\) in a very short interval. Because \\(x\\le 999\\) and \\(x+32\\) has four digits,\n\\[\n1000\\le x+32\\le 999+32=1031.\n\\]\nEvery number in this interval begins with the digits \\(10\\). A palindrome beginning with \\(10\\) must end with \\(01\\), so the only possibility is \\(1001\\).\n\nThus \\(x=1001-32=969\\), which is indeed a three-digit palindrome. Its digit sum is\n\\[\n9+6+9=24.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(24\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
