"year": 2010,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A ticket to a school play costs \\(x\\) dollars, where \\(x\\) is a whole number. A group of \\(9\\)th graders buys tickets costing a total of \\(\\$48,\\) and a group of \\(10\\)th graders buys tickets costing a total of \\(\\$64.\\) How many values for \\(x\\) are possible?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(2\\)",
"C": "\\(3\\)",
"D": "\\(4\\)",
"E": "\\(5\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "A zero-dollar ticket could not produce either positive total, so \\(x>0\\). Since each group buys a whole number of tickets, \\(x\\) must divide both \\(48\\) and \\(64\\).\n\nAny common factor also divides their difference,\n\\[64-48=16.\\]\nThe positive factors of \\(16\\) are\n\\[1,\\ 2,\\ 4,\\ 8,\\ 16.\\]\nEach of these divides both \\(48=3\\cdot16\\) and \\(64=4\\cdot16\\), giving positive whole-number ticket counts. Thus the list contains every possible price, with no repetitions. There are \\(\\boxed{5}\\) values, choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2010B/solutions",
