"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 6,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A palindrome, such as \\(83438,\\) is a number that remains the same when its digits are reversed. The numbers \\(x\\) and \\(x + 32\\) are three-digit and four-digit palindromes, respectively. What is the sum of the digits of \\(x?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(20\\)",
"B": "\\(21\\)",
"C": "\\(22\\)",
"D": "\\(23\\)",
"E": "\\(24\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Since \\(x\\) has three digits, \\(x\\leq999\\), so \\(x+32\\leq1031\\). Since \\(x+32\\) has four digits, it is at least \\(1000\\). Therefore\n\\[\n1000\\leq x+32\\leq1031.\n\\]\nEvery number in this interval begins with the digits \\(10\\). For a four-digit palindrome, the last two digits must reverse the first two, so the only possibility is \\(1001\\).\n\nThus \\(x=1001-32=969\\), which is indeed a three-digit palindrome. Its digit sum is\n\\[\n9+6+9=\\boxed{24},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
