"year": 2010,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Logan is constructing a scaled model of his town. The city’s water tower stands \\(40\\) meters high, and the top portion is a sphere that holds \\(100{,}000\\) liters of water. Logan’s miniature water tower holds \\(0.1\\) liters. How tall, in meters, should Logan make his tower?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0.04\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{0.4}{\\pi}\\)",
"C": "\\(0.4\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\\)",
"E": "\\(4\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "A scaled model uses the same positive factor \\(s\\) for every length. Its volume is therefore multiplied by \\(s^3\\). Since both water capacities are given in liters,\n\\[\ns^3=\\frac{0.1}{100{,}000}=\\frac{1}{1{,}000{,}000}=\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)^3.\n\\]\nThus \\(s=1/100\\). The height must use this same length factor, so Logan should make his tower\n\\[\n40\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)=0.4\\text{ meters}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{0.4}\\) meters, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
