"batch": "NA",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "As Emily is riding her bicycle on a long straight road, she spots Emerson skating in the same direction \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) mile in front of her. After she passes him, she can see him in her rear view mirror until he is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) mile behind her. Emily rides at a constant rate of \\(12\\) miles per hour, and Emerson skates at a constant rate of \\(8\\) miles per hour. For how many minutes can Emily see Emerson?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( \\ 6 \\)",
"B": "\\( \\ 8 \\)",
"C": "\\( \\ 12 \\)",
"D": "\\( \\ 15 \\)",
"E": "\\( \\ 16 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Emily first sees Emerson \\(\\frac12\\) mile ahead and last sees him \\(\\frac12\\) mile behind. During that interval, she gains a total of\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac12=1\\text{ mile}\n\\]\non him.\n\nBecause they travel in the same direction, Emily gains distance at \\(12-8=4\\) miles per hour. The time needed to gain \\(1\\) mile is therefore\n\\[\n1\\div4=\\frac14\\text{ hour}.\n\\]\nSince an hour contains \\(60\\) minutes, she can see Emerson for \\(\\frac14\\cdot60=\\boxed{15}\\) minutes, choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
