"id": "2011_AMC_10B_Q05",
"bank": "amc",
"responseType": "choice",
"domain": [],
"skill": [],
"year": 2011,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 5,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In multiplying two positive integers \\(a\\) and \\(b,\\) Ron reversed the digits of the two-digit number \\(a.\\) His erroneous product was \\(161.\\) What is the correct value of the product of \\(a\\) and \\(b?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(116 \\)",
"B": "\\( 161 \\)",
"C": "\\( 204 \\)",
"D": "\\(214 \\)",
"E": "\\(224 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "The wording does not explicitly require the reversed number to retain two digits. If a leading zero is dropped, reversing \\(10\\) gives \\(01\\), whose numerical value is \\(1\\). We must account for this possibility before claiming a unique product.\n\nLet \\(r\\) be the numerical value obtained by reversing the digits of \\(a\\). Then \\(rb=161\\), and \\(1\\le r\\le99\\). Since \\(161=7\\cdot23\\), with both factors prime, its positive factors are \\(1,7,23,161\\). The factor \\(161\\) is too large to be \\(r\\), leaving exactly three cases.\n\nIf \\(r=1\\), then \\(a=10\\) and \\(b=161\\), so \\(ab=1610\\).\n\nIf \\(r=7\\), then \\(a=70\\) and \\(b=23\\), so \\(ab=1610\\).\n\nIf \\(r=23\\), then \\(a=32\\) and \\(b=7\\), so \\(ab=224\\).\n\nThese cases exhaust the possible reversed factors. Thus the statement as written permits both \\(224\\) and \\(1610\\), so it does not determine a unique product. If we additionally require the reversed number to have two digits, only \\(r=23\\) remains, giving \\(224\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\). This is the only listed possible product.",
