"year": 2011,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 6,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The players on a basketball team made some three-point shots, some two-point shots, and some one-point free throws. They scored as many points with two-point shots as with three-point shots. Their number of successful free throws was one more than their number of successful two-point shots. The team’s total score was \\(61\\) points. How many free throws did they make?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(13\\)",
"B": "\\(14\\)",
"C": "\\(15\\)",
"D": "\\(16\\)",
"E": "\\(17\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Let \\(x\\) be the number of successful two-point shots. These contributed \\(2x\\) points. The three-point shots contributed the same number of points, so they also contributed \\(2x\\), even though their shot count was different. There were \\(x+1\\) successful free throws, each worth one point.\n\nAdding the three contributions gives\n\\[\n2x+2x+(x+1)=61,\n\\]\nso \\(5x=60\\) and \\(x=12\\). Therefore the team made \\(12+1=13\\) free throws.\n\nThis is feasible: \\(12\\) two-point shots and \\(8\\) three-point shots each contribute \\(24\\) points, and \\(24+24+13=61\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{13}\\), choice A.",
