"number": 15,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many positive two-digit integers are factors of \\(2^{24}-1?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(4\\)",
"B": "\\(8\\)",
"C": "\\(10\\)",
"D": "\\(12\\)",
"E": "\\(14\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Factoring the large number reveals which prime factors a two-digit divisor can contain. Repeated difference of squares gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{24}-1\n&=(2^{12}-1)(2^{12}+1)\\\\\n&=63\\cdot65\\cdot4097\\\\\n&=3^2\\cdot5\\cdot7\\cdot13\\cdot17\\cdot241.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(241\\) is prime: none of \\(2,3,5,7,11,13\\), the primes below \\(\\sqrt{241}<16\\), divides it.\n\nA two-digit divisor cannot contain \\(241\\). It also cannot contain both \\(13\\) and \\(17\\), because \\(13\\cdot17=221>99\\). This leaves three disjoint cases.\n\nIf the divisor contains neither \\(13\\) nor \\(17\\), it divides \\(3^2\\cdot5\\cdot7=315\\). The divisors of \\(3^2\\cdot5=45\\) are \\(1,3,5,9,15,45\\). Using these either alone or multiplied by \\(7\\) gives precisely the two-digit values\n\\[\n15,\\ 21,\\ 35,\\ 45,\\ 63,\n\\]\nso this case contributes \\(5\\).\n\nIf the divisor contains \\(13\\), it is \\(13\\) times a divisor of \\(315\\) at most \\(7\\). Those possible cofactors are \\(1,3,5,7\\), giving\n\\[\n13,\\ 39,\\ 65,\\ 91.\n\\]\nThis contributes \\(4\\).\n\nIf it contains \\(17\\), its cofactor must be a divisor of \\(315\\) at most \\(5\\). The possibilities \\(1,3,5\\) give\n\\[\n17,\\ 51,\\ 85,\n\\]\ncontributing \\(3\\).\n\nEvery two-digit divisor belongs to exactly one of these cases. Within each case, distinct cofactors give distinct divisors. Thus the total is\n\\[\n5+4+3=\\boxed{12},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2011B/solutions",
