"question": "There are \\(270\\) students at Colfax Middle School, where the ratio of boys to girls is \\(5 : 4.\\) There are \\(180\\) students at Winthrop Middle School, where the ratio of boys to girls is \\(4 : 5.\\) The two schools hold a dance and all students from both schools attend. What fraction of the students at the dance are girls?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{18}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{15}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{22}{45}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{23}{45}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Each school's ratio has \\(9\\) parts altogether, but a part represents a different number of students at each school.\n\nAt Colfax, each part contains \\(270\\div9=30\\) students. Girls account for \\(4\\) parts, so there are \\(4\\cdot30=120\\) girls.\n\nAt Winthrop, each part contains \\(180\\div9=20\\) students. Girls account for \\(5\\) parts, so there are \\(5\\cdot20=100\\) girls.\n\nThe dance therefore has \\(120+100=220\\) girls among \\(270+180=450\\) students. The required fraction is\n\\[\n\\frac{220}{450}=\\frac{22}{45},\n\\]\nwhich is choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2011/solutions",
