"year": 2012,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 7,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For a science project, Sammy observed a chipmunk and a squirrel stashing acorns in holes. The chipmunk hid \\(3\\) acorns in each of the holes it dug. The squirrel hid \\(4\\) acorns in each of the holes it dug. They each hid the same number of acorns, although the squirrel needed \\(4\\) fewer holes. How many acorns did the chipmunk hide?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 30 \\)",
"B": "\\( 36 \\)",
"C": "\\( 42 \\)",
"D": "\\( 48 \\)",
"E": "\\( 54 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The common acorn total must be divisible by both \\(3\\) and \\(4\\). Their least common multiple is \\(12\\), so that total consists of whole batches of \\(12\\) acorns.\n\nFor each batch, the chipmunk needs \\(12\\div3=4\\) holes, while the squirrel needs \\(12\\div4=3\\) holes. Thus each batch produces a difference of exactly one hole. A difference of four holes requires four batches, giving\n\\[4\\cdot12=48\\]\nacorns for each animal. Indeed, \\(48\\) acorns require \\(16\\) chipmunk holes and \\(12\\) squirrel holes, exactly four fewer. The chipmunk hid \\(\\boxed{48}\\) acorns, choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
