"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many ordered pairs of positive integers \\((M,N)\\) satisfy the equation \\[\\frac{M}{6}=\\frac{6}{N}\\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 6 \\)",
"B": "\\( 7 \\)",
"C": "\\( 8 \\)",
"D": "\\( 9 \\)",
"E": "\\( 10 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Because \\(N\\) is positive, both denominators are nonzero. Multiplying the equation by \\(6N\\) gives the equivalent condition\n\\[\nMN=36.\n\\]\nThus we need the positive factor pairs of \\(36\\). In each pair, the smaller factor is at most \\(6\\), since two numbers greater than \\(6\\) have product greater than \\(36\\). Testing the integers from \\(1\\) through \\(6\\) gives\n\\[\n(1,36),\\ (2,18),\\ (3,12),\\ (4,9),\\ (6,6).\n\\]\nThese exhaust the factor pairs because \\(5\\) is the only tested integer that does not divide \\(36\\).\n\nEach of the first four pairs gives two ordered pairs by reversing the entries. The pair \\((6,6)\\) gives only one. Every resulting pair satisfies the original equation, and no two entries in this count describe the same ordered pair. Therefore the answer is\n\\[\n4\\cdot2+1=9,\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2012B/solutions",
