"batch": "A",
"number": 5,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A fruit salad consists of blueberries, raspberries, grapes, and cherries. The fruit salad has a total of \\(280\\) pieces of fruit. There are twice as many raspberries as blueberries, three times as many grapes as cherries, and four times as many cherries as raspberries. How many cherries are there in the fruit salad?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(8\\)",
"B": "\\(16\\)",
"C": "\\(25\\)",
"D": "\\(64\\)",
"E": "\\(96\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Use the number of blueberries as one share so that every comparison uses the same unit. Raspberries then occupy \\(2\\) shares. Cherries occupy \\(4\\cdot2=8\\) shares, and grapes occupy \\(3\\cdot8=24\\) shares.\n\nThese four kinds of fruit account for all \\(280\\) pieces, so there are\n\\[\n1+2+8+24=35\n\\]\nshares altogether. Each share contains \\(280\\div35=8\\) pieces. Cherries occupy \\(8\\) shares, giving\n\\[\n8\\cdot8=64\n\\]\ncherries, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
