"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A triangle has area \\(30,\\) one side of length \\(10,\\) and the median to that side of length \\(9.\\) Let \\(\\theta\\) be the acute angle formed by that side and the median. What is \\(\\sin\\theta?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{10}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{20}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{10}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The area determines the perpendicular height above the given side. Let that side be \\(AB\\), let \\(C\\) be the opposite vertex, and let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(AB\\). Thus the given median is \\(CM=9\\).\n\nDrop a perpendicular from \\(C\\) to the line \\(AB\\), meeting it at \\(H\\). The triangle's area gives\n\\[\n30=\\frac12\\cdot10\\cdot CH,\n\\qquad CH=6.\n\\]\nBecause \\(MH\\) lies on the line of the given side, the acute angle at \\(M\\) in right triangle \\(CMH\\) is \\(\\theta\\). Its opposite side is \\(CH\\) and its hypotenuse is \\(CM\\), so\n\\[\n\\sin\\theta=\\frac{CH}{CM}=\\frac69=\\frac23.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac23}\\), choice D.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
