"year": 2012,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "B",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A dessert chef prepares the dessert for every day of a week starting with Sunday. The dessert each day is either cake, pie, ice cream, or pudding. The same dessert may not be served two days in a row. There must be cake on Friday because of a birthday. How many different dessert menus for the week are possible?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(729\\)",
"B": "\\(972\\)",
"C": "\\(1024\\)",
"D": "\\(2187\\)",
"E": "\\(2304\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "Friday is fixed as cake, so begin there and choose desserts backward for Thursday, Wednesday, Tuesday, Monday, and Sunday. At each step, the next day's dessert has already been chosen. Excluding that dessert leaves exactly three choices.\n\nSaturday also has three choices because its dessert must differ from Friday's cake. By the multiplication principle, the number of menus is\n\\[\n3^5\\cdot3=3^6=729.\n\\]\nThis process checks every consecutive pair in the Sunday-through-Saturday week. Every valid menu can be produced in this order, and its daily desserts determine exactly one sequence of choices, so no menu is omitted or counted twice. The answer is \\(\\boxed{729}\\), choice A.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2012B/solutions",
