"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Isabella must take four \\(100\\)-point tests in her math class. Her goal is to achieve an average grade of at least \\(95\\) on the tests. Her first two test scores were \\(97\\) and \\(91.\\) After seeing her score on the third test, she realized that she could still reach her goal. What is the lowest possible score she could have made on the third test?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 90 \\)",
"B": "\\( 92 \\)",
"C": "\\( 95 \\)",
"D": "\\( 96 \\)",
"E": "\\( 97 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "An average of at least \\(95\\) on four tests requires at least\n\\[\n4\\cdot95=380\n\\]\ntotal points. Isabella’s first two scores total \\(97+91=188\\), so her last two scores must total at least \\(380-188=192\\).\n\nThe fourth test can contribute at most \\(100\\) points. Therefore, the third score must be at least \\(192-100=92\\). A third score of \\(92\\) followed by \\(100\\) gives exactly \\(380\\) points, so this bound is attainable. The lowest possible score is \\(\\boxed{92}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
