"solution": "The digits are one \\(0\\), one \\(1\\), and two identical \\(2\\)s. Once we place \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), the remaining two positions must contain \\(2\\).\n\nThe \\(0\\) cannot be the first digit, so it has \\(3\\) possible positions. After choosing its position, the \\(1\\) has \\(3\\) remaining positions. Thus there are\n\\[\n3\\cdot3=9\n\\]\npossibilities. Each is a four-digit number and cannot equal \\(1000\\), so each exceeds \\(1000\\). Every valid number determines exactly one position for \\(0\\) and one for \\(1\\), ensuring none is omitted or counted twice. The answer is \\(\\boxed{9}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
