"choices": {
"A": "\\( 2 \\)",
"B": "\\( 3 \\)",
"C": "\\( 4 \\)",
"D": "\\( 5 \\)",
"E": "\\( 6 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Using the recorded retail convention that each pencil’s price is a whole number of cents, that price must divide both purchase totals, \\(143\\) cents and \\(187\\) cents.\n\nThe positive factors of \\(143\\) are \\(1,11,13,143\\). Among these, only \\(1\\) and \\(11\\) divide \\(187\\), since \\(187=11\\cdot17\\). The pencils cost more than one cent each, so their price must be \\(11\\) cents.\n\nJamar bought \\(143\\div11=13\\) pencils, and Sharona bought \\(187\\div11=17\\). Therefore, Sharona bought \\(17-13=\\boxed{4}\\) more pencils, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
