"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 14,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In the BIG N, a middle school football conference, each team plays every other team exactly once. If a total of \\(21\\) conference games were played during the \\(2012\\) season, how many teams were members of the BIG N conference?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 6 \\)",
"B": "\\( 7 \\)",
"C": "\\( 8 \\)",
"D": "\\( 9 \\)",
"E": "\\( 10 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Method 1: Add the teams one at a time\n\nImagine adding the teams to the conference one at a time. The second team creates \\(1\\) game against the first team. The third creates \\(2\\) more games, one against each earlier team. Each new team similarly adds one game against every earlier team.\n\nEvery game is counted exactly once, when the later of its two teams is added. With seven teams, the total is\n\\[\n1+2+3+4+5+6=21.\n\\]\nFewer teams give fewer games, and adding any more teams increases the total. Therefore there were \\(7\\) teams, choice \\(\\text{B}\\).\n\nMethod 2: Count team appearances\n\nEach game involves two teams, so the \\(21\\) games contain \\(2\\cdot21=42\\) team appearances.\n\nLet \\(n\\ge2\\) be the integer number of teams. Each team plays the other \\(n-1\\) teams, so counting appearances team by team gives\n\\[\nn(n-1)=42.\n\\]\nSince \\(7\\cdot6=42\\), seven teams work. For integer \\(n\\ge2\\), increasing \\(n\\) increases both positive factors, so no other team count gives the same total. The answer is \\(7\\), choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
