"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The smallest number greater than \\(2\\) that leaves a remainder of \\(2\\) when divided by \\(3,\\) \\(4,\\) \\(5,\\) or \\(6\\) lies between what numbers?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(40\\) and \\(50\\)",
"B": "\\(51\\) and \\(55\\)",
"C": "\\(56\\) and \\(60\\)",
"D": "\\(61\\) and \\(65\\)",
"E": "\\(66\\) and \\(99\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Subtracting the remainder \\(2\\) turns each division condition into exact divisibility. Thus, if \\(N>2\\) is the required integer, \\(N-2\\) must be a positive common multiple of \\(3,4,5,6\\).\n\nBecause \\(4\\) and \\(5\\) have no common factor greater than \\(1\\), their positive common multiples are \\(20,40,60,\\ldots\\). The first of these divisible by \\(6\\) is \\(60\\), which is also divisible by \\(3\\). Therefore the least possible value of \\(N-2\\) is \\(60\\).\n\nAdding \\(2\\) back gives\n\\[\nN=60+2=62.\n\\]\nSince \\(2\\) is smaller than every divisor, this does give remainder \\(2\\) in all four divisions. The number \\(62\\) lies between \\(61\\) and \\(65\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2012/solutions",
