"number": 5,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Tom, Dorothy, and Sammy went on a vacation and agreed to split the costs evenly. During their trip Tom paid \\(\\$105,\\) Dorothy paid \\(\\$125,\\) and Sammy paid \\(\\$175.\\) In order to share costs equally, Tom gave Sammy \\(t\\) dollars, and Dorothy gave Sammy \\(d\\) dollars. What is \\(t-d?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(15\\)",
"B": "\\(20\\)",
"C": "\\(25\\)",
"D": "\\(30\\)",
"E": "\\(35\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Method 1: Compare Tom's and Dorothy's final costs\n\nAfter paying Sammy, Tom's total cost is \\(105+t\\) dollars and Dorothy's total cost is \\(125+d\\) dollars. Their final costs are equal, so\n\\[\n105+t=125+d.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(105+d\\) from both sides gives\n\\[\nt-d=125-105=\\boxed{20}.\n\\]\nTom initially paid \\(20\\) dollars less than Dorothy, so he must pay Sammy \\(20\\) dollars more than she does. The answer is B.\n\nMethod 2: Calculate each person's equal share\n\nThe total trip cost was\n\\[\n105+125+175=405\n\\]\ndollars. Sharing this equally among three people gives \\(405\\div3=135\\) dollars per person.\n\nTom must pay \\(t=135-105=30\\) dollars, and Dorothy must pay \\(d=135-125=10\\) dollars. Sammy then has a net cost of \\(175-30-10=135\\) dollars as well, so these payments do make all three costs equal. Hence\n\\[\nt-d=30-10=\\boxed{20},\n\\]\nchoice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
