"year": 2013,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A student council must select a two-person welcoming committee and a three-person planning committee from among its members. There are exactly \\(10\\) ways to select a two-person team for the welcoming committee. It is possible for students to serve on both committees. In how many different ways can a three-person planning committee be selected?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(10\\)",
"B": "\\(12\\)",
"C": "\\(15\\)",
"D": "\\(18\\)",
"E": "\\(25\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "First determine the number of council members. If there are \\(n\\) members, choosing a first person and then a different second person gives \\(n(n-1)\\) ordered selections. Each two-person team appears twice, once in each order. Thus\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2=10,\n\\qquad n(n-1)=20.\n\\]\nFor integers \\(n\\ge2\\), this product increases as \\(n\\) increases. Since \\(5\\cdot4=20\\), the council has exactly five members.\n\nNow choosing a three-person planning committee is equivalent to choosing the two members left out. Each omitted pair determines exactly one committee, and each committee determines exactly one omitted pair. There are \\(10\\) such pairs. Serving on the welcoming committee does not remove anyone from consideration, because membership on both committees is allowed.\n\nThere are \\(10\\) planning committees, choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2013A/solutions",
