"batch": "B",
"number": 8,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Ray’s car averages \\(40\\) miles per gallon of gasoline, and Tom’s car averages \\(10\\) miles per gallon of gasoline. Ray and Tom each drive the same number of miles. What is the cars’ combined rate of miles per gallon of gasoline?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 10 \\)",
"B": "\\( 16 \\)",
"C": "\\( 25 \\)",
"D": "\\( 30 \\)",
"E": "\\( 40 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "The combined rate is total miles divided by total gallons. Rescaling both equal trip lengths multiplies their gasoline use by the same factor, so it does not change this ratio. We may therefore calculate using \\(40\\) miles for each driver.\n\nRay uses \\(40/40=1\\) gallon, and Tom uses \\(40/10=4\\) gallons. Together they travel \\(80\\) miles using \\(5\\) gallons, so their combined rate is\n\\[\n\\frac{80}{1+4}=16\n\\]\nmiles per gallon, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
