"domain": [],
"skill": [],
"year": 2013,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 14,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Abe holds \\(1\\) green and \\(1\\) red jelly bean in his hand. Bea holds \\(1\\) green, \\(1\\) yellow, and \\(2\\) red jelly beans in her hand. Each randomly picks a jelly bean to show the other. What is the probability that the colors match?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Bea's two red beans are two separate possible selections, even though their colors agree. Abe has \\(2\\) beans to choose from, and each can be paired with any of Bea's \\(4\\) beans. The separate random picks therefore give \\(2\\cdot4=8\\) equally likely pairs. Every outcome is counted once by specifying one bean from each hand.\n\nA green match pairs Abe's green bean with Bea's only green bean, giving \\(1\\) matching pair. A red match pairs Abe's red bean with either of Bea's two red beans, giving \\(2\\) matching pairs. These cases do not overlap, and no yellow match is possible because Abe has no yellow bean.\n\nConsequently, the probability of matching colors is\n\\[\n\\frac{1+2}{8}=\\frac{3}{8},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
