"question": "Which of the following numbers is a perfect square?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{14!15!}2\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{15!16!}2\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{16!17!}2\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{17!18!}2\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{18!19!}2\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The neighboring factorials share almost all their factors. Recall that \\(n!=1\\cdot2\\cdots n\\), so \\((n+1)!=(n+1)n!\\). Each choice has the form \\(n!(n+1)!/2\\), with \\(n=14,15,16,17,18\\), respectively.\n\nSince each of these factorials is even, \\(n!/2\\) is an integer. Rewrite the expression as\n\\[\n\\frac{n!(n+1)!}{2}\n=\\left(\\frac{n!}{2}\\right)^2 2(n+1).\n\\]\nAn integer square has an even exponent on every prime factor. Multiplying by the displayed square adds only even exponents, so we need only examine \\(2(n+1)\\). For choices A through E, these factors are\n\\[\n30,\\quad32,\\quad34,\\quad36,\\quad38.\n\\]\nThe numbers \\(30,34,38\\) each contain exactly one factor of \\(2\\), and \\(32=2^5\\), so these four choices cannot be squares. But \\(36=6^2\\), giving\n\\[\n\\frac{17!18!}{2}\n=\\left(6\\cdot\\frac{17!}{2}\\right)^2\n=(3\\cdot17!)^2.\n\\]\nThus the perfect square is choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
