"year": 2014,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 1,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Leah has \\(13\\) coins, all of which are pennies and nickels. If she had one more nickel than she has now, then she would have the same number of pennies and nickels. In cents, how much are Leah’s coins worth?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 33 \\)",
"B": "\\( 35 \\)",
"C": "\\( 37 \\)",
"D": "\\( 39 \\)",
"E": "\\( 41 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "With one additional nickel, Leah would have \\(14\\) coins, split equally between pennies and nickels. She would therefore have \\(14\\div2=7\\) of each.\n\nRemoving that hypothetical nickel leaves \\(7\\) pennies and \\(6\\) nickels. A penny is worth \\(1\\) cent and a nickel is worth \\(5\\) cents, so her original coins are worth\n\\[\n7\\cdot1+6\\cdot5=7+30=\\boxed{37}\\text{ cents},\n\\]\nchoice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
