"id": "2014_AMC_12A_Q16",
"bank": "amc",
"responseType": "choice",
"domain": [],
"skill": [],
"year": 2014,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 16,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The product \\((8)(888\\ldots8),\\) where the second factor has \\(k\\) digits, is an integer whose digits have a sum of \\(1000.\\) What is \\(k?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(901\\)",
"B": "\\(911\\)",
"C": "\\(919\\)",
"D": "\\(991\\)",
"E": "\\(999\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The repeated eights make the multiplication predictable: after the first two columns, the same carry repeats. Let \\(R=8(1+10+\\cdots+10^{k-1})\\), the number with \\(k\\) eights.\n\nIf \\(k=1\\), then \\(8R=64\\), whose digit sum is only \\(10\\). Now suppose \\(k\\ge2\\). In multiplying \\(R\\) by \\(8\\), the units column gives \\(8\\cdot8=64\\), so we write \\(4\\) and carry \\(6\\). The next column gives \\(64+6=70\\), so we write \\(0\\) and carry \\(7\\). Every remaining column gives \\(64+7=71\\), so we write \\(1\\) and keep carrying \\(7\\). The final carry becomes the leading digit.\n\nThus the product has a leading \\(7\\), then \\(k-2\\) ones, and finally \\(04\\). Its digit sum is\n\\[\n7+(k-2)+0+4=k+9.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(k+9=1000\\), giving \\(\\boxed{k=991}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2014A/solutions",
