"year": 2014,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 23,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The fraction \\[\\dfrac{1}{99^2}=0.\\overline{b_{n-1}b_{n-2}\\ldots b_2b_1b_0},\\] where \\(n\\) is the length of the period of the repeating decimal expansion. What is the sum \\(b_0+b_1+\\cdots+b_{n-1}?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(874\\)",
"B": "\\(883\\)",
"C": "\\(887\\)",
"D": "\\(891\\)",
"E": "\\(892\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Because \\(99^2=9801\\) and \\(100=99+1\\), bringing down two decimal places at a time produces a simple remainder pattern. Each quotient is written as a two-digit block, retaining leading zeros.\n\nStart with remainder \\(1\\). For \\(j=0,1,\\ldots,97\\),\n\\[\n100(1+99j)=9801j+\\bigl(1+99(j+1)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe final term is between \\(0\\) and \\(9801\\), so this is a valid division step: remainder \\(1+99j\\) produces block \\(j\\) and next remainder \\(1+99(j+1)\\). Thus the first blocks are \\(00,01,\\ldots,97\\), after which the remainder is \\(9703\\). The next step is\n\\[\n100\\cdot9703=99\\cdot9801+1.\n\\]\nIt produces block \\(99\\) and returns to the initial remainder. Therefore the repeating block is\n\\[\n00,01,02,\\ldots,97,99,\n\\]\ncontaining \\(99\\) two-digit blocks, or \\(198\\) decimal digits.\n\nWe must check that this is the shortest period. If a period had length \\(d\\) and digit-block integer \\(B\\), shifting and subtracting the repeated decimal would give\n\\[\n\\frac{10^d-1}{9801}=B.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(11\\) would divide \\(10^d-1\\). Since \\(10\\equiv-1\\pmod{11}\\), this is impossible for odd \\(d\\). An even period shorter than \\(198\\) would return the division remainder to \\(1\\) after fewer than \\(99\\) two-digit steps. But the remainders \\(1+99j\\), for \\(0\\le j\\le98\\), are distinct, and only the first is \\(1\\). Hence the minimal period really is \\(198\\).\n\nAcross all two-digit blocks \\(00\\) through \\(99\\), each digit occurs ten times in each position. Their total digit sum is\n\\[\n2\\cdot10(0+1+\\cdots+9)=900.\n\\]\nOur period contains exactly these blocks except \\(98\\), whose digits sum to \\(17\\). The answer is \\(900-17=\\boxed{883}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2014A/solutions",
