"question": "A magazine printed photos of three celebrities along with three photos of the celebrities as babies. The baby pictures did not identify the celebrities. Readers were asked to match each celebrity with the correct baby picture. What is the probability that a reader guessing at random will match all three correctly?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{9} \\)",
"B": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{6} \\)",
"C": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\)",
"D": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{3} \\)",
"E": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Each baby picture must be matched to exactly one celebrity. Consider the celebrities in a fixed order. The first can be assigned any of the three baby pictures, the second can be assigned either remaining picture, and the third must receive the last picture. Thus there are\n\\[\n3\\cdot2\\cdot1=6\n\\]\npossible matchings. This includes every matching exactly once, because its three assignments determine it completely.\n\nA random guess makes these six matchings equally likely, and exactly one matches all three correctly. Therefore the probability is \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\), choice B.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
