"number": 24,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "One day the Beverage Barn sold \\(252\\) cans of soda to \\(100\\) customers, and every customer bought at least one can of soda. What is the maximum possible median number of cans of soda bought per customer on that day?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 2.5 \\)",
"B": "\\( 3.0 \\)",
"C": "\\( 3.5 \\)",
"D": "\\( 4.0 \\)",
"E": "\\( 4.5 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Arrange the \\(100\\) purchase amounts from smallest to largest. Since there are an even number of customers, the median is the average of the \\(50\\)th and \\(51\\)st amounts. We can bound these amounts by counting how many cans they would force the customers to buy.\n\nIf the \\(50\\)th amount were at least \\(4\\), then that customer and the \\(50\\) customers after them would each have bought at least \\(4\\) cans. The other \\(49\\) customers each bought at least \\(1\\), requiring at least\n\\[\n51\\cdot4+49=253\n\\]\ncans. This exceeds \\(252\\), so the \\(50\\)th amount is at most \\(3\\).\n\nIf the \\(51\\)st amount were at least \\(5\\), the last \\(50\\) customers would each have bought at least \\(5\\) cans. Together with at least \\(1\\) can for each of the first \\(50\\), this would require\n\\[\n50\\cdot5+50=300\n\\]\ncans. Thus the \\(51\\)st amount is at most \\(4\\). The median is consequently at most \\((3+4)/2=3.5\\).\n\nTo attain this bound, let \\(49\\) customers buy \\(1\\) can each, one customer buy \\(3\\), and \\(50\\) customers buy \\(4\\) each. These are \\(100\\) customers buying\n\\[\n49+3+50\\cdot4=252\n\\]\ncans. The middle amounts are \\(3\\) and \\(4\\), so the maximum median is \\(\\boxed{3.5}\\) cans, choice C.",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
