"year": 2015,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 9,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Two right circular cylinders have the same volume. The radius of the second cylinder is \\(10\\%\\) more than the radius of the first. What is the relationship between the heights of the two cylinders?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "The second height is \\(10\\%\\) less than the first.",
"B": "The first height is \\(10\\%\\) more than the second.",
"C": "The second height is \\(21\\%\\) less than the first.",
"D": "The first height is \\(21\\%\\) more than the\nsecond.",
"E": "The second height is \\(80\\%\\) of the first."
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "For equal volumes, a larger base area must be balanced by a smaller height. Let \\(r>0\\) be the first cylinder's radius, and let \\(h_1,h_2>0\\) be the respective heights. The second radius is \\(1.1r\\). Using cylinder volume as base area times height gives\n\\[\n\\pi r^2h_1=\\pi(1.1r)^2h_2.\n\\]\nCanceling the positive factor \\(\\pi r^2\\), we obtain\n\\[\nh_1=1.21h_2.\n\\]\nThus the first height exceeds the second by \\(0.21h_2\\), which is \\(21\\%\\) of the second height. The answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2015A/solutions",
