"year": 2015,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 10,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many rearrangements of \\(abcd\\) are there in which no two adjacent letters are also adjacent letters in the alphabet? For example, no such rearrangements could include either \\(ab\\) or \\(ba.\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(1\\)",
"C": "\\(2\\)",
"D": "\\(3\\)",
"E": "\\(4\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Look first at the letters with the fewest possible neighbors. The letter \\(b\\) cannot be beside \\(a\\) or \\(c\\), so its only possible neighbor is \\(d\\). An interior letter would have two distinct neighbors, so \\(b\\) must be at an endpoint. Likewise, \\(c\\) can only be beside \\(a\\), so \\(c\\) must occupy the other endpoint.\n\nIf \\(b\\) comes first, its neighbor must be \\(d\\), and the neighbor of the final \\(c\\) must be \\(a\\). This forces \\(bdac\\). If \\(c\\) comes first, the same restrictions force \\(cadb\\).\n\nBoth orders satisfy the condition. Every valid order must have one of these two endpoint orientations, and each orientation forces exactly one order. Thus there are \\(\\boxed{2}\\) rearrangements, choice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
