"year": 2015,
"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "B",
"number": 13,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The line \\(12x+5y=60\\) forms a triangle with the coordinate axes. What is the sum of the lengths of the altitudes of this triangle?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 20 \\)",
"B": "\\( \\dfrac{360}{17} \\)",
"C": "\\( \\dfrac{107}{5} \\)",
"D": "\\( \\dfrac{43}{2} \\)",
"E": "\\( \\dfrac{281}{13} \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "First find where the line meets the axes. Setting \\(y=0\\) gives \\(x=5\\), and setting \\(x=0\\) gives \\(y=12\\). Thus the triangle has vertices \\(O=(0,0)\\), \\(A=(5,0)\\), and \\(B=(0,12)\\).\n\nThe coordinate axes are perpendicular, so \\(OA=5\\) and \\(OB=12\\) are themselves two altitudes: each runs from a vertex perpendicular to the opposite side. The remaining altitude runs from \\(O\\) to \\(AB\\).\n\nBy the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\nAB=\\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=13.\n\\]\nThe triangle's area is \\(\\tfrac12\\cdot5\\cdot12=30\\). Calling the altitude to \\(AB\\) \\(h\\), we can express the same area as\n\\[\n\\frac12\\cdot13h=30,\n\\]\nwhich gives \\(h=60/13\\). Therefore the sum of the three altitude lengths is\n\\[\n5+12+\\frac{60}{13}\n=\\boxed{\\frac{281}{13}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
