"year": 2015,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 9,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "A box contains \\(2\\) red marbles, \\(2\\) green marbles, and \\(2\\) yellow marbles. Carol takes \\(2\\) marbles from the box at random; then Claudia takes \\(2\\) of the remaining marbles at random; and then Cheryl takes the last \\(2\\) marbles. What is the probability that Cheryl gets \\(2\\) marbles of the same color?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{10}\\)",
"B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{6}\\)",
"C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\)",
"D": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)",
"E": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "Imagine distinguishing the six marbles with labels and shuffling them into a uniformly random order. Give the first two to Carol, the next two to Claudia, and the last two to Cheryl. This produces the same random selections as the draws in the question.\n\nFix the marble in the fifth position, Cheryl's first marble. Each of the five other marbles is equally likely to occupy the sixth position. Exactly one of those five has the same color as the fifth marble. This count holds whichever marble is in the fifth position, so it covers every possibility.\n\nThe probability that Cheryl's marbles match is therefore \\(\\boxed{\\frac15}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
