"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 14,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "What is the value of \\(a\\) for which \\[\\dfrac{1}{\\log_2 a} + \\dfrac{1}{\\log_3 a} + \\dfrac{1}{\\log_4 a} = 1?\\]",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(9\\)",
"B": "\\(12\\)",
"C": "\\(18\\)",
"D": "\\(24\\)",
"E": "\\(36\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The original logarithms require \\(a>0\\), and their denominators are nonzero exactly when \\(a\\ne1\\). On this domain, change of base gives\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\log_b a}=\\log_a b.\n\\]\nThis makes all three terms logarithms with the same base, allowing us to combine them:\n\\[\n\\log_a2+\\log_a3+\\log_a4\n=\\log_a(2\\cdot3\\cdot4)\n=\\log_a24.\n\\]\nThe given equation is therefore \\(\\log_a24=1\\). By the definition of a logarithm, this means \\(a^1=24\\), so \\(a=24\\). This value satisfies \\(a>0\\) and \\(a\\ne1\\), and every transformation was reversible on that domain. Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{24}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
