"year": 2015,
"level": "AMC 12",
"batch": "A",
"number": 22,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "For each positive integer \\(n,\\) let \\(S(n)\\) be the number of sequences of length \\(n\\) consisting solely of the letters \\(A\\) and \\(B,\\) with no more than three \\(A\\)s in a row and no more than three \\(B\\)s in a row. What is the remainder when \\(S(2015)\\) is divided by \\(12?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(0\\)",
"B": "\\(4\\)",
"C": "\\(6\\)",
"D": "\\(8\\)",
"E": "\\(10\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The useful feature to track is the last uninterrupted run of equal letters. For \\(n\\ge4\\), this run has length \\(j=1,2,\\) or \\(3\\). Deleting the whole last run leaves a nonempty valid sequence of length \\(n-j\\). Conversely, any valid sequence of length \\(n-j\\) has exactly one extension whose final run has length \\(j\\): append \\(j\\) copies of the letter opposite to its last letter. These three cases are disjoint and include every valid sequence. Therefore\n\\[\nS(n)=S(n-1)+S(n-2)+S(n-3)\\qquad(n\\ge4).\n\\]\nEvery sequence of length at most three is allowed, so\n\\[\nS(1)=2,\\qquad S(2)=4,\\qquad S(3)=8.\n\\]\n\nTo find a remainder modulo \\(12\\), we can first find the remainders modulo \\(4\\) and modulo \\(3\\). Reducing each recurrence calculation modulo \\(4\\) gives\n\\[\n(S(1),\\ldots,S(7))\n\\equiv(2,0,0,2,2,0,0)\\pmod4.\n\\]\nThe triple at positions \\(5,6,7\\) equals the triple at positions \\(1,2,3\\). Since three consecutive terms determine every following term, this proves that the residues repeat with period \\(4\\). As \\(2015=4\\cdot503+3\\),\n\\[\nS(2015)\\equiv S(3)\\equiv0\\pmod4.\n\\]\n\nThe corresponding calculations modulo \\(3\\) give\n\\[\n(S(1),\\ldots,S(8))\n\\equiv(2,1,2,2,2,0,1,0)\\pmod3,\n\\]\n\\[\n(S(9),\\ldots,S(16))\n\\equiv(1,2,0,0,2,2,1,2)\\pmod3.\n\\]\nNow the triple at positions \\(14,15,16\\) equals the initial triple. The same recurrence argument proves period \\(13\\). Since \\(2015=13\\cdot154+13\\),\n\\[\nS(2015)\\equiv S(13)\\equiv2\\pmod3.\n\\]\nThe remainders between \\(0\\) and \\(11\\) divisible by \\(4\\) are \\(0,4,8\\). Only \\(8\\) has remainder \\(2\\) upon division by \\(3\\). Thus the requested remainder is \\(\\boxed{8}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc12/2015A/solutions",
