"batch": "NA",
"number": 11,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "In the small country of Mathland, all automobile license plates have four symbols. The first must be a vowel (A, E, I, O, or U), the second and third must be two different letters among the \\(21\\) non-vowels, and the fourth must be a digit (0 through \\(9\\)). If the symbols are chosen at random subject to these conditions, what is the probability that the plate will read “AMC8”?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{22{,}050} \\)",
"B": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{21{,}000} \\)",
"C": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{10{,}500} \\)",
"D": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{2{,}100} \\)",
"E": "\\( \\dfrac{1}{1{,}050} \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "Count the possibilities one position at a time. There are \\(5\\) choices for the vowel, \\(21\\) choices for the second symbol, and \\(20\\) choices for the third symbol because it must differ from the second. There are \\(10\\) choices for the final digit, including \\(0\\).\n\nChoosing the symbols in order counts every valid plate exactly once, giving\n\\[\n5\\cdot21\\cdot20\\cdot10=21{,}000\n\\]\nequally likely plates. The plate AMC8 satisfies every restriction: A is a vowel, M and C are different non-vowels, and 8 is a digit. It is exactly one favorable outcome, so the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{21{,}000}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
