"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "A",
"solution": "To find a tens digit, it is enough to track the last two digits. In a product, digits farther left contribute multiples of \\(100\\), so only the last two digits of each factor affect the final two digits.\n\nSince \\(15^2=225\\), the number \\(2015^2\\) ends in \\(25\\). Also, \\(25\\cdot25=625\\), so multiplying two numbers ending in \\(25\\) gives another number ending in \\(25\\). Repeated multiplication therefore preserves this ending.\n\nThe exponent is even, and\n\\[\n2015^{2016}=(2015^2)^{1008}.\n\\]\nThis is a product of \\(1008\\) factors ending in \\(25\\), so it too ends in \\(25\\). Subtracting \\(2017=2000+17\\) removes whole hundreds and then changes the ending from \\(25\\) to \\(25-17=8\\). The final two digits are \\(08\\), so the tens digit is \\(0\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
"solutionSource": "Original Math Practice solution",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2016B/solutions",
