"year": 2022,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 9,
"area": "Prealgebra",
"question": "A cup of boiling water (\\(212^\\circ\\) F) is placed to cool in a room whose temperature remains constant at \\(68^\\circ\\) F. Suppose the difference between the water temperature and the room temperature is halved every \\(5\\) minutes. What is the water temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, after \\(15\\) minutes?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(77\\)",
"B": "\\(86\\)",
"C": "\\(92\\)",
"D": "\\(98\\)",
"E": "\\(104\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "B",
"solution": "It is the water's difference from room temperature that halves. Initially that difference is \\(212-68=144\\) degrees.\n\nThere are three \\(5\\)-minute intervals in \\(15\\) minutes, so the temperature excess changes as follows:\n\n\\[\n144\\longrightarrow72\\longrightarrow36\\longrightarrow18.\n\\]\n\nAfter \\(15\\) minutes, the water is still \\(18\\) degrees warmer than the room. Its temperature is therefore\n\n\\[\n68+18=\\boxed{86^\\circ\\mathrm F},\n\\]\n\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\). Keeping track of the excess makes it clear why the room temperature must be added back at the end.",
"solutionSource": "Adapted from MAA official solutions",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://drive.usercontent.google.com/uc?id=1h0XYnHhGAtM4i3JxvGUgozPrlhZiYZQb&export=download",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2022/solutions",
"figureSources": {},
